window.TERN_SEARCH=[{"p":"Tern","h":"Tern","u":"index.html#tern","t":"Tern is a compiled language that is as easy to write as Python and as safe and fast as Rust. It has no references: every value is a copy, so nothing can dangle, nothing needs a garbage collector, and data races and aliasing bugs are compile errors. Tern compiles to C and then to a small native binary, a WebAssembly module, or a library for iOS and Android. A JSON web API with a SQLite database is about 70 KB, and it serves more requests per second than the same app in Go or in Rust with axum."},{"p":"Tern","h":"Start here","u":"index.html#start-here","t":"- New to Tern? Take the Tour of Tern (tour/01-getting-started.md): thirteen short chapters, from hello world to parallel programs. - Know Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript? Read Coming from another language (guides/coming-from.md) for side-by-side translations and the mistakes people make first. - Looking something up? The language reference (reference.md) covers the whole language on one page; builtins (builtins.md) and the standard library (std/index.md) list every function. - Got a compile error? Search for its text in the error index (errors.md), or run tern check --json and read the hint ."},{"p":"Tern","h":"What makes Tern different","u":"index.html#what-makes-tern-different","t":"--- --- Values, not references b = a copies (lazily, so it's cheap). A function can only change what you pass it with & . See how memory works (guides/memory.md). Safe parallelism parallel: runs each line as a task, and parallel for spreads a loop over every core. Two tasks touching the same variable is a compile error. See parallel programming (guides/parallelism.md). No garbage collector, no lifetimes Values can't point at each other, so reference counting frees everything and there is nothing to annotate. Batteries for services An HTTP server, SQLite, and JSON are in the standard library (s"},{"p":"Tern","h":"For AI assistants","u":"index.html#for-ai-assistants","t":"Tern is designed to be learnable by language models. Point an assistant at llms.txt (../llms.txt) or give it llms-full.txt (../llms-full.txt), which contains the whole reference, grammar, standard library, and examples in one file. Compile errors carry hints for habits from other languages ( def , let , && , range() , append , None , ...), and tern check --json reports them in a form tools can read."},{"p":"1. Getting started","h":"1. Getting started","u":"tour/01-getting-started.html#1-getting-started","t":"This chapter gets Tern running on your machine. You will install the two things the compiler needs, run a first program, meet the four commands you will use every day, and learn how to read the compiler's error messages, which are designed to tell you what to write instead."},{"p":"1. Getting started","h":"What Tern is","u":"tour/01-getting-started.html#what-tern-is","t":"Tern is a small, statically typed, compiled language. It reads like Python: blocks are made by indentation, there are no semicolons or braces, and most programs need very few type annotations. Underneath, the compiler ( tern , itself written in Tern) translates your program to C, and clang turns the C into a fast native binary. There is no garbage collector and no runtime to install. The idea that sets Tern apart is that it has no references . Every value, including arrays, maps, and structs, behaves like a copy. A function can only change a variable of yours if its parameter says mut and you "},{"p":"1. Getting started","h":"Installing","u":"tour/01-getting-started.html#installing","t":"Tern runs on macOS and Linux (arm64 and x86_64). Install it with: That downloads the build for your machine, checks its SHA-256 sum, unpacks it into ~/.tern , and links the tern command into ~/.local/bin (it tells you if that directory isn't on your PATH yet). Running it again updates Tern in place. tern finds the standard library and runtime next to its own binary, so it works from any directory. You also need clang , which builds the C that Tern produces. On macOS, install the command line developer tools, which include clang: On Linux, install clang with your package manager (for example su"},{"p":"1. Getting started","h":"Hello, world","u":"tour/01-getting-started.html#hello-world","t":"Save this as hello.tn : and run it: A few things to notice already: - A program starts at fn main(): . There is no code at the top level of a file other than imports and definitions (functions, structs, enums). - The body of a function is indented, like Python. Use 4 spaces. - print takes any number of values and prints them separated by spaces, followed by a newline. Here is a slightly bigger program. You don't need to understand every line yet: greeting takes a str and an int and returns a str : the return type goes after the ) , with no arrow. f\"...\" is an f-string, which puts values into t"},{"p":"1. Getting started","h":"The commands","u":"tour/01-getting-started.html#the-commands","t":"You will mostly use four commands: - run compiles to a temporary binary and runs it. Anything after -- is passed to the program: tern run prog.tn -- input.txt . tern prog.tn input.txt is the short form. - check runs every compile-time check (types, mut rules, data races) without calling clang. It is the fastest way to find out whether a program is correct, and it is what your editor or an AI agent should run after every change. - build produces a real executable. A hello world is a few dozen kilobytes and starts instantly. - guide prints the language reference (../reference.md). guide strings "},{"p":"1. Getting started","h":"Reading an error message","u":"tour/01-getting-started.html#reading-an-error-message","t":"Tern tries hard to make its errors useful. Suppose you write Python out of habit: check prints each problem as file:line: error: message . When the mistake looks like something from another language, a hint: line follows with the Tern way to write it. (In this tour, the file name is left off, so the lines start with the line number.) All the errors in a file are reported at once, so you can fix them in one pass. Here the fixes are exactly what the hints say: Some mistakes can only be found while the program runs, such as reading past the end of an array. These stop the program with a message o"},{"p":"1. Getting started","h":"Try it","u":"tour/01-getting-started.html#try-it","t":"Write a program that prints a small multiplication table: the lines 1 x 7 = 7 through 5 x 7 = 35 . You will need a loop; for i in 1..6: counts from 1 to 5 (the end is not included). Use an f-string for each line. Then make a deliberate mistake, such as writing \"x\" + i instead of an f-string, and read what check tells you."},{"p":"1. Getting started","h":"Solution","u":"tour/01-getting-started.html#solution","t":"Next: 2. Values and types (02-values-and-types.md). Reference: \u00a71 Running programs (../reference.md 1-running-programs)."},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"2. Values and types","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#2-values-and-types","t":"This chapter covers the building blocks every program uses: variables, the four basic types ( int , float , bool , str ), arithmetic, and conversions. The main thing to learn is that Tern infers types for you but never converts between them silently: when an int meets a float , you say which one you want."},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"Variables","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#variables","t":"A variable is created the first time you assign to it. There is no let , var , or const : The compiler works out each variable's type from its first value: name is a str , age an int , height a float , and likes_tea a bool . That type is fixed from then on. Assigning a value of another type is an error, not a quiet change of meaning: You can write the type yourself with name: type = value . This is rarely needed for the basic types, but it documents intent, and it is how you create an empty collection ( chapter 6 (06-arrays-and-maps.md)): Every variable needs a value when it is created: x: int"},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"Numbers","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#numbers","t":"int is a 64-bit signed integer and float is a 64-bit floating-point number. A number with a dot is a float ; without one it is an int . Floats need digits on both sides of the dot ( 1.0 , 0.5 ), and underscores can group digits: The rules worth remembering: - / on two int s is integer division. It truncates toward zero, so 7 / 2 is 3 and -7 / 2 is -3 . There is no // . - % is the remainder, and its sign follows the left side. - There is no for powers; use math.pow ( import math ), or multiply. - Dividing an int by zero is a runtime error. int arithmetic wraps around on overflow rather than sto"},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"No implicit conversions","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#no-implicit-conversions","t":"In most languages 1 + 2.5 quietly turns the 1 into a float. Tern refuses, because this kind of silent conversion hides bugs (integer division where you wanted a fraction, or a fraction truncated where you wanted a whole number). You convert explicitly: float(i) turns an int into a float. int(f) turns a float into an int by dropping the fraction (toward zero). For rounding, use math.round , math.floor , or math.ceil ."},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"Booleans","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#booleans","t":"bool has two values, written true and false in lowercase. Combine them with the words and , or , and not (there is no && , , or ! ): and and or short-circuit: the right side runs only if it is needed. Comparisons ( == , != , < , <= , , = ) work on numbers and on strings. There is no \"truthiness\": an if needs a real bool , so write if len(xs) 0: rather than if xs: ."},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"Strings","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#strings","t":"A str is text in double quotes. + joins two strings; repeats one. To put another kind of value into a string, use str(x) or an f-string, which evaluates each {...} : Strings use double quotes only, and a string must fit on one line (use \\n for a line break). The usual escapes work: \\n , \\t , \\\" , \\\\ , and \\u00e9 or \\u{1F600} for any Unicode character. Chapter 7 (07-strings.md) covers strings in depth: slicing, searching, splitting, and formatting numbers."},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"Comments","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#comments","t":"A comment starts with and runs to the end of the line. There are no block comments or docstrings; write several lines."},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"Mistakes from other languages","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#mistakes-from-other-languages","t":"Because Tern looks like Python, Python habits are the most common source of errors. The compiler recognizes many of them and says what to write instead: Only the first error is shown here because the program could not be parsed past line 3. After fixing it, check reports the next one: unknown variable True , with the hint \"booleans are lowercase: true \"."},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"Try it","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#try-it","t":"A recipe needs 250 grams of flour for 4 people. Write a program that takes a number of people in a variable guests (an int ) and prints how much flour to use, as a float . Then print it again rounded down to whole grams, as an int . For 6 guests the answer is 375.0 grams. Make sure the computation does not lose the fraction for, say, 3 guests."},{"p":"2. Values and types","h":"Solution","u":"tour/02-values-and-types.html#solution","t":"Writing grams_for_4 guests / 4 would compute with ints and give 187 for 3 guests. Next: 3. Control flow (03-control-flow.md). Reference: \u00a72 Lexical rules (../reference.md 2-lexical-rules), \u00a73 Variables and types (../reference.md 3-variables-and-types), \u00a74 Operators (../reference.md 4-operators)."},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"3. Control flow","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#3-control-flow","t":"This chapter shows how a Tern program makes decisions and repeats work: if / elif / else , for loops over ranges and collections, while loops, and break , continue , and pass . It also explains one rule that surprises Python programmers: a variable created inside a block ends with that block."},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"if, elif, else","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#if-elif-else","t":"Conditions don't need parentheses. Each branch ends with : and an indented block: A block with a single statement may go on the same line as its : , as in the last line. Use this sparingly; most code reads better with the block on its own line. It is elif , never else if . The condition must be a bool : if count: is an error; write if count 0: . To choose between two values, use a conditional expression, as in Python: label = \"big\" if x 2 else \"small\" . Only the chosen side is evaluated. (There is no c ? a : b .) An if statement that assigns a variable does the same job for longer code, which "},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"Where variables live","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#where-variables-live","t":"A variable lives from its first assignment to the end of the block it was created in. So this does not compile: Each result exists only inside its own branch. The fix is to create the variable before the if , with a starting value, and then assign to it: This rule makes it impossible to read a variable that was only set on some paths, which is a common bug in languages where it is allowed."},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"for loops over ranges","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#for-loops-over-ranges","t":"for i in a..b: counts from a up to, but not including, b . There is no range() : A range whose start is not below its end runs zero times. There is no step value; to count down or skip numbers, use a while loop (below). Inside the loop, the loop variable is read-only: assigning i = 5 is an error, because it would not do what it looks like (the next round would not continue from 5)."},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"for loops over collections","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#for-loops-over-collections","t":"for x in xs: visits each element of an array, and for i, x in xs: also gives the index. Arrays get their own chapter ( chapter 6 (06-arrays-and-maps.md)), but loops over them are too useful to wait for: A for over a string visits it one byte at a time, as a one-byte str . Loops over maps ( for key, value in m: ) are covered with maps."},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"while loops","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#while-loops","t":"while repeats as long as its condition is true. Use it when you don't know the number of rounds in advance, or when a range doesn't fit, such as counting down: There is no do ... while or loop . Write while true: and leave with break ."},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"break, continue, and pass","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#break-continue-and-pass","t":"break leaves the innermost loop. continue skips to its next round. pass does nothing; it fills a block that must exist but has nothing to do:"},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"Mistakes from other languages","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#mistakes-from-other-languages","t":"The compiler knows the usual alternatives and points you to the Tern way:"},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"Try it","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#try-it","t":"Write FizzBuzz for 1 to 15: print Fizz for multiples of 3, Buzz for multiples of 5, FizzBuzz for multiples of both, and the number otherwise. Then change the program to stop at the first number above 10 that is a multiple of 3, using break ."},{"p":"3. Control flow","h":"Solution","u":"tour/03-control-flow.html#solution","t":"For the second part, add if i 10 and i % 3 == 0: break at the top of the loop body. Next: 4. Functions (04-functions.md). Reference: \u00a75 Control flow (../reference.md 5-control-flow)."},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"4. Functions","u":"tour/04-functions.html#4-functions","t":"This chapter shows how to define and call functions: typed parameters, return types, recursion, and the method-call shorthand x.f() . It also introduces the first of Tern's safety rules, that a function can't change its parameters, and shows how to return more than one result and report failure without exceptions."},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Defining a function","u":"tour/04-functions.html#defining-a-function","t":"A function starts with fn , lists its parameters with their types, and puts the return type after the ) : - Every parameter needs a type. Types are how the compiler catches mistakes at the call site, and they document the function for the next reader. - The return type follows the ) with no arrow, as in Go. ( - float is also accepted if you are used to Python annotations or Rust.) - A function with no return type, like announce , returns nothing. You can use return with no value to leave it early. - Functions can be called before they are defined in the file, so you can order them however read"},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Every path must return","u":"tour/04-functions.html#every-path-must-return","t":"If a function has a return type, every way through it must end in return . The compiler checks this: What should sign(0) return? The compiler can't guess, so it asks you to decide. Adding an else branch (or a final return \"zero\" ) fixes it."},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Recursion","u":"tour/04-functions.html#recursion","t":"Functions may call themselves:"},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Method-call syntax","u":"tour/04-functions.html#method-call-syntax","t":"There are no classes, but any function can be called as if it were a method: x.f(a, b) is exactly the same call as f(x, a, b) . The value before the dot becomes the first argument: This is how built-in functions like upper work too, and it is how you will write \"methods\" for your own structs in the next chapter (05-structs.md)."},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Parameters are read-only","u":"tour/04-functions.html#parameters-are-read-only","t":"Here is the first rule that is different from most languages. Inside a function, you can't change a parameter: Why? In Tern, a function receives its arguments as values, never as references. If a function could quietly change its parameters, a reader could not tell whether countdown(x) changes x or not. So parameters are read-only, and when a function is allowed to change the caller's variable, both the function and the call say so ( mut and & , chapter 9 (09-values-and-mut.md)). For now, the fix is the first half of the hint: copy the parameter into a local variable and change that. The copy "},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Returning more than one value","u":"tour/04-functions.html#returning-more-than-one-value","t":"To return several values, return a tuple: write the types in parentheses after the ) , return the values separated by commas, and unpack them at the call: For more than two or three values, a struct (the next chapter) with named fields reads better. A function can also change variables the caller passes in with & , when their parameters are declared mut : lo: mut int means \"this function may change the caller's variable\", and the caller marks each such argument with & . You can see at the call site exactly which variables might change. Chapter 9 (09-values-and-mut.md) explains mut fully."},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Reporting failure","u":"tour/04-functions.html#reporting-failure","t":"There are no exceptions. A function that can fail returns a Result : either Ok(value) or Err(why) . The caller checks which it got, here with is , which also names the value inside: A value that may simply be missing is an Option : Some(value) or None . Chapter 8 (08-enums-and-match.md) has more on both, including ? , which passes a failure up to the caller in one character. text.to_int(-1) parses a string as an int, giving -1 if it isn't a number. The strings chapter (07-strings.md) covers it and similar functions."},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Sharing a name with a builtin","u":"tour/04-functions.html#sharing-a-name-with-a-builtin","t":"Built-in functions such as get , find , len , and min need no import. Your own functions can have the same names. A call goes to yours when its first argument fits your first parameter, and to the builtin otherwise: The full list of builtins is in \u00a720 of the reference (../reference.md 20-builtin-names)."},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Try it","u":"tour/04-functions.html#try-it","t":"Write fn is_prime(n: int) bool that checks divisors from 2 while d d <= n , and use it to print the prime numbers below 30 on one line. Since print always ends the line, build the line as a string first and print it once. Then write fn count_primes(limit: int) int that calls is_prime as a method: n.is_prime() ."},{"p":"4. Functions","h":"Solution","u":"tour/04-functions.html#solution","t":"Next: 5. Structs (05-structs.md). Reference: \u00a76 Functions (../reference.md 6-functions)."},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"5. Structs","u":"tour/05-structs.html#5-structs","t":"A struct groups related values under one name. This chapter shows how to define structs, build them, read and change their fields, nest them, and give them \"methods\" by writing ordinary functions. Tern has no classes, self , or inheritance; structs plus functions cover the same ground with less machinery."},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"Defining and building a struct","u":"tour/05-structs.html#defining-and-building-a-struct","t":"A struct lists its fields and their types. Fields of the same type can share a line: You build a struct by calling its name like a function. Give the fields in order, or name them ( title=\"Emma\" ) in any order. Named arguments exist only for this: they make long constructor calls readable, and they don't break when someone reorders the fields. print shows a whole struct as Name{field: value, ...} , which is handy while debugging."},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"Every field is required","u":"tour/05-structs.html#every-field-is-required","t":"A struct has no default field values, and every field must be given: When you want defaults, write a function that builds the struct. By convention it is named new_something :"},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"Changing fields","u":"tour/05-structs.html#changing-fields","t":"Fields of a struct in a variable can be assigned directly, including fields of nested structs and arrays inside the struct:"},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"Methods are functions","u":"tour/05-structs.html#methods-are-functions","t":"To give a struct behavior, write a function that takes it as the first parameter. Thanks to method-call syntax ( chapter 4 (04-functions.md method-call-syntax)), you can call it with a dot: r.area() and area(r) are the same call. There is no special \"inside the struct\" scope: a method is just a function, so it can live in any file and anyone can add one. Notice that scaled returns a new Rect instead of changing r . That is the natural style in Tern, because parameters are read-only. When a function should change the struct in place, mark the parameter mut : With a mut first parameter, visits.t"},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"Mistakes from other languages","u":"tour/05-structs.html#mistakes-from-other-languages","t":"Structs hold data only. Writing a class, or a method with self inside the struct, gets a hint pointing you to plain functions: == compares two structs field by field (and the arrays and maps inside them element by element), so two points with the same coordinates are equal. When \"equal\" means something else, such as \"the same id\", write a function that says so: A struct can also be a map key: seen: {Point: bool} = {} ."},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"Structs that contain structs","u":"tour/05-structs.html#structs-that-contain-structs","t":"A struct can contain other structs, arrays of structs, and even an array of its own type, which is how you make a tree: A struct can't contain itself directly (a Folder field inside Folder would be infinitely large). For graphs where nodes point at each other, keep the nodes in an array and store indexes, as shown in the reference (../reference.md 21-idioms)."},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"Try it","u":"tour/05-structs.html#try-it","t":"Define struct Temperature with a celsius: float field. Write to_fahrenheit(t: Temperature) float ( c 9 / 5 + 32 ) and warmer(t: Temperature, by: float) Temperature , which returns a new value. Then make a mut version, warm(t: mut Temperature, by: float) , and call both styles from main ."},{"p":"5. Structs","h":"Solution","u":"tour/05-structs.html#solution","t":"Next: 6. Arrays and maps (06-arrays-and-maps.md). Reference: \u00a77 Structs (../reference.md 7-structs)."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"6. Arrays and maps","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#6-arrays-and-maps","t":"Most programs work with collections. Tern has two: arrays ( T , an ordered list of values of one type) and maps ( {K: V} , a table from keys to values). This chapter shows how to build, read, change, loop over, and search both, and the one rule about changing a collection while you loop over it."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Arrays","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#arrays","t":"An array literal is a list in square brackets. All elements have the same type, and the array's type is written int , str , Point , and so on: - Indexes start at 0. There are no negative indexes; the last element is xs len(xs) - 1 . - Reading or writing outside the array stops the program with a runtime error such as index 3 out of bounds (length 3) . So does pop() on an empty array. - x in xs tells you whether the array contains x . - It is push , not append , and len(xs) , not xs.length . The compiler's hints remind you."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Empty arrays and repeated values","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#empty-arrays-and-repeated-values","t":"An empty array needs a type, either written out or taken from its first use: value n makes an array of n copies of a value. For the 2D board , each row is its own copy, so writing board 0 1 changes only the first row. (In Python, 0 3 2 shares one row between both, a classic bug. In Tern, values are never shared.) If the compiler can't tell what an empty array will hold, it asks:"},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Slices","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#slices","t":"xs a..b is a new array of the elements from index a up to, not including, b . Leave out either end to go to the start or the end: A slice is a copy: changing rest does not change days ."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Looping and changing","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#looping-and-changing","t":"You already know for x in xs: and for i, x in xs: . While a loop runs over an array, the array can't be changed, because it is unclear what the loop should see: The two usual fixes are exactly what the error says. To change elements in place, loop over the indexes. To add or drop elements, build a new array:"},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Sorting, filtering, and mapping","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#sorting-filtering-and-mapping","t":"Arrays have three functions that take another function as an argument. You will learn to write such functions yourself in chapter 10 (10-closures.md); for now, read fn(x) x 2 as \"a function that, given x , returns x 2 \": All three return new arrays and leave the original alone. sort_by sorts by a key that is an int , float , or str , and it is stable (equal keys keep their order). For the plain cases there are shortcuts: temps.sorted() is temps.sort_by(fn(t) t) , temps.reversed() is a reversed copy, and sum(temps) adds them up ( 119 here)."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Maps","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#maps","t":"A map stores values under keys. Keys are int or str , and a map remembers the order in which keys were first inserted: Reading a key that isn't there with m k is a runtime error ( key not found ). When a key might be missing, check with k in m , or use m.get(k, default) , which returns the default instead. For \"not in\", write not (k in m) , with the parentheses. An empty map needs a type too: counts: {str: int} = {} ."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Counting and grouping","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#counting-and-grouping","t":"Two patterns come up constantly. Counting uses get with a default of 0 ; grouping creates a list the first time a key is seen: by_length n .push(w) changes the array stored in the map in place, just as hero.inventory.push(...) changed an array inside a struct in the last chapter. A map can't be changed while you loop over it either. To update every value, loop over a copy of its keys: for k in m.keys(): m k = 0 ."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Arrays of structs","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#arrays-of-structs","t":"Collections of structs are the bread and butter of real programs: tasks 0 .hours -= 1 changes the element in the array. Note that t = tasks 0 followed by t.hours -= 1 would change only t , a copy. Chapter 9 (09-values-and-mut.md) is all about this."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Try it","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#try-it","t":"Given a list of (name, department) pairs written as two parallel arrays, names = \"ana\", \"ben\", \"cy\", \"dee\", \"eli\" and depts = \"eng\", \"ops\", \"eng\", \"sales\", \"ops\" , build a map from department to the list of names in it, then print each department with its head count, sorted by department name."},{"p":"6. Arrays and maps","h":"Solution","u":"tour/06-arrays-and-maps.html#solution","t":"Next: 7. Strings (07-strings.md). Reference: \u00a78 Arrays (../reference.md 8-arrays-t), \u00a711 Maps (../reference.md 11-maps-k-v)."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"7. Strings","u":"tour/07-strings.html#7-strings","t":"Text processing is a large part of everyday programming: reading input, splitting lines, searching, and formatting output. This chapter covers Tern's str type in depth: indexing and slicing, the built-in string functions, parsing numbers, f-strings, and the fmt module for formatting numbers. It ends with a small parser that ties them together."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Strings are values","u":"tour/07-strings.html#strings-are-values","t":"A str is a sequence of bytes, written in double quotes. Like every value in Tern, a string behaves like a copy, and it can't be changed in place. Functions like upper and replace return a new string: Assigning into a string is an error, and so is using int() to parse one: Building a long string with += in a loop is fine: when nothing else shares the string, Tern grows it in place."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Indexing and slicing","u":"tour/07-strings.html#indexing-and-slicing","t":"len(s) is the length in bytes. s i is the byte at index i , as a one-byte string, and s a..b is a substring, with the same rules as array slices: For ASCII text (English letters, digits, punctuation) a byte is a character. Text in other scripts is stored as UTF-8, where one character can take several bytes, so len(\"h\u00e9llo\") is 6, and s 1 of it is only the first half of \u00e9 . Strings as a whole (printing, comparing, find , split , replace ) work fine with any UTF-8 text; only the positions are in bytes."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Searching and testing","u":"tour/07-strings.html#searching-and-testing","t":"s.find(sub) returns the index of the first match, or -1 when there is none. sub in s is the same test as s.contains(sub) ."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Changing case, trimming, replacing","u":"tour/07-strings.html#changing-case-trimming-replacing","t":"trim removes whitespace at both ends. replace replaces every occurrence. upper and lower change ASCII letters only."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Splitting and joining","u":"tour/07-strings.html#splitting-and-joining","t":"s.split(sep) cuts a string into an array of pieces; sep.join(parts) glues an array of strings together. As in Python, join is called on the separator: Empty pieces are kept: \"a,,b\".split(\",\") has three elements."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Parsing numbers","u":"tour/07-strings.html#parsing-numbers","t":"s.to_int(default) and s.to_float(default) parse a whole string as a number. If the string is not a number, you get the default you passed, so there is no exception to handle. Pick a default that can't be a real value, or check first: Going the other way, str(x) turns an int , float , or bool into text."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Bytes and characters","u":"tour/07-strings.html#bytes-and-characters","t":"s.byte(i) gives the byte at i as an int , and chr(n) makes a one-byte string from a number. Together they handle simple character arithmetic, like a Caesar cipher: To write a byte or a character by its number, use an escape: \\xNN is one byte, and \\u00e9 or \\u{1F600} is a Unicode character (stored as its UTF-8 bytes, so it can take up to four): For binary data (an image's pixels, a file's bytes, a network buffer), use byte : one byte per element, numbers from 0 to 255. s.to_bytes() turns a string into one and bs.to_str() turns it back, without copying. Arithmetic on bytes gives an int , and byt"},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"f-strings and formatting","u":"tour/07-strings.html#f-strings-and-formatting","t":"An f-string evaluates each {expression} and inserts the result. The value must be an int , float , bool , or str . {{ and }} are literal braces: f-strings have no format specs. For fixed decimals, padding, and digit grouping, use the standard fmt module: import fmt makes the module's functions available as fmt.name(...) . Modules are covered in chapter 12 (12-modules.md), and the fmt page (../std/fmt.md) lists every function."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Putting it together","u":"tour/07-strings.html#putting-it-together","t":"A tiny parser for key = value configuration lines, skipping blanks and comments. (The loop variable raw is read-only, like all loop variables, so the trimmed line gets its own name.)"},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Try it","u":"tour/07-strings.html#try-it","t":"Write fn is_palindrome(s: str) bool that ignores case and spaces, so \"Never odd or even\" is a palindrome. Build a cleaned string with only the non-space characters, lowercased, then compare bytes from both ends toward the middle with a while loop."},{"p":"7. Strings","h":"Solution","u":"tour/07-strings.html#solution","t":"Next: 8. Enums and match (08-enums-and-match.md). Reference: \u00a710 Strings (../reference.md 10-strings-str), \u00a712 Printing (../reference.md 12-printing)."},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"8. Enums and match","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#8-enums-and-match","t":"An enum is a type whose value is one of a fixed set of variants, each of which can carry its own data. Together with match , enums replace several features from other languages: constants, None / null , exceptions for expected failures, and class hierarchies. This chapter shows how to define enums, take them apart with match , and use match on plain numbers and strings."},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"A simple enum","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#a-simple-enum","t":"The simplest enum is a list of names: You write a variant as Enum.Variant . Variants without data can be compared with == and != , and they print as Suit.Hearts . Compared with using strings ( \"hearts\" ) or numbers ( 1 ), an enum can't be misspelled and can't hold a value outside the set, and the compiler can check that you handled every case."},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"match","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#match","t":"match picks a block by the variant of a value. Each arm names a variant and has its own block; there is no fallthrough and no case keyword: In a pattern you can write the bare variant name ( Red ) or the full one ( Light.Red ). A match on an enum must handle every variant. If you forget one, the compiler lists what is missing. That is what makes enums safe to extend: add a variant, and the compiler shows you every match that needs updating. When you really do want to treat the rest alike, end with a _: arm, which matches anything."},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"Variants that carry data","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#variants-that-carry-data","t":"Each variant can have its own fields, written like function parameters. match binds them to names, by position: - Build a variant like a struct: Shape.Circle(1.0) or with names, Shape.Rect(w=2.0, h=3.0) . - In a pattern, _ skips a value you don't need ( Rect(w, _) ). - The names in the pattern are yours to choose: Circle(r) binds the radius to r . The data inside a variant can only be read with match , never with a dot. That is the point: a Shape might not be a circle, so s.radius would be a bug waiting to happen. == compares the variant and then its values:"},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"\"Maybe\" values without null","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#maybe-values-without-null","t":"Tern has no null . When a result may be missing, say so with an enum. The caller then has to handle the missing case, because match must be complete: This shape is so common that two generic enums are built in. Option T is Some(value) or None ; Result T, E is Ok(value) or Err(error) , for operations that can fail with a reason. x is Pattern tests one variant and, in an if or while condition, names its values; x? gives the value inside Some / Ok , or returns the None / Err from the function right away:"},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"Recursive enums","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#recursive-enums","t":"A variant can hold values of its own enum, which makes trees easy. Here is a tiny calculator:"},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"match on numbers, strings, and bools","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#match-on-numbers-strings-and-bools","t":"match also works on int , str , and bool values, with literal patterns. Like a match on an enum, it must handle every value, so it needs a _: arm for \"anything else\" (a bool match can list true and false instead). Write _: pass when the rest needs nothing: There are no guards ( Circle(r) if r 1 ); put an if inside the arm instead. There is no switch : match is the only multi-way branch besides if / elif ."},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"Try it","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#try-it","t":"Model a vending machine's state as an enum: Idle , HasMoney(cents: int) , and Dispensing(item: str) . Write fn step(s: State, coin: int) State that moves from Idle to HasMoney when a coin arrives, adds coins while in HasMoney , and switches to Dispensing(\"soda\") once 150 cents or more have been put in; from Dispensing it goes back to Idle . Feed it the coins 100, 25, 25, 0, 50 and print each state."},{"p":"8. Enums and match","h":"Solution","u":"tour/08-enums-and-match.html#solution","t":"Next: 9. Values, mut, and & (09-values-and-mut.md). Reference: \u00a79 Enums and match (../reference.md 9-enums-and-match)."},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"9. Values, mut, and &","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#9-values-mut-and-","t":"This is the chapter that explains what makes Tern different. In Tern every value behaves like a copy: there are no references, so two variables never share data. A function can change a variable of yours only when its parameter says mut and you pass the variable with & . You will learn how this works, why it is cheap, what take is for, and how these rules turn aliasing bugs into compile errors."},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"Assignment copies","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#assignment-copies","t":"In Python, JavaScript, Java, or Go, assigning a list or an object gives you a second name for the same data. Change it through one name and the other sees the change. In Tern, assignment always gives you an independent value: This holds everywhere a value moves: passing an argument, storing a value in a struct or an array, taking a value out of a map, returning from a function. Here is the classic bug that this prevents. A team is built from a list, and later the list is changed for another purpose: In a language with references, core.members and roster would be the same list, and adding cy to"},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"Copies are cheap: copy-on-write","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#copies-are-cheap-copy-on-write","t":"Copying a million-element array on every assignment would be slow, so Tern doesn't. Arrays, maps, and strings share their storage until one side is written, and only then is the data copied. Reading never copies: Running this with TERN_STATS=1 tern run prog.tn prints a summary line to stderr that includes 1 copy-on-write copies : the only copy happened when c was written. And when the compiler can prove a value is never shared, it skips even the check. So you can write code as if everything is copied, and pay only for copies you actually need. The memory guide (../guides/memory.md) explains ho"},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"Parameters are read-only","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#parameters-are-read-only","t":"You met this rule in chapter 4 (04-functions.md parameters-are-read-only). A parameter can't be assigned, and a collection parameter can't be changed with push , pop , or element assignment: Why not just let the function change its own copy, as Python does with numbers? Because the reader of add_bonus(s, 5) would then have to guess: does this change s or not? In Tern there are two clear options, and both are visible in the code: 1. Return a new value. The caller decides what to do with it: s = with_bonus(s, 5) . 2. Declare the parameter mut , and have the caller pass &s ."},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"mut and &","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#mut-and-","t":"A mut parameter lets the function change the caller's variable. The caller must write & before the argument, so every argument a call might change is marked (the one exception, method syntax on a mut first parameter, is below): Forgetting the & is an error, not a silent copy: & can be put before a variable, a field ( &game.player ), or an element ( &accounts 2 ), and only as an argument to a mut parameter. It is not a pointer; you can't store it or return it. It means \"this call may change this, right now\". With method syntax, the dot plays the role of the & : when the first parameter is mut ,"},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"Elements are values too","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#elements-are-values-too","t":"Taking an element out of an array gives you a copy. To change the element itself, write through the array, or pass the element with & : The same goes for for a in accts: . The loop variable is a (read-only) copy of each element; to change elements, loop over indexes and write accts i ."},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"Aliasing is a compile error","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#aliasing-is-a-compile-error","t":"If a function could receive the same value twice, once as mut and once more, a change through one parameter would show up in the other in the middle of the function. This is called aliasing, and it causes some of the hardest bugs to find in C, C++, and Go. Tern refuses such calls: In a language with references, append_all(names, names) would loop over src while the same list grows through dst , and never finish. The fix is to say what you mean: pass a copy, which is a separate value. The check is conservative for array elements. The compiler can't always prove that two indexes differ, so trans"},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"take: handing a value over","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#take-handing-a-value-over","t":"There is a third parameter mode. A take parameter receives the value itself: the function owns it and may change it freely, and the caller can no longer use it. Use it when a function consumes a large value and returns a modified version, to avoid a copy: normalized changes the array it was given and returns it, so no copy is ever made. The price is that after normalized(raw) , raw is gone. Using it again is an error: If you still need the value, pass copy(data) , an explicit copy. You will rarely need take at all: plain parameters and copy-on-write already avoid copies in almost all code. Rea"},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"Why it's worth it","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#why-its-worth-it","t":"These rules can feel strict for the first few programs. Here is what they buy: - Local reasoning. To know what a call can change, read the call: only & arguments (and the value before the dot for a mut method). Nothing else in your program can change. - No aliasing bugs. Two names never refer to the same data, so a change here never shows up unexpectedly over there. - Safe parallelism. Because nothing is shared implicitly, the compiler can check that parallel tasks don't write the same data, which is the subject of chapter 11 (11-parallelism.md). - No garbage collector and no lifetimes. Every "},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"Try it","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#try-it","t":"Write a small bank: struct Bank with an accounts: {str: int} map from owner to balance. Write fn open_account(b: mut Bank, owner: str) , fn deposit(b: mut Bank, owner: str, amount: int) , and a read-only fn total(b: Bank) int . Take a snapshot before = bank , make some deposits, and print both totals to see that the snapshot didn't change."},{"p":"9. Values, mut, and &","h":"Solution","u":"tour/09-values-and-mut.html#solution","t":"Next: 10. Functions as values (10-closures.md). Reference: \u00a714 Values, mut, and the safety rules (../reference.md 14-values-mut-and-the-safety-rules). Guide: How memory works (../guides/memory.md)."},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"10. Functions as values","u":"tour/10-closures.html#10-functions-as-values","t":"Functions in Tern are values: you can store them in variables, pass them to other functions, return them, and keep them in struct fields and arrays. This chapter covers lambdas ( fn(x) x 2 ), nested functions, function types, and how closures capture variables, then puts map , filter , and sort_by to real use."},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"Lambdas","u":"tour/10-closures.html#lambdas","t":"A lambda is a small unnamed function: fn(parameters) expression . It has no : and no return ; its body is one expression, and that expression's value is the result: When the lambda is passed somewhere that already says the parameter types, you can leave them out. That is the common case with map , filter , and sort_by : When nothing says the type, the compiler asks you to write it:"},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"Function types","u":"tour/10-closures.html#function-types","t":"The type of a function is written like its signature without names: fn(int) int takes an int and returns an int ; fn(str, int) bool takes two values and returns a bool ; fn(str) returns nothing. Use these types for parameters, return values, struct fields, and array elements: make_multiplier returns a new function each time it is called, and each one remembers its own k . A function that remembers values from where it was created is called a closure."},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"Nested functions","u":"tour/10-closures.html#nested-functions","t":"A lambda is limited to one expression. For anything longer, define a named function inside another function. It can use the enclosing function's variables, can call itself, and can be passed by name like any other function:"},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"Closures capture by copy","u":"tour/10-closures.html#closures-capture-by-copy","t":"A closure copies the variables it uses at the moment it is created. Changing the variable afterwards does not affect the closure, and the closure can't change the variable: This is the same rule as everywhere else in Tern: values are copies. It means a closure can never change your variables behind your back, and a closure that is stored and called later (for example as a button's click handler) behaves the same no matter what happened in the meantime. Trying to change a captured variable is an error: The error names both fixes. Either return the new value and let the caller store it, or pass "},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"Functions in structs","u":"tour/10-closures.html#functions-in-structs","t":"A struct field can hold a function. Calling b.on_click(3) calls the function stored in the field. This is how you write callbacks and simple plug-in behavior without classes or interfaces:"},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"map, filter, and sort_by in practice","u":"tour/10-closures.html#map-filter-and-sort_by-in-practice","t":"With lambdas, most collection processing becomes a short chain. Each call returns a new array, so the original is never changed: - sort_by(key) sorts by the key the function returns: an int , float , or str . To sort numbers in descending order, negate the key. - The sort is stable: elements with equal keys keep their original order (Heat stays before Up). To sort by two keys, sort by the second key first, then by the first. - There is no reduce . A sum or a maximum is a three-line loop, which is also the fastest way to write it."},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"Mistakes from other languages","u":"tour/10-closures.html#mistakes-from-other-languages","t":"Arrow functions ( x = x + 1 ) and Rust closures ( x x + 1 ) get similar hints."},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"Try it","u":"tour/10-closures.html#try-it","t":"Write fn compose(f: fn(int) int, g: fn(int) int) fn(int) int that returns a function applying f and then g . Use it to build inc_then_square from two lambdas, and apply it to 1, 2, 3 with map . Then sort the words \"kiwi\", \"fig\", \"banana\", \"date\" by length, keeping alphabetical order for words of the same length (hint: two sort_by calls)."},{"p":"10. Functions as values","h":"Solution","u":"tour/10-closures.html#solution","t":"Next: 11. Parallelism (11-parallelism.md). Reference: \u00a713 Functions as values (../reference.md 13-functions-as-values-closures)."},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"11. Parallelism","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#11-parallelism","t":"Modern computers have many cores, and Tern makes using them a matter of one keyword. This chapter covers parallel: blocks (run a few different things at once), do: groups, and parallel for (spread a loop across all cores). Because Tern has no shared references, the compiler can check every parallel block for data races, and this chapter shows the errors it gives and how to fix each one."},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"parallel: blocks","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#parallel-blocks","t":"Inside a parallel: block, every statement is a separate task, and all of them run at the same time. The block ends when every task has finished: The two calls run on two cores. A variable created by a one-line task, like low and high , can be used after the block. There are no threads to create, no locks, and nothing to join: the block itself waits."},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"do: groups","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#do-groups","t":"When one task needs several statements, group them with do: . The statements inside a do: run in order, as one task, alongside the other tasks of the block: Variables created inside a do: group are local to it, like ev here. To use a value computed in a do: group after the block, create the variable before the block (as with evens and largest ) and assign it inside. Every task can read data , because nobody writes it."},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"The race checker","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#the-race-checker","t":"A data race happens when two tasks use the same variable at the same time and at least one of them writes it. The result then depends on timing, and the bug may show up once in a thousand runs. In Tern, a race is a compile error: The checker can be exact because Tern has no references. A task can only reach a variable by naming it (or passing it with & to a mut parameter), so the compiler sees every read and write. In a language with pointers, two innocent-looking names might refer to the same data. The fix is what the message says: give each task its own variable, and combine after the block,"},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"parallel for","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#parallel-for","t":"parallel for i in a..b: runs the iterations of a loop at the same time, spread across all cores. It is the tool for \"do the same work to every element\": The rule is simple: iteration i may write only element i of an array ( steps i , or fields of it, like ps i .x ). It may read anything that no iteration writes."},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"parallel for races and their fixes","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#parallel-for-races-and-their-fixes","t":"The two classic mistakes are accumulating into one variable, and reading a neighbor that another iteration is writing: For a total, let each iteration write its own slot, then combine after the loop. When each iteration does very little (like one addition), give each iteration a whole chunk of the array instead, so the per-task overhead is spread over real work: The running-sum loop ( smooth i = smooth i - 1 + ... ) is different: each step depends on the previous one, so it is not parallel work at all. Write it as an ordinary for loop. The compiler's error is telling you something true about t"},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"When to use it","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#when-to-use-it","t":"- Use parallel: for a few independent, sizable pieces of work: two searches, loading and parsing different files, computing several statistics of one data set. - Use parallel for when every element needs the same, reasonably expensive work. - Don't parallelize tiny loops. Starting tasks costs a little; if each iteration is a single addition, the plain loop is faster. Measure. - Output from print inside parallel tasks can appear in any order. Compute in parallel, then print after the block. - On WebAssembly, parallel blocks run their tasks one after another, so programs still work there, just w"},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"Try it","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#try-it","t":"You have the scores of 8 students on 5 tests each, as an array of arrays. With a parallel for , compute every student's average into an array of floats. Then, in a parallel: block, compute the best average and the worst average at the same time (two do: groups), and print both."},{"p":"11. Parallelism","h":"Solution","u":"tour/11-parallelism.html#solution","t":"Next: 12. Modules and packages (12-modules.md). Reference: \u00a715 Parallelism (../reference.md 15-parallelism). Guide: Parallel programming (../guides/parallelism.md)."},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"12. Modules and packages","u":"tour/12-modules.html#12-modules-and-packages","t":"Real programs outgrow one file. This chapter shows how Tern splits a program into modules (one file each) and packages (a directory of files), how import finds them, how to use the names they define, and how to keep helper functions private with a leading _ . There is no package manager and no configuration file: the directory layout is the whole story."},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"Using a module","u":"tour/12-modules.html#using-a-module","t":"You have already used modules from the standard library. import name makes a module available, and its functions and types are then written name.thing : from name import a, b brings specific names in so you can use them without the prefix. The module name is not in scope then, unless you also import it: Asking for a name the module doesn't have is caught at compile time: The standard library overview (../std/index.md) lists every module and function."},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"Your own modules","u":"tour/12-modules.html#your-own-modules","t":"Every .tn file is a module, named after the file. Put money.tn next to your main file, and import money finds it. A module contains the same things as any Tern file (imports, structs, enums, functions), but no fn main() : the program starts in the file you run. Here is a small program in three parts. First, money.tn , a module that handles prices in cents so that amounts never suffer from floating-point rounding: Other files refer to its type as money.Price and to its functions as money.from_cents , and so on. Modules can import other modules, including standard ones, as money.tn imports fmt ."},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"Packages: a directory is one module","u":"tour/12-modules.html#packages-a-directory-is-one-module","t":"When a module grows too big for one file, make it a directory. All the .tn files in a directory together form one package, imported by the directory's name. Files in the same package share one namespace: they see each other's structs and functions without importing anything. Running it: Notice how the \"methods\" work across modules. s.add_item(...) in main.tn finds add_item even though it lives in inventory : x.f(...) looks for f in the module where the type of x is defined. Likewise it.price.show() finds money.show , because Price is defined in money . This is what lets you call d.exec(...) on"},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"How import finds a module","u":"tour/12-modules.html#how-import-finds-a-module","t":"For import name , the compiler looks in this order and takes the first match: 1. library/std/name.tn , a standard library module; 2. library/name/ , a first-party package such as roost , queue , or pg ; 3. name.tn in the directory of the main file (the file you passed to run , check , or build ); 4. name/ , a directory of .tn files next to the main file; 5. deps/name/ , a third-party package fetched by tern get (only in a project with a tern.mod ). Some consequences: - Imports are always resolved from the main file's directory, even inside a package. That is why inventory/item.tn writes import"},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"Private names","u":"tour/12-modules.html#private-names","t":"A struct, enum, or function whose name starts with _ is private to its module (or package): other modules can't use it, with or without from ... import . Use this for helpers that are not part of the module's promise to its users, so you can change them freely: The same error appears for inventory._tidy(\"x\") in the shop example. Inside inventory , both item.tn and stock.tn may call _tidy , because they are the same package."},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"Sharing code between projects","u":"tour/12-modules.html#sharing-code-between-projects","t":"There is no package manager, registry, or version file. To reuse a module in another project, copy its file or directory next to that project's main file. For a library of your own, keep it in its own repository and copy (or link) it in. This keeps builds simple and reproducible: everything a program uses is either in std/ or in its own directory."},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"Try it","u":"tour/12-modules.html#try-it","t":"Split the bank from chapter 9 (09-values-and-mut.md try-it) into two files: bank.tn with the Bank struct and its functions (make a private _check_owner(b: Bank, owner: str) bool helper that deposit uses to ignore unknown owners), and main.tn that imports it and calls b.deposit(...) with method syntax. Then try calling bank._check_owner from main.tn and read the error."},{"p":"12. Modules and packages","h":"Solution","u":"tour/12-modules.html#solution","t":"Next: 13. Where to go next (13-next-steps.md). Reference: \u00a717 Modules and packages (../reference.md 17-modules-and-packages)."},{"p":"13. Where to go next","h":"13. Where to go next","u":"tour/13-next-steps.html#13-where-to-go-next","t":"You have now seen all of Tern: values and types, control flow, functions, structs, arrays and maps, strings, enums, the value rules with mut and & , closures, parallelism, and modules. This last chapter puts everything together in one complete program, then points you to the rest of the documentation, depending on what you want to build."},{"p":"13. Where to go next","h":"A complete program","u":"tour/13-next-steps.html#a-complete-program","t":"This program reads a small warehouse log, applies it to an inventory, and prints a report. It uses almost every feature from the tour: structs and an enum for the data, match to apply events, mut to update the inventory in place, a map to index items by SKU, string parsing for the input, a parallel: block for two independent summaries, closures with filter and sort_by , and the fmt module for the table. Things to notice: - apply takes items as mut and index read-only, so the call apply(&items, index, ...) tells you exactly what can change. - Errors are ordinary values: parse returns a Bad vari"},{"p":"13. Where to go next","h":"Try it","u":"tour/13-next-steps.html#try-it","t":"Extend the program: add a Price(sku: str, cents: int) event, written price A1 2750 in the log, that changes an item's price. The compiler will point at the match in apply until you handle the new variant. Then add a second report line with the most valuable item."},{"p":"13. Where to go next","h":"The rest of the documentation","u":"tour/13-next-steps.html#the-rest-of-the-documentation","t":"Guides explain one topic in depth: - Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript (../guides/coming-from.md): a translation table for your habits, and the mistakes people make most often. - How memory works (../guides/memory.md): copy-on-write, ownership, and why there is no garbage collector. - Parallel programming (../guides/parallelism.md): more patterns for parallel and parallel for , and how the race checker thinks. - Performance tips (../guides/performance.md): how to write fast Tern, and how to measure. - Building web apps (../guides/web-apps.md): HTTP servers, JSON, and SQLite. - Callin"},{"p":"13. Where to go next","h":"Some small projects to try","u":"tour/13-next-steps.html#some-small-projects-to-try","t":"- A word-frequency counter: read a file with fs.read , split it into words, count them in a map, and print the top 10 with sort_by . - A to-do list on the command line: store tasks as JSON with json.encode and json.decode , and take commands from os.args() . - A Mandelbrot or Game of Life renderer that prints ASCII art, then speed it up with parallel for over the rows. - A small JSON API with http and sqlite , following the web apps guide (../guides/web-apps.md). Whatever you build, run tern check often. The compiler's messages, and their hints, are the fastest teacher Tern has. Back to the st"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","u":"guides/coming-from.html#coming-from-python-go-rust-or-javascript","t":"This guide translates what you already know into Tern. There is one section per language. Each shows common code side by side with its Tern version, lists what carries over and what is different, and ends with the mistakes people from that language make most often, with the compiler's actual error message and hint. Read the section for the language you know best; the language reference (../reference.md) has the complete rules."},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Tern in one paragraph","u":"guides/coming-from.html#tern-in-one-paragraph","t":"Tern reads like Python but is statically typed and compiled to native code. Every value is a copy : assigning or passing an array, map, or struct never creates a second name for the same data (copies are lazy, so this is cheap). There are no references, pointers, None / null , classes, exceptions, or generics. A function can change its caller's variable only if its parameter is declared mut and the caller writes & at the call site. Because nothing is shared, the compiler can check parallel code for data races before it runs. You are used to In Tern --- --- classes and methods a struct plus fun"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Coming from Python","u":"guides/coming-from.html#coming-from-python","t":""},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Side by side","u":"guides/coming-from.html#side-by-side","t":"A class becomes a struct plus functions. Where Python would give a field a default, write a constructor function. Method-call syntax still works: acct.deposit(50) calls deposit(&acct, 50) when the first parameter is mut . A function that returns None when it finds nothing returns a sentinel, or fills a mut parameter and returns whether it found something. An exception becomes a returned status. Comprehensions become map and filter , which take a lambda written fn(x) expression . sorted(key=...) becomes sort_by , which returns a new array (plain sorted(xs) is xs.sorted() ). sum takes an array, "},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"What carries over","u":"guides/coming-from.html#what-carries-over","t":"- Indentation for blocks, : to open them, comments, if / elif / else , while , for x in xs: , break , continue , pass . - and , or , not , in ; f-strings; slices like s 1.. , xs ..2 (with .. , not : ). - String methods under slightly different names: split , join (called on the separator, as in Python), replace , find , upper , lower , trim (for strip ), starts_with , ends_with . - Dicts and lists as built-in {K: V} maps and T arrays. Maps keep insertion order."},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"What's different","u":"guides/coming-from.html#whats-different","t":"- Types are required on parameters and return values, and a variable's type can't change. 1 + 2.0 is an error: convert with float(1) . - Assignment copies. b = a then b.push(4) leaves a unchanged. In Python both names would see the change. There is no copy.deepcopy , because you never need it. - Parameters are read-only. A Python function can quietly append to the list you pass it; a Tern function can't, unless the parameter is mut and you pass &xs . The & at the call site tells the reader which arguments may change. - True / False / None are true / false /(nothing). There is no range() : writ"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Common mistakes","u":"guides/coming-from.html#common-mistakes","t":"Calling Python's list methods: Returning None : Say that the result may be missing: return an Option int , and the caller unwraps it. Writing a class: A format spec in an f-string: A list comprehension: Adding a number to a string: Changing a list that was passed in: The fix is fn add_tag(tags: mut str , t: str) called as add_tag(&tags, \"sale\") , or tags.add_tag(\"sale\") ."},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Coming from Go","u":"guides/coming-from.html#coming-from-go","t":""},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Side by side","u":"guides/coming-from.html#side-by-side-1","t":"Go is the closest in spirit: structs plus functions, explicit error returns, one binary. Methods on a struct become functions that take it first, and a pointer receiver becomes a mut parameter. Go's (value, error) and (value, ok) returns become a struct, a mut parameter plus a bool , or an enum. There is no nil : a result is always present. Goroutines with a sync.WaitGroup become a parallel: block, which waits for its tasks at the end. Each task writes its own variable, so there is nothing to lock. for i := 0; i < n; i++ becomes for i in 0..n: , and for i, x := range xs becomes for i, x in xs:"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"What carries over","u":"guides/coming-from.html#what-carries-over-1","t":"- Structs, functions, and composition instead of inheritance. No classes, no exceptions. - Explicit error handling by return value, and a small standard library you import by name. - One native binary. http.serve is built in, with a thread per core and an event loop. - Packages are directories of files that share a namespace, and names are private by a naming rule (Go: lowercase; Tern: a leading _ ). - A data race is a bug, and the tools catch it: Go at run time with -race (sometimes); Tern at compile time (always)."},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"What's different","u":"guides/coming-from.html#whats-different-1","t":"- No pointers. &x exists only as an argument to a mut parameter. A struct can't point at another struct; linked data uses indexes into an array. - Slices don't share memory. In Go, b := a 1:3 and b = a share the backing array. In Tern, every slice and assignment is a copy (made lazily, so it's cheap). - No zero values by default. A struct is built with all its fields, and a missing map key is a runtime error unless you use m.get(k, default) or check k in m . - Goroutines are tasks: h = task.spawn(fn() work(x)) , then h.join() ( import task ); channels are ch: chan int = task.channel(8) . errgr"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Common mistakes","u":"guides/coming-from.html#common-mistakes-1","t":"Declaring with := : Starting a goroutine: Returning two values works as in Go, with a tuple type: Adding to a shared total from a parallel loop (a race Go would compile): Two tasks writing one variable: && and nil :"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Coming from Rust","u":"guides/coming-from.html#coming-from-rust","t":""},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Side by side","u":"guides/coming-from.html#side-by-side-2","t":"A struct with an impl block becomes a struct plus functions. &mut self becomes a mut parameter, and &self a plain one. There are no lifetimes because nothing is borrowed: read-only parameters are passed without copying, and the compiler proves it's safe. ( push is also a builtin; a call picks yours when its first argument is a Stack . See builtin names (../reference.md 20-builtin-names).) Option T and Result T, E are built in ( Some , None , Ok , Err ), and ? returns early as in Rust. if let Some(v) = x is written if x is Some(v): . match works as in Rust: it must cover every variant, and it b"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"What carries over","u":"guides/coming-from.html#what-carries-over-2","t":"- The safety guarantees you rely on: no null, no dangling references, no use after move, no data races, no aliased mutation. Tern checks them at compile time too. - Enums with payloads, exhaustive match , and recursive enums (without Box ). - Values freed deterministically by reference counting, with no garbage collector. - take parameters are Rust's move: the caller can't use the value afterwards. - Performance in the same range for most code (see the benchmarks in the README)."},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"What's different","u":"guides/coming-from.html#whats-different-2","t":"- No references or lifetimes. &mut T in a parameter type becomes mut T ; &T is just T (read-only, and passed without a copy). You write & only at the call site. - Mutation is exclusive per call, not per scope. The rule is simply that a &x argument can't overlap any other argument of the same call. - Copies are implicit and lazy. b = a is like a cheap clone() that copies only if one side is later changed. There is no clone() to call. - No traits or generics. Builtins like map , sort_by , and json.encode work on every type, but your own functions take one concrete type each. - ? works as in Rust"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Common mistakes","u":"guides/coming-from.html#common-mistakes-2","t":"let bindings: Generic types in signatures: Writing &mut in the parameter type: Forgetting the & at the call site (Rust would say \"expected &mut \"): Closures written with x : Iterator adapters: Passing the same value twice (the borrow checker's \"cannot borrow as mutable more than once\"):"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Coming from JavaScript","u":"guides/coming-from.html#coming-from-javascript","t":""},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Side by side","u":"guides/coming-from.html#side-by-side-3","t":"Objects and classes become structs, which are declared once with typed fields. Every field must be given when building one. reduce has no builtin: it is a loop. map , filter , and a sort with a key work much as in JavaScript, except that sort_by returns a new array and takes a key function instead of a comparator. null and undefined become an enum (or a sentinel), and try / catch becomes a returned status. JSON.parse becomes json.decode , which fills a typed value and returns false on bad input instead of throwing. Objects are shared by reference in JavaScript; Tern values are not. Here the Ja"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"What carries over","u":"guides/coming-from.html#what-carries-over-3","t":"- map and filter with short lambdas: x = x 2 is fn(x) x 2 . - Functions as values, closures, and callbacks stored in struct fields. - JSON everywhere: json.encode and json.decode work on any struct, array, or map. - A web server in a few lines, with a handler that takes a request and returns a response. - === semantics for == on numbers, strings, and bools: no coercion, ever."},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"What's different","u":"guides/coming-from.html#whats-different-3","t":"- Static types. Every parameter has a type, and a variable keeps its type. Numbers are either int or float , and they don't mix: 1 + 2.0 is an error. - Values, not references. Assigning an object or array copies it, and == on structs or arrays is an error rather than a reference comparison. - No null or undefined . Every variable starts with a value, and every struct field must be given when the struct is built. - No event loop or async . Code runs top to bottom; a parallel block runs tasks on multiple cores and waits for all of them. - Generics use square brackets: fn first T (xs: T ) T inste"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Common mistakes","u":"guides/coming-from.html#common-mistakes-3","t":"const and let : .length : Single-quoted strings: Arrow functions: console.log : The ternary operator: Comparing arrays with == compares their elements (JavaScript's === would compare identities; Tern has none): Leaving out a field, as you might with an object literal:"},{"p":"Coming from Python, Go, Rust, or JavaScript","h":"Next steps","u":"guides/coming-from.html#next-steps","t":"- The tour (../tour/01-getting-started.md) walks through the language in order. - Values, mut, and & (../tour/09-values-and-mut.md) explains the part that is new to everyone. - The cookbook (cookbook.md) has short programs for everyday tasks."},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Building web apps","u":"guides/web-apps.html#building-web-apps","t":"This guide builds HTTP services with the http , json , and sqlite modules, from the smallest server to a JSON API backed by SQLite. It covers the shape of a handler, routing, query parameters and headers, JSON in and out, status codes, shared state (read-only and changing), the database, what happens when a handler fails, the server's limits, deploying the single binary, and testing, both by calling handlers directly and with curl ."},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"The smallest server","u":"guides/web-apps.html#the-smallest-server","t":"http.serve never returns. It runs an HTTP/1.1 server with an event loop on every core, keep-alive, and pipelining, and calls your handler once per request. The handler is an ordinary function: it gets a Request value and returns a Response value. http (../std/http.md) lists every field and function. The http module has helpers that build common responses: http.text(s) , http.html(s) , http.json(s) (all status 200), http.status(code, s) (plain text with any status), http.not_found() , and http.redirect(url) (a 302). You can also build a Response yourself, or change the one a helper returns."},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Handler shapes","u":"guides/web-apps.html#handler-shapes","t":"A handler takes one of three forms, and the form decides how requests share data: Handler Serve with Requests run --- --- --- fn handle(req: http.Request) http.Response http.serve(port, handle) in parallel fn handle(req: http.Request, app: App) http.Response http.serve(port, handle, app) in parallel; app is read-only fn handle(req: http.Request, app: mut App) http.Response http.serve(port, handle, &app) one at a time App is any type you like: a struct of settings, a map, a sqlite.Db . The last two forms are covered in App state ( app-state-read-only-or-mut) below."},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Test handlers without a server","u":"guides/web-apps.html#test-handlers-without-a-server","t":"A handler is a plain function, so you can call it with a request you build yourself with http.new_request(method, target, body) , where target is the path with its query string. This is the quickest way to test routing and responses: no port, no curl , and the output is exact. The rest of this guide uses the same trick: every tested example below calls its handler directly."},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Routing","u":"guides/web-apps.html#routing","t":"There is no router object. A handler is a series of if checks on req.method and req.path , usually handing each route to its own function. For paths with parameters, http.match(path, pattern, &params) compares a path with a pattern such as \"/users/{id}/posts/{post}\" . On a match it returns true and fills params with the (URL-decoded) values; otherwise it returns false and leaves params alone. http.match compares whole segments: /users/{id} doesn't match /users/7/likes , and a trailing slash makes a different path. Parameters are always strings; convert them with to_int and reject bad values wi"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Query parameters and headers","u":"guides/web-apps.html#query-parameters-and-headers","t":"req.query(name) returns the decoded value of one query parameter, or \"\" when it is missing. req.header(name) looks up a request header, ignoring case, and also returns \"\" when it is missing; req.cookie(name) reads one cookie. To add a response header, call res.set_header(name, value) ; res.get_header(name) reads one back. + and %21 in the query are decoded. The raw query string is in req.raw_query if you need to handle repeated parameters yourself, and req.queries() and req.headers() return them all as maps."},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"JSON in and out","u":"guides/web-apps.html#json-in-and-out","t":"json.encode turns any value into JSON text, and json.decode(text, &value) fills a value from JSON. Decoding is all-or-nothing: if the body isn't valid JSON of the right shape, it returns false and changes nothing, which maps directly to a 400 . Keys missing from the body keep the value you started with, so start from sensible defaults, and validate required fields yourself. Unknown keys in the body are ignored, and a JSON null keeps the default. For output, encode a struct or an array of structs; field names become the JSON keys. If you need a key that isn't a valid field name, build the text "},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Status codes","u":"guides/web-apps.html#status-codes","t":"Use http.status(code, message) for any status with a plain-text body, or set res.status on a response built by another helper. The conventions the examples here follow: Status When --- --- 200 success ( http.text , http.html , http.json ) 201 created something; add a Location header 204 success with no body: http.status(204, \"\") 302 http.redirect(url) 400 the request is malformed (bad JSON, a non-numeric id) 404 http.not_found() , or http.status(404, \"no note 7\") 405 the path exists but not with this method 422 well-formed but invalid (a required field is empty) 500 sent for you when a handler"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"App state: read-only or mut","u":"guides/web-apps.html#app-state-read-only-or-mut","t":"Most services need data that outlives one request: configuration, a cache, a database handle. Pass it as the handler's second parameter. Read-only state ( app: App ) is shared by every server thread at once. It can't be changed after the server starts, so requests never wait on each other: Changing state ( app: mut App , served with &app ) runs requests one at a time: the server holds a lock around each call to the handler. That is the whole concurrency story for shared state: no data races are possible, and there is no lock to forget. Why the two shapes? Tern values are never shared by refere"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"SQLite","u":"guides/web-apps.html#sqlite","t":"import sqlite links the system SQLite. sqlite.open(path) opens (or creates) a database file. Then: - d.exec(sql, params) int runs a statement and returns the last inserted row id. - d.query(sql, params) {str: str} returns rows as maps from column name to text. - d.query_into(sql, params, &out) int reads rows into your structs, matching columns to fields by name, and returns the number of rows. out can be an array of structs (it is replaced by the rows) or one struct (filled from the first row, if there is one). Parameters are always a str and are bound with ? placeholders, never pasted into th"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"The database in a server","u":"guides/web-apps.html#the-database-in-a-server","t":"A sqlite.Db is a small handle, so put it in your app state (or pass it as the app itself). A read-only d: sqlite.Db parameter can still run INSERT s: the data lives in the database file, not in a Tern value, and \"read-only\" is about Tern values. So the usual shape is a read-only app, with requests running in parallel and SQLite coordinating the writes. What the runtime does for you: - Each server thread opens its own connection with its own prepared-statement cache, so queries run in parallel. The file is put in WAL mode, which lets readers and a writer work at the same time. A writer waits up"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"A complete API","u":"guides/web-apps.html#a-complete-api","t":"Here is a notes API with everything above: routing with parameters, JSON in and out, status codes, and SQLite. It reads its port and database path from the environment."},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Testing with curl","u":"guides/web-apps.html#testing-with-curl","t":"Build it, start it on a free port, and try each route. This is a real session with the program above ( -i shows the status line and headers): Note that /search returns ids as strings ( \"id\":\"1\" ): d.query returns every value as text. Use query_into when the JSON types matter."},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"When a handler fails","u":"guides/web-apps.html#when-a-handler-fails","t":"A runtime error inside a handler (a missing map key, an index out of range, division by zero, a failed SQL statement, even inside a parallel block in the handler) doesn't stop the server. That request gets a 500 with the body internal server error , the error is logged to stderr, and the server keeps serving. The /broken route above logged: The failed request's open database snapshot is closed, and with mut state, the lock is released. What the handler changed in a mut app before the error stays changed, so validate input before you start changing state. Don't rely on 500s for expected problem"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Timeouts and limits","u":"guides/web-apps.html#timeouts-and-limits","t":"The server enforces a few limits on its own. Change them with environment variables when starting the binary: Limit Default Environment variable --- --- --- Server threads one per core TERN_HTTP_THREADS Idle keep-alive connections closed after 60 seconds TERN_HTTP_IDLE_SEC A request must arrive completely within 10 seconds TERN_HTTP_HEADER_SEC Largest request body (larger gets 413 Payload Too Large ) 16 MB TERN_HTTP_MAX_BODY_MB Largest header block (larger gets 400 Bad Request ) 64 KB Headers per request (the rest are ignored) 64 There is no time limit on the handler itself. Each server thread"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Building and deploying","u":"guides/web-apps.html#building-and-deploying","t":"A Tern web app builds to one native binary: It depends only on the C library and, if you import sqlite , the system SQLite ( libsqlite3 ). Copy it to a server built for the same OS and CPU (build on Linux for Linux) and run it. It binds to the port on all network interfaces, and it logs to stderr. TLS belongs in a reverse proxy in front of the binary. With Caddy, which gets certificates automatically, the whole configuration is: With nginx, proxy to http://127.0.0.1:8090 and set a client_max_body_size that matches TERN_HTTP_MAX_BODY_MB . Since the proxy faces the internet, firewall the app's o"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Errors with Result: web, pg and ?","u":"guides/web-apps.html#errors-with-result-web-pg-and-","t":"The roost framework and the pg , redis , fs , tls and auth packages also return Result values (the try_ functions, and pg.fetch ), so a handler can write the happy path straight down and turn every failure into a response in one place. Errors are strings that say which module failed, what, and why: \"pg: query failed: ERROR 42P01: relation \\\"users\\\" does not exist\" . users: User = db.fetch(...)? needs no type argument: ? passes the type the result is assigned to into the call. Where there's nothing to infer from, write it: db.fetch User (sql, params) . The functions without try_ stay as they we"},{"p":"Building web apps","h":"Where to look next","u":"guides/web-apps.html#where-to-look-next","t":"- examples/web/ : hello.tn , api.tn (read-only shared data), todo.tn (changing state with mut ), and crash.tn (runtime errors becoming 500s). - The http (../std/http.md), json (../std/json.md), and sqlite (../packages/sqlite.md) module pages. - Parallel programming (parallelism.md), for speeding up one slow request."},{"p":"Clients from OpenAPI specs","h":"Clients from OpenAPI specs","u":"guides/openapi-clients.html#clients-from-openapi-specs","t":"Many services publish an OpenAPI 3.x document describing their HTTP API (Stripe, GitHub, Slack, Twilio, OpenAI...). tools/openapi_client.tn turns one into a typed Tern package: a Client , one function per operation, and a struct per schema, over roost 's HTTP client ( tern openapi-client runs tools/openapi_client.tn ). It writes OUTDIR/NAME.tn , a package of one module, and OUTDIR/README.md . Put NAME.tn next to your program (or in deps/NAME/ ) and import NAME . The comment at the top of NAME.tn lists every public function with its signature, one per line: read that first. --tag (repeatable, o"},{"p":"Clients from OpenAPI specs","h":"Using a generated client","u":"guides/openapi-clients.html#using-a-generated-client","t":"- NAME.client(base_url, token) sends the token the way the spec's security scheme says (bearer, an API key header or query parameter, or basic auth with \"user:password\" ). with_bearer , with_api_key , with_query_key , with_basic , with_header and with_timeout return a changed copy. Every client sends a User-Agent (GitHub requires one). - Operations are named from operationId in snake_case ( repos/list-for-user becomes repos_list_for_user ), or from the method and path when there is none. Arguments: path parameters, then required query and header parameters, then optional ones as Option T , the"},{"p":"Clients from OpenAPI specs","h":"How schemas become types","u":"guides/openapi-clients.html#how-schemas-become-types","t":"Schema Tern type --- --- object with properties (also through allOf ) a struct; component schemas keep their names ( full-repository is FullRepository ), inline ones are named after where they appear ( PostCustomersBodyAddress ) string , integer , number , boolean str , int , float , bool array T object with only additionalProperties {str: T} optional or nullable ( nullable: true , type: T, \"null\" , anyOf: T, null ) Option T string enum str , with the values in a comment oneOf / anyOf of different shapes, free-form objects str holding the raw JSON (commented \"raw JSON\") A oneOf / anyOf alterna"},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Parallel programming","u":"guides/parallelism.html#parallel-programming","t":"This guide goes past the tour chapter on parallelism (../tour/11-parallelism.md). It explains how parallel: and parallel for actually run (fork-join on a work-stealing scheduler), exactly what the compiler's race checker allows and refuses and why, the patterns that cover most real programs, how to tell whether parallelism will pay off, and what happens on WebAssembly and when a task hits a runtime error."},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"The model: fork-join","u":"guides/parallelism.html#the-model-fork-join","t":"Tern has two parallel statements, and both are fork-join : the program forks into tasks at the start of the block and joins them at the end. Nothing started inside a block outlives it, so when the next line after the block runs, every task has finished and every result is in place. - In parallel: , every line is a task, and do: groups several lines into one task. A variable created by a one-line task ( threes = ... ) is visible after the block. Variables created inside a do: are local to it ( fifteens is gone after the block), so create a variable before the block, like both , when a do: task "},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"How it runs: work stealing","u":"guides/parallelism.html#how-it-runs-work-stealing","t":"The runtime starts one worker thread per core the first time a parallel block runs (set TERN_THREADS=4 in the environment to use a different number). Each worker has its own queue of tasks: - Starting a block pushes its tasks onto the current worker's queue. The task records live on the stack, so starting a task doesn't allocate. - A worker takes its own newest task first (it is likely still in the cache). A worker with nothing to do steals the oldest task from another worker's queue, which tends to be the biggest piece of remaining work. - The thread waiting at the end of a block doesn't slee"},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"What the race checker allows","u":"guides/parallelism.html#what-the-race-checker-allows","t":"The rule for parallel: is: a variable that one task writes can't be read or written by any other task in the same block. Reading a variable from any number of tasks is fine. The rule for parallel for is: iteration i may write only element i of an array ( out i = ... , out i .field = ... , fill(&out i ) ), and if it writes out i , it may read only out i from that array. Variables created inside the loop body belong to one iteration, so they are free to use. These are allowed:"},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"What it refuses, and why","u":"guides/parallelism.html#what-it-refuses-and-why","t":"Each of these would be a data race: two threads touching the same memory at the same time, with at least one of them writing. In most languages that compiles and fails sometimes, at random, under load. Tern refuses it. Two tasks writing one variable: One task writing what another reads. bump(&count) counts as a write, since & marks an argument the function may change: Every iteration writing one shared variable. This is the most common mistake, and the message says how to fix it: Writing any element other than out i , including pushing onto a shared array: i 2 never collides with another itera"},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Why the checker can be this simple","u":"guides/parallelism.html#why-the-checker-can-be-this-simple","t":"The checker works because of how the rest of Tern is built. There are no references or pointers, so two variables never share memory: checking by name is checking by memory. Parameters are read-only unless declared mut , and a mut argument is marked with & at the call site, so the checker sees every write a task can make by reading the task itself, without following calls into other functions. Closures capture copies, so a closure can't be a hidden way to write a variable either. The checker is conservative: it refuses some programs that would be fine, like evens i 2 above. It never accepts a "},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Pattern: per-task results, combined after","u":"guides/parallelism.html#pattern-per-task-results-combined-after","t":"Give each task its own result variable, then combine them after the block. This is the fix the error messages suggest, and it is usually the fastest way too, since tasks never wait for each other."},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Pattern: parallel for writing out[i]","u":"guides/parallelism.html#pattern-parallel-for-writing-outi","t":"When every element can be computed on its own, allocate the output first and have iteration i fill out i . Then do anything that needs the whole array (a sum, a max, a sort) in an ordinary loop afterwards."},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Pattern: chunking","u":"guides/parallelism.html#pattern-chunking","t":"parallel for already chunks its range, so a loop over elements is fine as it is. Chunk by hand when each task needs its own running state: a partial sum, a map, a buffer. Loop over chunk numbers, and give each chunk one slot of the output. A few times more chunks than cores (16 to 64 on a typical machine) gives the scheduler room to balance uneven chunks without adding much overhead."},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Pattern: parallel map-reduce","u":"guides/parallelism.html#pattern-parallel-map-reduce","t":"Map each chunk to a partial result (here, a word-count map), then reduce the partial results into one. The partial results live in an array with one slot per chunk, so the map step passes the race checker; the reduce step is an ordinary loop."},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Pattern: recursive divide and conquer","u":"guides/parallelism.html#pattern-recursive-divide-and-conquer","t":"A recursive function can split its work with parallel: at each level. Below a cutoff size it runs sequentially, so the program doesn't create millions of tiny tasks."},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Performance: when it pays off","u":"guides/parallelism.html#performance-when-it-pays-off","t":"Parallelism has a fixed cost per block: tasks must be queued, idle workers woken, and everyone joined at the end. On a 14-core laptop that cost is a few microseconds for a small parallel: block and can reach a few hundred microseconds for a parallel for when many sleeping workers have to be woken. Some measurements from that machine: Work Sequential parallel for --- --- --- 2,000,000 Collatz chains (about 100ns each) 220ms 23ms 10,000,000 \u00d7 out i = i 2 6ms 2ms a parallel for over 64 elements, repeated 100,000 times 1ms over 9s The rules of thumb that follow: - Give each block real work. Aim fo"},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"On WebAssembly","u":"guides/parallelism.html#on-webassembly","t":"The wasm target has one thread, so parallel: and parallel for run their tasks one after another, in order, on that thread. The program means the same thing, since the race checker guarantees that tasks can't observe each other; it just doesn't run faster. The race checks still apply, so code that compiles for wasm also compiles, and is race-free, natively. You can see the sequential behavior on a native build with TERN_THREADS=1 ."},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Runtime errors inside tasks","u":"guides/parallelism.html#runtime-errors-inside-tasks","t":"A runtime error inside a task (an index out of range, a missing map key, division by zero) behaves like one anywhere else: - In a program , it prints runtime error in f (prog.tn:N): ... and ends the program with exit status 2. The block never completes, and no code after it runs. - In a web request , the block first waits for all of its tasks to finish, then the error is raised in the request, which answers 500 while the server keeps running. See Building web apps (web-apps.md). There is no way to catch the error inside the task. When a task can fail in an expected way (bad input, say), return"},{"p":"Parallel programming","h":"Long-running tasks","u":"guides/parallelism.html#long-running-tasks","t":"parallel is for work that starts and finishes together. For tasks that outlive a block, channels between tasks, or shared state behind a lock, there is the task module, which is documented separately."},{"p":"How memory works","h":"How memory works","u":"guides/memory.html#how-memory-works","t":"Tern has no garbage collector and no lifetimes, yet every value is freed exactly once and nothing can dangle. This page explains how: value semantics, copy-on-write storage, reference counting inserted by the compiler, read-only parameters that cost nothing to pass, and take for handing a value off. It also shows how to see when copies really happen ( TERN_STATS=1 ), which values live forever, and the limits of the heap."},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Every value is a copy","u":"guides/memory.html#every-value-is-a-copy","t":"Assigning a value, passing it, returning it, or storing it in an array always behaves as if the whole value were copied. There are no references, so changing one variable can never change another: This is the rule the rest of the language is built on. Because values can't point at each other, nothing can dangle (so there are no lifetimes to write) and nothing can form a cycle (so reference counting alone frees everything). The tour covers the rule from the programmer's side in Values, mut, and & (../tour/09-values-and-mut.md)."},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Copies are lazy: copy-on-write","u":"guides/memory.html#copies-are-lazy-copy-on-write","t":"Copying a big array on every assignment would be slow, so Tern doesn't. What a value looks like in memory depends on its type: Type Stored as --- --- int , float , bool , ptr the value itself struct its fields, side by side (a plain C struct) enum a tag plus the fields of its variant; recursive fields are boxed T , str , {K: V} , fn(...) a reference to a heap block with a reference count So b = a on a struct copies its fields, and ys = xs on an array only copies a reference and adds one to the count. The array is really copied the first time one of the sharers writes to it while the count is a"},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Reference counting","u":"guides/memory.html#reference-counting","t":"The compiler inserts the reference-count updates itself, in the style of Perceus (the scheme used by Koka and Lean). You never write them, and there is no runtime tracing: - Making another holder of a block (an assignment, a struct field, an array element, a closure capture) adds one to its count. - When a holder goes away, the count drops by one. At zero, the block is freed right then, and so is everything it holds. - A variable's holdings are released when the block of code that created it ends. - Counts are updated atomically, so values can be shared between parallel tasks and server thread"},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Why there are no cycles and no GC","u":"guides/memory.html#why-there-are-no-cycles-and-no-gc","t":"Reference counting has one classic weakness: two objects that point at each other keep each other alive forever. In Tern that can't happen. A value holds copies, never references, so a value can't contain itself, directly or indirectly. Even a closure only holds copies of what it captured when it was created, so it can't refer back to itself. With no cycles possible, counting alone frees everything, and a garbage collector would have nothing to do. The practical results: memory is freed as soon as the last holder lets go, there are no collection pauses, and peak memory stays close to what the "},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Parameters are borrowed","u":"guides/memory.html#parameters-are-borrowed","t":"A read-only parameter (the default) costs nothing to pass: no copy and no count update. The function reads the caller's block directly. This is safe because of Tern's exclusivity rule: while a call runs, nothing else can change or free the value passed to a read-only parameter. The callee can't change it (that is why parameters are read-only), and the caller can't either, because the caller is waiting for the call to return and can't pass the same variable with & in the same call. Rust gets the same effect with a borrow &T ; Tern infers it for every read-only parameter. A mut parameter is the "},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Moves: take and push","u":"guides/memory.html#moves-take-and-push","t":"Sometimes a function wants to own a value: to change it and return it, or to store it. With a read-only parameter, the function has to copy it first: A take parameter moves the value in instead. The caller gives up its variable, so the function holds the only reference and can change it in place: (A mut parameter, fn add_footer(lines: mut str ) called as add_footer(&lines) , also avoids the copy. Use take when the function builds something new from the value, or stores it somewhere.) After a move, the variable is gone, and using it is a compile error. Pass copy(x) to keep your own copy; copy i"},{"p":"How memory works","h":"When copies actually happen","u":"guides/memory.html#when-copies-actually-happen","t":"A copy happens only when a block is written while another holder shares it. These were measured with TERN_STATS=1 : Code Real copies --- --- ys = xs then only reads 0 ys = xs then ys 0 = 1 1, of the whole array m2 = m then m2 \"k\" = 1 1, of the whole map p = team 0 then p.name = \"zed\" 0 (the struct's fields were copied; the string is replaced, not written) team i .scores.push(0) 0 (writes the element in place) p = team i , p.scores.push(0) , team i = p 1 per element ( p.scores is shared with team i at the push) a read-only parameter, read 0 a read-only parameter copied to a local, then written "},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Immortal values","u":"guides/memory.html#immortal-values","t":"Some blocks are never freed. They are marked \"immortal\" in their count word, so holding them costs no count updates: - String literals, which are part of the program's data, not the heap (with a terminating NUL, so C can read them in place); on WebAssembly they are made when the program starts. - All 256 one-byte strings, so s i never allocates. - The empty array, string, and map, which are one shared block (so memory filled with zeros reads as or \"\" ). - Named functions used as values. - Any block with more than about 16 million holders at once: instead of overflowing, its count saturates and"},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Where memory lives, and its limits","u":"guides/memory.html#where-memory-lives-and-its-limits","t":"- One heap region, then arenas. Heap blocks start out in one reserved 32GB address range. Inside structs and arrays, a reference to another block is a 64-bit offset from the region's start, so once the region is full, blocks go on in more address space the runtime maps as needed: the heap can grow as far as the machine allows, as in Go and Rust. The native runtime reserves the region at a fixed address. The portable runtime (libraries, iOS, Android, --portable ) reserves it wherever the OS allows, halving the size until the reservation succeeds, down to 256MB. On WebAssembly, where memory is a"},{"p":"How memory works","h":"Reading TERN_STATS","u":"guides/memory.html#reading-tern_stats","t":"Set TERN_STATS=1 when the program is compiled ( run compiles and runs in one step). The counters are compiled in only then, so normal builds pay nothing for them. - allocations : heap blocks created. (On WebAssembly every program starts with 256, the one-byte strings, plus one per string literal it uses.) - freed : blocks freed. - leaked : allocations minus frees minus immortal blocks. It should always be 0; the test suite requires it. - copy-on-write copies : shared blocks copied because one holder wrote to them. This is the number to watch when tuning. The line goes to stderr, so it doesn't "},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Performance tips","u":"guides/performance.html#performance-tips","t":"Most Tern code is fast without any tuning: it compiles to C, clang optimizes it with -O3 , and there is no garbage collector. When a program is slower than it should be, the cause is usually one of a few things: a hidden copy of a big array, a slow sort key, a string built from the wrong end, or parallel tasks that are too small. This page shows how to find each one with TERN_STATS and emit , how to fix it, and how Tern compares with Go and Rust on the repository's benchmarks, including where Rust is faster. Timings on this page were measured on an Apple M4 Pro (14 cores) with programs built b"},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Measure first","u":"guides/performance.html#measure-first","t":"TERN_STATS must be set when the program is compiled; its counters aren't in normal builds. The number to watch is copy-on-write copies : each one is a whole array, string, or map copied because it was written while shared. A count that grows with your input, like one copy per loop pass, is almost always a bug worth fixing. Time a built binary, not tern run , which includes compile time."},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Avoid hidden copies","u":"guides/performance.html#avoid-hidden-copies","t":"Assignment never copies right away, but writing to a value that is shared does. The classic case is ys = xs followed by ys 0 = 1 : that copies all of xs . It most often hides in a function that takes a read-only parameter, changes a local copy, and returns it: One copy of the whole array per call. With a mut parameter, the function changes the caller's array in place: Scaled up to a 100,000-element array and 10,000 calls, the first version takes 0.46s (10,000 copies of 800KB) and the second under 10ms. The same thing happens with array elements. p = team i copies the struct, and its array fiel"},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Hand values off with take","u":"guides/performance.html#hand-values-off-with-take","t":"When a function consumes a value and returns a changed version of it (a builder, a pipeline stage), declare the parameter take . The caller's variable moves into the function, so there's no copy: On the 100,000-element example above, fn record(counts: take int , bucket: int) int called as counts = record(counts, i % 7) also makes zero copies. xs.push(v) moves v when it isn't used afterwards, so pushing a freshly built string or array into a list never copies it. Use mut when the function changes a value in place, and take when it keeps or returns it."},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Use flat arrays of numbers","u":"guides/performance.html#use-flat-arrays-of-numbers","t":"An int , float , or bool is one block of plain values: no reference counts per element. An array of arrays is one block per row, and each row is a separate value. Watch out for 0 n n : it makes n references to one row, so the first write to each row copies it: The results are correct, since each row really is its own value. But for a grid you fill yourself, one flat array indexed by r n + c makes no copies and one allocation: At 3000 \u00d7 3000, filling the flat grid took 5ms and the nested one 10ms. More about arrays: - bool is bit-packed : 8 flags per byte. The 200-million-entry sieve benchmark "},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Build strings from the front","u":"guides/performance.html#build-strings-from-the-front","t":"s += piece (or s = s + piece ) appends in place when s isn't shared: the string grows into spare capacity, so a loop of appends is linear. Adding to the front copies the whole string every time: With 100,000 pieces, s += f\"{i},\" took 2ms, collecting the pieces in an array and calling \"\".join(parts) also took 2ms, and s = f\"{i},\" + s took 1.8s. To build text in reverse order, push the pieces and join them in a reversed loop."},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Sort by numbers when you can","u":"guides/performance.html#sort-by-numbers-when-you-can","t":"xs.sort_by(key) computes each key once and sorts stably. Number keys ( int or float ) use a radix sort. String keys are radix-sorted on their first 8 bytes, and only keys that share those are compared as whole strings. On 1,000,000 structs, sorting by an int field took about 22ms, and sorting by a str field holding the same numbers took about 37ms. If records have both a numeric id and a string form of it, sort by the number. Pass the lambda directly to sort_by , map , and filter : a closure the compiler can see is called directly, so it inlines like a hand-written loop."},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Give parallel tasks enough work","u":"guides/performance.html#give-parallel-tasks-enough-work","t":"parallel for splits its range into contiguous chunks, up to 8 per core (at most 256), so even tiny loop bodies parallelize well: the 2-million-task Collatz benchmark runs as fast as Rust's rayon. Each iteration should write only its own out i . parallel: blocks are different: each line is a task, and starting and joining a block costs much more than a function call. In recursive code, stop splitting once the work gets small and fall back to the sequential version: For fib(40) , the sequential version took 0.17s and this version 0.02s on 14 cores. Without the cutoff (splitting all the way down "},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Read the generated C","u":"guides/performance.html#read-the-generated-c","t":"tern emit prog.tn prints the C that clang compiles. Search it for _unique( : that's a copy-on-write check, and where it sits tells you what the loop costs. For this function: The compiler emits two versions: The first checks once that xs isn't shared, then writes freely. The second ( _u ) is called when the caller's array is already known to be unshared with a fixed length: no check at all, and the length arrives as a parameter, so after inlining LLVM can see it. A _unique( call inside a loop means the compiler couldn't prove the array stays unshared there, usually because it is also copied, r"},{"p":"Performance tips","h":"Benchmarks against Go and Rust","u":"guides/performance.html#benchmarks-against-go-and-rust","t":"These are the repository's own benchmarks, on an Apple M4 Pro (14 cores), 2026-09-28, with runs interleaved across languages and every implementation printing the same answer. Run them with tern run bench/run.tn , bench/async/run.tn , bench/web2/run.tn , and bench/platforms.tn . The full tables, with the method and the load during each run, are in bench/results.md , bench/async/results.md , bench/web2/results.md , bench/platforms_results.md , and bench/linux_results.md (Linux, in a VM). Compute (median of 7; bench/results.md ): Benchmark Tern Go Rust --- ---: ---: ---: 2M tiny tasks ( parallel"},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","u":"guides/c-interop.html#calling-c-and-tern-as-a-library","t":"Tern compiles to C, so it can call C functions directly and C can call Tern. This guide covers both directions: declaring C functions with extern (headers, bundled .c files, libraries, renaming), how each Tern type crosses the boundary, arrays, out-parameters, callbacks, and opaque ptr handles; then export fn , building a static or shared library with a generated header, handling runtime errors from C, and calling in from several threads."},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Your first C call","u":"guides/c-interop.html#your-first-c-call","t":"An extern block names a C header and lists the functions you want, with Tern types: The header is include d into the generated C, and each call goes through a small wrapper that converts Tern values to their C form. There is no runtime marshalling layer: a call to hypot compiles to a call to hypot . The signatures are checked against the header by the C compiler, which also converts between compatible types. That is why int (64-bit in Tern) works for a C function that takes or returns a C int or size_t : strlen(\"h\u00e9llo\") is 6 because Tern strings are UTF-8 bytes. getenv returns NULL for a missi"},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"The extern block","u":"guides/c-interop.html#the-extern-block","t":"Every part after extern is optional: Part What it does --- --- \"header.h\" include \"header.h\" ; the header's prototypes are used. Headers next to your .tn file are found too. source \"impl.c\" compile this C file along with the program (path relative to the .tn file; repeatable) link \"z\" link with -lz . A value starting with - is passed as linker flags as-is (repeatable) = \"c_name\" call the C function c_name under a different Tern name On the command line, -l lib , -L dir , and -I dir do the same for libraries and headers you don't want to name in the source. Linking a system library (zlib ships "},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Renaming with = \"c_name\"","u":"guides/c-interop.html#renaming-with--c_name","t":"Use a rename when the C name clashes with a Tern builtin or reads badly in Tern code. An extern function can't take a builtin's name ( abs , min , max , find , copy , ...), so fn abs(n: int) int in an extern block is an error; give it another name instead:"},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Without a header","u":"guides/c-interop.html#without-a-header","t":"extern: with no header makes the compiler declare each function itself, using the exact C types from the table below. That's convenient for a function in a C file you compile with source , but the C function must really have those types. llabs takes and returns long long , which is 64-bit, so this is correct: Declaring C's abs (which takes a 32-bit int ) this way would be wrong, and nothing would catch it. When a header exists, name it."},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"How types cross the boundary","u":"guides/c-interop.html#how-types-cross-the-boundary","t":"Tern parameter C type Notes --- --- --- int int64_t with a header, C converts to narrower types float double bool bool ptr void an opaque C pointer (below) str const char valid only during the call; see \"Strings\" int / float / byte const int64_t / const double / const uint8_t a pointer to the elements; pass the length separately mut int / mut float / mut bool / mut ptr int64_t / double / bool / void an out-parameter; the caller writes &x mut int / mut float / mut byte int64_t / double / uint8_t C may write the elements fn(int) int and similar int64_t ( )(void , int64_t) plus void a callback: t"},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Strings","u":"guides/c-interop.html#strings","t":"A str argument arrives in C as a NUL-terminated const char . When the string has spare capacity for the terminating NUL (string literals always do, and most built strings do), Tern writes the NUL there and passes its own bytes: no copy. Otherwise it passes a temporary copy. Either way the pointer is valid only until the C function returns, and C must not write through it. A string containing a NUL byte looks shorter to C. A str result is copied into a new Tern string, and NULL becomes \"\" . With a plain str result Tern never frees the pointer C returned, which suits static strings ( strerror , "},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Out-parameters with mut","u":"guides/c-interop.html#out-parameters-with-mut","t":"A mut scalar parameter becomes a pointer to the caller's variable. modf splits a float into its whole and fractional parts and returns the whole part through a double : The same rules as any mut call apply: &x marks the argument, and one call can't pass the same variable twice."},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Arrays","u":"guides/c-interop.html#arrays","t":"int , float , and byte pass as a pointer to their first element. C doesn't get the length, so pass len(xs) as its own argument. Read-only arrays are passed as const , with no copy. With mut , C may write the elements. Arrays are copy-on-write values in Tern, so if the array's storage is shared with another variable, Tern makes it unique first: C's writes change only the array you passed. C can't change the length. Here is a C file compiled along with the program, and the Tern side. (This page's tests only type-check this program, since they don't have the .c file; the same code runs in tests/r"},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"C structs","u":"guides/c-interop.html#c-structs","t":"Declare a C struct inside the extern block, with the fields you need, as Tern types. = \"...\" names the C type when it differs ( \"SDL_Rect\" , \"struct tm\" ). The struct is an ordinary Tern struct on the Tern side, and crosses to C field by field, so C's own field types are used: an int field holds a Tern int , a float or unsigned char field a Tern float or int , and fields you don't declare are left zero. - By value ( r: Rect ) passes the C struct itself; a struct result comes back as a Tern value. - mut ( r: mut Rect ) passes a pointer, which is what most C APIs take, const or not. C's changes "},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Callbacks: passing Tern functions to C","u":"guides/c-interop.html#callbacks-passing-tern-functions-to-c","t":"A parameter with a function type, like f: fn(int) int , becomes two C arguments: a function pointer whose first parameter is a void , and that void (the \"user data\"). C calls the function pointer with the user data first: Any Tern function value works: a lambda, a closure that captured variables, a nested fn , or a top-level function. The user data is the closure itself, so C libraries that follow the common \"function pointer plus void context\" convention can call back into Tern directly. Rules for callbacks: - Their parameters and result must be int , float , bool , or ptr (or no result). fn("},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Opaque handles: ptr","u":"guides/c-interop.html#opaque-handles-ptr","t":"ptr is a C pointer that Tern only stores and passes along: a FILE , a database connection, an object from a C library. null is the null pointer, p.is_null() tests for it, and == compares two pointers. With a header, C converts between void and the real pointer type for you: Pass null where C accepts a null pointer: A ptr is a plain value: copying it copies the address, and a struct may hold one. Tern never frees what it points to, so call the library's close or free function yourself ( fclose above). null has type ptr only; other types have no null."},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Tern as a library: export fn","u":"guides/c-interop.html#tern-as-a-library-export-fn","t":"The other direction: mark functions export fn , and C (or anything that can call C: Swift, Kotlin through JNI, Python's ctypes ) can call them. A library doesn't need fn main() ; if it has one (handy for trying the functions with tern run ), library builds leave it out. Exported functions take int , float , bool , str , or ptr parameters (read-only; no mut or take ) and return one of those or nothing. Only the main file can export. Everything else in the program (structs, arrays, parallel , other modules) works as usual behind the exported functions. Build it: The header is named after the lib"},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Strings across an export","u":"guides/c-interop.html#strings-across-an-export","t":"- A const char argument is copied into a Tern string; NULL is treated as \"\" . The caller keeps ownership of its string. - A str result is a new malloc 'd, NUL-terminated char that the caller owns. Free it with tern_free() (which calls free , but using it keeps working if that ever changes)."},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Runtime errors","u":"guides/c-interop.html#runtime-errors","t":"A runtime error that would end a normal program (an index out of bounds, a missing map key, division by zero, pop() on an empty array) doesn't end the host process. The call returns 0 , 0.0 , false , or NULL , and tern_last_error() returns the message, just like the message a program would print. The library keeps working for later calls. - The compiler works out which exported functions can fail, and marks them in the header with can fail: check tern_last_error() . Functions that can't fail ( word_count and shout above) skip the error-catching setup entirely, so they cost no more than a plain"},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Threads and startup","u":"guides/c-interop.html#threads-and-startup","t":"There is no init function to call. The Tern runtime starts on the first call into the library, on whichever thread makes it, and several host threads may call exported functions at the same time. parallel blocks inside an exported function run on Tern's own worker threads, as in a normal program. examples/embed/ shows a C program calling a Tern library from two threads: Libraries use the portable runtime, which finds each thread's heap through thread-local storage instead of from the stack. That's what lets Tern code run on threads it didn't create. It costs about 20% on allocation-heavy code "},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Other hosts","u":"guides/c-interop.html#other-hosts","t":"The library is plain C, so anything with a C FFI can load it. tests/embed/py_host.sh loads a --shared build from Python with ctypes : Declare string results as c_void_p , not c_char_p , so you keep the pointer to free. For Swift and Kotlin, see iOS and Android (mobile.md); for JavaScript, see WebAssembly and JavaScript (wasm.md)."},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Performance","u":"guides/c-interop.html#performance","t":"Crossing the boundary is cheap in both directions. From bench/platforms_results.md (Apple M4 Pro, median of 5 runs, against the same programs in Rust): benchmark Tern Rust --- ---: ---: calling C: 200M calls + 5M string arguments 200ms 245ms called from C: 200M calls + 2M string calls 392ms 428ms Strings go to C without a copy when they have room for the NUL, and exported functions that can't fail skip error handling. Both are small effects; the point is that a C call is a C call."},{"p":"Calling C, and Tern as a library","h":"Limits","u":"guides/c-interop.html#limits","t":"- Only int , float , bool , str , ptr , int , float , byte , extern structs of those, and callbacks of scalar values cross the boundary. C unions, fixed-size arrays inside C structs, and arrays of structs don't: use a ptr to C-side data with accessor functions. - Exported functions take and return only int , float , bool , str , and ptr . - A callback must be called during the call it was passed to, and, without --portable , on the same thread. - extern: without a header trusts your signatures; a mismatch with the real C function is undefined behavior that nothing reports. - C code runs outsid"},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","u":"guides/wasm.html#webassembly-and-javascript","t":"Tern compiles to WebAssembly (WASI) with one flag, --target wasm . This guide covers installing the toolchain, building and running whole programs under wasmtime or Node, building a library that JavaScript can call (with a generated loader and TypeScript types), how values cross between Tern and JavaScript, how runtime errors surface, using a module in the browser, and what doesn't work on wasm."},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"Installing the toolchain","u":"guides/wasm.html#installing-the-toolchain","t":"Tern compiles to C, so wasm needs a C compiler that targets wasm32-wasip1 . Either install Homebrew's LLVM with the WASI libraries: or install a wasi-sdk (https://github.com/WebAssembly/wasi-sdk) and point WASI_SDK_PATH at it ( tern also looks in /opt/wasi-sdk ). If neither is found, tern stops with a message saying which packages to install. To run programs you need wasmtime (https://wasmtime.dev) or Node.js 18 or newer. JavaScript libraries need only Node or a browser."},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"Building and running a program","u":"guides/wasm.html#building-and-running-a-program","t":"Any program that doesn't use sqlite or http builds for wasm unchanged: tern run uses wasmtime if it's installed and Node's node:wasi otherwise. Either way it gives the program the current directory, /tmp , and the environment. When you run wasmtime yourself, the module sees only what you grant: without --dir , fs.read returns \"\" and fs.write returns false , as they do for any unreadable path, and without -S inherit-env=y , os.env returns its default. A runtime error in a wasm program prints runtime error in f (prog.tn:N): ... to stderr and exits with status 2, the same as natively."},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"Building a library for JavaScript","u":"guides/wasm.html#building-a-library-for-javascript","t":"Mark the functions JavaScript should see with export fn . A library file can still have a main ; --lib ignores it, and it lets you test the functions natively with tern run : This writes three files next to each other: - textstats.wasm : the module (about 50 KB here). - textstats.mjs : an ES module that loads it and wraps each exported function. It works in browsers, Node, and other JavaScript runtimes, and needs no npm packages. - textstats.d.ts : TypeScript types for the loader. Call it from Node: With no argument, load() loads the .wasm file with the same name next to the .mjs . You can als"},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"How values cross","u":"guides/wasm.html#how-values-cross","t":"Tern JavaScript argument JavaScript result --- --- --- int bigint or number bigint float number number bool any value (truthy or falsy) boolean str string (sent as UTF-8) string ptr number number no return type undefined - Ints are 64-bit, so they come back as bigint . Convert with Number(x) when you know the value fits in a double. An int argument accepts a number , but it must be a whole number: BigInt(1.5) throws a RangeError in JavaScript before Tern is called. - Strings are copied into the module's memory for the call and freed afterwards. Returned strings are copied out and freed by the "},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"Runtime errors become TernError","u":"guides/wasm.html#runtime-errors-become-ternerror","t":"A runtime error inside an exported function (an index out of bounds, a missing map key, division by zero) doesn't end anything. The call unwinds, and the loader throws a TernError whose message is the same text a program would print. The module keeps working, so later calls behave normally, as the nth_word example above shows. This costs nothing for functions that can't fail. The compiler works out which exported functions might hit a runtime error, and the loader checks for an error only after those."},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"Printing from a library","u":"guides/wasm.html#printing-from-a-library","t":"print inside a library goes to process.stdout under Node and to console.log in a browser (a line at a time; stderr goes to console.error ). To send it somewhere else, pass a write function: A library has no files, arguments, or environment: the loader provides only printing, clocks ( time ), and random numbers ( random , from crypto.getRandomValues ). fs.read returns \"\" and os.args() is empty."},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"In the browser","u":"guides/wasm.html#in-the-browser","t":"examples/wasm/ has a complete page. Build the library, serve the directory over HTTP (the loader uses fetch , which doesn't work on file:// URLs), and open the page: The page imports the loader as a module and calls Tern on every keystroke: Counting the primes below 1,000,000 takes about 77 ms in the page (Chromium, Apple M4 Pro), and lib.wasm is about 51 KB."},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"Threads: --target wasm-threads","u":"guides/wasm.html#threads---target-wasm-threads","t":"--target wasm has one thread. --target wasm-threads builds for WebAssembly's shared memory, and then parallel: blocks and parallel for really run in parallel, on a pool of Web Workers ( worker_threads under Node) that the generated loader starts. The same source works for both targets; only the build changes: - load(source, { threads: 8 }) picks the number of threads (default: the number of cores); lib.close() stops the workers. Programs read TERN_THREADS . - In a browser , shared memory needs a cross-origin-isolated page (served with Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin and Cross-Origin-Em"},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"Limits","u":"guides/wasm.html#limits","t":"- One thread on wasm . With --target wasm , the tasks of a parallel: block and the iterations of a parallel for run one after another (use wasm-threads for real threads). The race checks still apply, so the same code is correct everywhere. - No database or web server. import sqlite and http.serve are compile errors on wasm: - Runtime errors in programs end the program (exit status 2). Only libraries recover and report them as TernError . - Memory grows in chunks of at least 1 MB as the program allocates, and is never given back to the host; freed memory is reused by later allocations. Tern cod"},{"p":"WebAssembly and JavaScript","h":"Performance","u":"guides/wasm.html#performance","t":"bench/platforms.tn runs the compute benchmarks under wasmtime against Rust built for wasm32-wasip1 (full table in bench/platforms_results.md ; median of 5 runs): benchmark Tern Rust --- ---: ---: --- binary-trees 3.65 s 5.98 s Tern 1.64x faster sieve 731 ms, 33 MB 747 ms, 199 MB tie, 6x less memory fib 1.76 s 1.76 s tie tasks 354 ms 357 ms tie n-body 1.18 s 1.42 s Tern 1.2x faster (wasm SIMD) .wasm size 32\u201344 KB 49\u2013113 KB Tern builds use wasm SIMD, which browsers, Node, and wasmtime all support; set TERN_WASM_SIMD=0 when compiling for a host that rejects it. Programs shaped like what wasm is u"},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"iOS and Android","u":"guides/mobile.html#ios-and-android","t":"Tern compiles for iPhones and iPads (arm64 devices and the iOS Simulator) and for Android (arm64 and x86-64). This guide shows what you need installed, how to build command-line programs and libraries for each platform, how Swift and Kotlin/Java call a Tern library, and how the runtime behaves inside an app: when it starts, which threads may call it, and what happens on a runtime error. The examples come from examples/mobile/ in the repository, and tern run tests/runner/main.tn -- run --mobile runs them."},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"The targets","u":"guides/mobile.html#the-targets","t":"Pick a platform with --target : --target Builds for C toolchain --- --- --- ios iPhone / iPad, arm64, iOS 15.0 or later Xcode ( xcrun --sdk iphoneos clang ) ios-sim the iOS Simulator on an Apple-silicon Mac Xcode ( xcrun --sdk iphonesimulator clang ) android (or android-arm64 ) Android arm64, API level 24 or later the Android NDK android-x86_64 x86-64 Android (emulators on Intel machines, Chromebooks) the Android NDK What each one needs: - iOS : Xcode, with an iOS Simulator runtime installed if you use ios-sim (Xcode Settings Components). tern calls xcrun , so the command-line tools must point"},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"Command-line programs","u":"guides/mobile.html#command-line-programs","t":"build and run take --target like any other flag: What run does on each target: - ios-sim : if no Simulator is booted, it boots one (the last iPhone in xcrun simctl list , or the device whose UDID is in $TERN_IOS_DEVICE ), then runs the program with xcrun simctl spawn booted . TERN_ environment variables such as TERN_STATS=1 are passed through. - android : it pushes the binary to /data/local/tmp with adb , runs it there (again passing TERN_ variables), prints its output, and deletes it. If no device or emulator is connected, it says so; start one first, for example emulator -avd Pixel_9a -no-wi"},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"Libraries for apps","u":"guides/mobile.html#libraries-for-apps","t":"A library is a .tn file with export fn functions. Only int , float , bool , and str cross in and out; the rest of the program uses any types it likes. See Calling C, and Tern as a library (c-interop.md) for the full rules; this section is the mobile side of it. A library may also have a fn main() . build --lib leaves it out of the library (it isn't exported), so you can test the library's functions from the desktop with tern run : Build it for each platform. The output directory must already exist. Each build also writes a C header named after the library, here build/scores.h : The shared libr"},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"Swift on iOS","u":"guides/mobile.html#swift-on-ios","t":"examples/mobile/ios/ builds examples/embed/lib.tn for the Simulator and calls it from Swift. In an Xcode app, add the .a and the .h to the target and include the header in the bridging header. From the command line, run.sh does the equivalent: The Swift side calls the exported functions directly. Numbers arrive as Int64 , Swift String s convert to const char automatically, and a returned string is a C string you copy and then free with tern_free : The full main.swift prints: For a device, build with --target ios instead. The library is arm64 only; it isn't a universal binary or an .xcframework"},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"Kotlin and Java on Android","u":"guides/mobile.html#kotlin-and-java-on-android","t":"The JVM calls native code through JNI, which looks up functions by special names ( Java_<package _<class _<method ). So an Android library has two parts: the Tern code, and a small C file of JNI glue that converts Java strings and forwards each call. examples/mobile/android/tern_jni.c is that glue for examples/embed/lib.tn : On the Kotlin side, the class loads the library and declares the functions: Build the Tern static library, then link it with the glue into one .so ( --whole-archive keeps every Tern function in it). This is what run.sh does: Copy build/libstats.so to app/src/main/jniLibs/a"},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"Threads and runtime startup","u":"guides/mobile.html#threads-and-runtime-startup","t":"Apps have their own threads (the main thread, GCD queues, Java threads), so the runtime works differently from a Tern program that owns main : - No setup call. The runtime starts on the first call into any exported function, on whatever thread makes it. If several threads make their first call at once, it still starts exactly once. - Any thread may call, and several may call at once. Each host thread gets its own Tern heap on its first allocation. When the thread ends, its free memory goes back to a shared pool for other threads to reuse. - parallel runs on Tern's own worker threads , one per "},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"Runtime errors in a library","u":"guides/mobile.html#runtime-errors-in-a-library","t":"A runtime error inside an exported call (an index out of bounds, a missing map key, division by zero) doesn't end the app. The call returns 0 , NULL , or false , and tern_last_error() returns the message on that thread. The header marks which functions can fail; the others skip the error handling entirely. - The message is per thread: read it on the thread that made the call. - It is cleared at the start of each call to a function marked \"can fail\", so check it right after that call. Calls to functions that can't fail leave it unchanged. - The Swift example reads it with tern_last_error().map "},{"p":"iOS and Android","h":"Limits","u":"guides/mobile.html#limits","t":"- Architectures. iOS builds are arm64 only, for devices and for the Simulator on Apple-silicon Macs. Android builds are arm64 or x86-64; there is no 32-bit ARM target. - Minimum versions. iOS 15.0 and Android API level 24, fixed by the compiler's target triples. - SQLite. On iOS, sqlite links against the SDK's SQLite. The NDK has no SQLite, so import sqlite is a compile error on Android: import sqlite is not available on android . - The web server. http.serve runs on Android (the epoll server path). The repository has no iOS example or test that uses it. - Heap size. Phones limit address space"},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Cookbook","u":"guides/cookbook.html#cookbook","t":"Short, complete programs for everyday tasks: files, text, collections, sorting, maps, formatting, JSON, and a few classic data structures. Each recipe is a whole program you can copy into a file and run with tern run file.tn ; the output shown under it is what it prints. The recipes stick to the standard library that ships with Tern."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Read a file line by line","u":"guides/cookbook.html#read-a-file-line-by-line","t":"fs.read returns the whole file as one string (or \"\" if it can't be read), and split(\"\\n\") turns it into lines. A file that ends with a newline gives an empty last piece, so skip empty lines or trim() the text first."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Write and append to a file","u":"guides/cookbook.html#write-and-append-to-a-file","t":"fs.write replaces the file; fs.append adds to the end. Both return false when the file can't be written, so check the result."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Parse CSV","u":"guides/cookbook.html#parse-csv","t":"Split the text into lines, then each line into columns. Store rows in a struct so the rest of the program works with named, typed fields. (This handles simple CSV without quoted commas.)"},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Command-line arguments","u":"guides/cookbook.html#command-line-arguments","t":"os.args() returns the program name followed by its arguments. Pass arguments after -- when using tern run : tern run greet.tn -- ada 3 . Run without arguments (as above) it prints the usage line. Use os.exit(1) for errors so scripts and shells can tell that the program failed."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Count words","u":"guides/cookbook.html#count-words","t":"A map from word to count, filled with get(key, 0) + 1 . Maps keep insertion order, so sort the keys by count to find the most common words. sort_by is stable, so words with the same count stay in the order they first appeared."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Remove duplicates from an array","u":"guides/cookbook.html#remove-duplicates-from-an-array","t":"Keep a map of what you have seen. There is no set type; a {str: bool} map works as one. This keeps the first occurrence of each value and the original order."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Reverse an array or a string","u":"guides/cookbook.html#reverse-an-array-or-a-string","t":"xs.reversed() returns a reversed copy of an array. For a string, build a new one; to walk an array backwards without copying it, use a while loop, since for ranges only count up. reversed_str works byte by byte, which is right for ASCII text. Reversing UTF-8 text character by character needs more care."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Sort structs by several keys","u":"guides/cookbook.html#sort-structs-by-several-keys","t":"sort_by sorts by one key and is stable. To sort by several keys, sort by the least important key first and the most important last. Negate a number to sort it descending."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Group items into a map of arrays","u":"guides/cookbook.html#group-items-into-a-map-of-arrays","t":"Create the array the first time a key appears, then push onto groups key . The push changes the array inside the map in place."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Count with a map","u":"guides/cookbook.html#count-with-a-map","t":"Counting by a computed key works the same way as counting words. Here, requests are counted by status class and the counts summed afterwards."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Build a string efficiently","u":"guides/cookbook.html#build-a-string-efficiently","t":"Appending with += is fast: a string that only this variable holds grows in place, like a string builder in other languages. For a list of pieces with separators, join is simplest."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Parse integers safely","u":"guides/cookbook.html#parse-integers-safely","t":"s.to_int(default) returns default when s isn't a whole number. When every int is a valid answer, parse twice with two different defaults: only a bad string gets both. to_int doesn't skip spaces, so trim() input from users and files first."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Format money, decimals, and padding","u":"guides/cookbook.html#format-money-decimals-and-padding","t":"F-strings have no format specs. The fmt module has the common ones. Keep money in whole cents (an int ) so sums are exact, and format with fmt.cents at the end."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Read and write JSON config","u":"guides/cookbook.html#read-and-write-json-config","t":"json.decode fills an existing value. Keys missing from the JSON keep the value you started with, so start from the defaults. On bad JSON it returns false and changes nothing. In a real program the text comes from fs.read(\"config.json\") , and fs.write saves json.encode(cfg) back."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Binary search","u":"guides/cookbook.html#binary-search","t":"On a sorted array, halve the range until the value is found. Return -1 when it isn't there."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Stack and queue","u":"guides/cookbook.html#stack-and-queue","t":"An array is a stack: push and pop work at the end. For a queue, keep a read index instead of removing from the front, which would copy the rest of the array each time."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Breadth-first search on a graph","u":"guides/cookbook.html#breadth-first-search-on-a-graph","t":"Tern has no pointers, so nodes refer to each other by index into an array. BFS finds the fewest hops from one node to every other."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"A tree as an enum","u":"guides/cookbook.html#a-tree-as-an-enum","t":"Enums can contain themselves, so a binary search tree is an enum with an empty case and a node case. Inserting returns a new tree; there are no pointers to update."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"A state machine with an enum and match","u":"guides/cookbook.html#a-state-machine-with-an-enum-and-match","t":"Model each state as an enum variant, and write one function that takes the current state and an event and returns the next state."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Shuffle an array","u":"guides/cookbook.html#shuffle-an-array","t":"The Fisher-Yates shuffle swaps each position with a random earlier one. random.int(lo, hi) includes both ends. The output differs on every run, so this example prints only facts that don't change. random is fast but not for cryptography: don't use it for passwords or tokens."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Time a piece of code","u":"guides/cookbook.html#time-a-piece-of-code","t":"time.now_ms() is wall-clock milliseconds. Read it before and after, and subtract. Run the work more than once, since the first run can include warm-up costs."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Retry with backoff","u":"guides/cookbook.html#retry-with-backoff","t":"Loop a fixed number of times, wait a little longer after each failure, and stop as soon as the operation works. Here the \"operation\" fails twice before succeeding."},{"p":"Cookbook","h":"Simple text templates","u":"guides/cookbook.html#simple-text-templates","t":"Replace {{name}} placeholders with values from a map. Braces are ordinary characters in a normal string; only f-strings treat them specially. Placeholders with no value are left as they are, which makes missing values easy to spot."},{"p":"Standard library","h":"Standard library","u":"std/index.html#standard-library","t":"Tern ships a standard library for the programs people write most: formatting, math, time, files and processes, JSON, logging, cryptography, SQLite, and web services with TLS, PostgreSQL and Redis. This page explains how to import modules, lists what each one is for, shows which functions need no import at all, and says which modules work on which platforms."},{"p":"Standard library","h":"Importing a module","u":"std/index.html#importing-a-module","t":"import name makes a module's functions and types available as name.thing : from name import a, b brings chosen names in so you can use them without the prefix. You can mix both forms: Imports go at the top level of a file, usually first. A module that is never imported costs nothing: SQLite, for example, is linked only into programs that import sqlite . The standard library is ordinary Tern modules in the std/ directory of the compiler, so import models looks in std/ before it looks next to your program. Don't name your own modules after the standard ones. See Modules and packages (../tour/12-"},{"p":"Standard library","h":"The modules","u":"std/index.html#the-modules","t":"Module What it's for --- --- fmt (fmt.md) Fixed decimals, padding, digit grouping, money (f-strings have no format specs) math (math.md) Square roots, rounding, logarithms, powers, trigonometry, pi random (random.md) Fast pseudo-random ints and floats (not for cryptography) time (time.md) Clocks, sleeping, RFC 3339 and strftime formatting and parsing, dates, durations os (os.md) Arguments, environment, exit codes, stdin / stderr, subprocesses, signals fs (fs.md) Files and directories: whole-file and line-by-line reading, stat, rename, errors json (json.md) Encoding any value to JSON, and decod"},{"p":"Standard library","h":"Builtins: no import needed","u":"std/index.html#builtins-no-import-needed","t":"Some functions are part of the language, not a module. They need no import. Your own functions may share their names; see builtin names (../reference.md 20-builtin-names): They are documented on the builtin functions (../builtins.md) page. Like any function, they can be called with method syntax: s.upper() is upper(s) , and xs.push(v) is push(&xs, v) . The same rule works for module functions whose first parameter has a module type: d.exec(sql, params) calls sqlite.exec(d, sql, params) , because d is a sqlite.Db ."},{"p":"Standard library","h":"Platform availability","u":"std/index.html#platform-availability","t":"Most modules work everywhere Tern runs. The exceptions depend on the operating system (sockets, SQLite, OpenSSL): Module macOS / Linux iOS Android WebAssembly --- --- --- --- --- fmt, math, random, time, json, re, log, crypto, compress, html, csv, path, flag, url, toml, binary, heap, pqueue, deque, bitset, term yes yes yes yes os, fs yes yes yes yes, inside the directories the host grants sqlite yes yes (SQLite from the SDK) no (the NDK has no SQLite) no migrate yes yes no (uses sqlite) no sqlgen yes yes no (uses sqlite) no http yes yes yes http.serve no; the helpers yes web, pg, redis, auth, "},{"p":"Standard library","h":"What is not in the standard library","u":"std/index.html#what-is-not-in-the-standard-library","t":"There is no MySQL client yet. For those, call a C library directly (see Calling C (../guides/c-interop.md))."},{"p":"fmt","h":"fmt","u":"std/fmt.html#fmt","t":"The fmt module turns numbers into text the way reports and receipts need them: a fixed number of decimals, zero padding, thousands separators, and money stored as cents. Tern's f-strings have no format specs ( {x:.2f} is an error), so this is where formatting lives. This page covers all six functions and their edge cases. fmt is written in Tern ( library/std/fmt.tn ); read it if you need to know exactly what happens."},{"p":"fmt","h":"fixed","u":"std/fmt.html#fixed","t":"x rounded to digits decimal places, always showing exactly that many digits. Halves round away from zero (like math.round (math.md round)), after x is multiplied by 10<sup digits</sup . - A value that rounds to zero prints without a minus sign: fixed(-0.004, 2) is \"0.00\" . - digits of 0 or less gives a whole number with no decimal point. - There is no digit grouping; combine with thousands for that (see the example at the end). - Binary floats can't store most decimals exactly, so a value that looks like a tie may round either way: 2.675 is really 2.67499999... . For money, store integer cents"},{"p":"fmt","h":"cents","u":"std/fmt.html#cents","t":"An amount stored as an integer number of cents, as a decimal with two places. Integer cents are the safe way to handle money: adding them never loses a cent."},{"p":"fmt","h":"zero_pad","u":"std/fmt.html#zero_pad","t":"n with leading zeros to make it width characters long. For a negative number the minus sign counts toward the width and the zeros go after it. A number that is already width characters or longer is returned unchanged."},{"p":"fmt","h":"pad_left","u":"std/fmt.html#pad_left","t":"s with copies of fill added on the left until it is width bytes long. Use it to right-align columns. Text already width bytes or longer is returned unchanged (never cut)."},{"p":"fmt","h":"pad_right","u":"std/fmt.html#pad_right","t":"s with copies of fill added on the right until it is width bytes long. Use it to left-align columns. Both functions count bytes , like len . For ASCII text that is the same as characters, but a letter such as \u00e9 is two bytes, so text containing it comes out one column short. fill should be one character: a longer fill is repeated whole, so the result can be longer than width ."},{"p":"fmt","h":"thousands","u":"std/fmt.html#thousands","t":"n with a comma between each group of three digits. The separator is always a comma; for other conventions, replace it: fmt.thousands(n).replace(\",\", \".\") ."},{"p":"fmt","h":"A complete example","u":"std/fmt.html#a-complete-example","t":"An invoice printed as an aligned table. Prices are kept in integer cents, so the total is exact:"},{"p":"math","h":"math","u":"std/math.html#math","t":"The math module has the floating-point functions: square root, rounding, logarithms and exponentials, powers, trigonometry, and pi, and popcount for bits. Every float function takes and returns float ; this page shows how to get an int back out, and what happens at the edges (negative square roots, logarithms of zero, halfway rounding). All of them take float arguments. Tern never converts an int for you, so write math.sqrt(float(n)) . To turn the result back into an int , use int(...) , which cuts off the fraction: int(math.round(x)) , int(math.floor(x)) . The functions map directly to the C "},{"p":"math","h":"sqrt","u":"std/math.html#sqrt","t":"The square root of x . The builtin sqrt(x) is the same function without the import. sqrt of a negative number is NaN."},{"p":"math","h":"pow","u":"std/math.html#pow","t":"x raised to the power y . Tern has no operator; this is it. pow(0.0, 0.0) is 1.0 , and a negative x with a fractional y is NaN. For integer powers of integers, a loop is exact where pow may not be for results above 2<sup 53</sup ."},{"p":"math","h":"floor","u":"std/math.html#floor","t":"The largest whole number not greater than x (rounds toward minus infinity)."},{"p":"math","h":"ceil","u":"std/math.html#ceil","t":"The smallest whole number not less than x (rounds toward plus infinity)."},{"p":"math","h":"round","u":"std/math.html#round","t":"x rounded to the nearest whole number. Halfway cases round away from zero : 2.5 becomes 3.0 and -2.5 becomes -3.0 . (This is C's round , not the \"banker's rounding\" of Python's round .) The three rounding functions return a float . int(x) on its own truncates toward zero, which differs from floor for negative numbers: Note -0.0 : IEEE floats have a negative zero, which compares equal to 0.0 and prints with its sign. To round to a number of decimal places for display, use fmt.fixed (fmt.md fixed) instead."},{"p":"math","h":"log","u":"std/math.html#log","t":"The natural logarithm (base e ) of x . log(0.0) is minus infinity and the log of a negative number is NaN. For other bases divide: log(x) / log(10.0) is log base 10."},{"p":"math","h":"exp","u":"std/math.html#exp","t":"e raised to the power x ; the inverse of log . Large arguments give infinity. NaN is the one value that is not equal to itself, so x != x tests for it. Print such a value and you see nan (or -nan on some machines)."},{"p":"math","h":"sin","u":"std/math.html#sin","t":""},{"p":"math","h":"cos","u":"std/math.html#cos","t":""},{"p":"math","h":"tan","u":"std/math.html#tan","t":"The sine, cosine, and tangent of an angle in radians . To convert from degrees, multiply by pi() / 180.0 . There are no inverse functions ( asin , atan2 , ...) or hyperbolic ones; call C's libm through extern if you need them (see Calling C (../guides/c-interop.md))."},{"p":"math","h":"pi","u":"std/math.html#pi","t":"The constant \u03c0, 3.141592653589793. It is a function because Tern has no global constants."},{"p":"math","h":"popcount","u":"std/math.html#popcount","t":"How many of x 's 64 bits are set (a negative number counts its sign bits too: -1 has 64). One CPU instruction; for comparing bit codes (Hamming distance is popcount(a ^ b) ), bit sets and hashes. simd.popcount counts 16 bytes at once."},{"p":"math","h":"A complete example","u":"std/math.html#a-complete-example","t":"Summary statistics for a list of readings, and the approximate distance between two points on Earth (the equirectangular approximation, accurate to well under 1% at city scale):"},{"p":"random","h":"random","u":"std/random.html#random","t":"The random module makes fast pseudo-random numbers: whole numbers in a range you choose, and floats between 0 and 1. This page covers both functions, how the generator is seeded, how it behaves across threads, and how to build shuffles and weighted choices from them. The generator is xoshiro256\\ \\ : very fast and statistically good, but not cryptographically secure . Don't use it for passwords, tokens, or session ids; for those, call the operating system's secure generator (such as arc4random or getrandom ) through C (see Calling C (../guides/c-interop.md))."},{"p":"random","h":"Seeding and threads","u":"std/random.html#seeding-and-threads","t":"Each thread has its own generator, seeded automatically the first time that thread asks for a number (from the clock and the thread's stack address). So: - every run of the program gives different numbers; - there is no seed function, so you can't replay a sequence. If you need repeatable numbers (tests, simulations you want to reproduce), write a small generator of your own in Tern, like the one at the end of this page; - threads never share or lock a generator, so parallel code can call random freely and it stays fast."},{"p":"random","h":"int","u":"std/random.html#int","t":"A random whole number from lo to hi , both included . random.int(1, 6) is a die roll. lo == hi always returns lo . If hi < lo the program stops with a runtime error ( random.int: hi < lo ). The result is lo + (x % span) for a 64-bit random x , so ranges that don't divide 2<sup 64</sup evenly are very slightly biased; for any range under a few billion the bias is far below anything measurable. Note that random.int is a module function, not the int() conversion builtin: with import random both exist, told apart by the random. prefix."},{"p":"random","h":"float","u":"std/random.html#float","t":"A random float x with 0.0 <= x < 1.0 , uniformly spread (53 random bits, so every value is a multiple of 2<sup -53</sup ). It is never 1.0 . Scale it for other ranges: lo + random.float() (hi - lo) ."},{"p":"random","h":"A complete example","u":"std/random.html#a-complete-example","t":"A Fisher-Yates shuffle, a weighted choice, and a small seedable generator for when you need the same sequence every run:"},{"p":"time","h":"time","u":"std/time.html#time","t":"The time module reads the clocks, pauses the program, and turns times into text and back: RFC 3339 timestamps, strftime-style layouts, calendar fields in UTC or the local time zone, and durations like \"1h30m\" . A time is a float of seconds since the Unix epoch, the value time.now() returns, so times compare, subtract and store as plain numbers."},{"p":"time","h":"now","u":"std/time.html#now","t":"The current time in seconds since the Unix epoch (1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC), with a fraction for the sub-second part (to about a microsecond at today's dates)."},{"p":"time","h":"now_ms","u":"std/time.html#now_ms","t":"The current time in milliseconds since the Unix epoch, as a whole number. It is the same clock as now , so time.now_ms() / 1000 equals int(time.now()) (give or take the moment between the two calls). Use now_ms for timestamps you store or compare, since int arithmetic is exact. Both read the wall clock ( CLOCK_REALTIME ). If the system clock is changed (by NTP or by hand), the next reading jumps with it, so a duration measured across that change can be wrong or even negative. For timing code that is almost never an issue, but don't rely on two readings being in order."},{"p":"time","h":"sleep","u":"std/time.html#sleep","t":"Pauses the current thread for at least ms milliseconds (the operating system may wake it a little later). sleep(0) and negative values return at once. Inside a parallel block only the task that calls it waits; the others keep running."},{"p":"time","h":"Monotonic clock","u":"std/time.html#monotonic-clock","t":"A clock that only moves forward, for measuring how long something took: unlike now , it doesn't jump when the system clock is set. Its zero point is arbitrary (usually boot)."},{"p":"time","h":"Formatting and parsing","u":"std/time.html#formatting-and-parsing","t":"RFC 3339 is the timestamp format of JSON APIs and logs. parse_rfc3339 accepts T or a space between date and time, any fraction of a second, and Z or a +hh:mm offset (an offset is required: a time without one is ambiguous). Layouts are C's strftime / strptime ones ( %Y %m %d %H %M %S %j %A %B %Z , ...) plus %L for milliseconds and %f for microseconds. A parse that fails returns 0.0 and time.error() says why; after a success time.error() is \"\" ."},{"p":"time","h":"Calendar fields and time zones","u":"std/time.html#calendar-fields-and-time-zones","t":"Date has the fields year , month (1-12), day , hour , minute , second , nano (nanoseconds), weekday (0 is Sunday), yday (1-366) and offset (seconds east of UTC). local uses the system's time zone (the TZ environment variable, or the machine's setting), with daylight saving time. date builds a UTC time from fields; values out of range carry over, so month 13 is January of the next year and day 0 is the last day of the previous month, which makes calendar arithmetic simple:"},{"p":"time","h":"Durations","u":"std/time.html#durations","t":"The format Go uses (units h , m , s , ms , us , ns ), plus days d (24h) and weeks w (7d), so a lookback like 7d or 2w can be written directly. parse_duration returns 0.0 and sets time.error() for text that isn't a duration (a bare number needs a unit)."},{"p":"time","h":"With Result","u":"std/time.html#with-result","t":"try_parse_rfc3339(s) , try_parse(s, layout) and try_parse_duration(s) return Result float, str : t = time.try_parse_rfc3339(s)? . The error reads like \"time: not an RFC 3339 time: yesterday\" ."},{"p":"time","h":"A complete example","u":"std/time.html#a-complete-example","t":"Timing a function over several runs and reporting the best and average times: Its output depends on the machine, for example:"},{"p":"os","h":"os","u":"std/os.html#os","t":"The os module connects a program to the process that runs it: command-line arguments, environment variables, the exit status, standard input and error, other programs, and signals. This page covers each and the patterns for small command-line tools."},{"p":"os","h":"args","u":"std/os.html#args","t":"The command-line arguments. args() 0 is the program itself (the path it was started with); the arguments you typed start at index 1. With tern run , put the program's arguments after -- : Every call returns a fresh array; call it once and keep the result. Arguments are raw strings: convert numbers with to_int / to_float and a default, and check len before indexing, since reading past the end is a runtime error. For flags ( --top 5 , -v , --help ), use the flag (flag.md) module rather than reading os.args() by hand: it fills a struct, checks each value's type, and writes the usage."},{"p":"os","h":"env","u":"std/os.html#env","t":"The value of the environment variable name , or default if it isn't set. A variable that is set to the empty string returns \"\" , not the default. Names are case-sensitive. There is no way to set or list variables."},{"p":"os","h":"exit","u":"std/os.html#exit","t":"Ends the program at once with exit status code : 0 means success, anything else failure. Standard output is flushed first, so everything already printed appears. Nothing else runs afterwards: no code after the call, no other tasks. Operating systems keep only the low 8 bits of the status, so use 0 to 125. Returning from main exits with status 0, and a runtime error exits with status 2, so exit is for reporting failure yourself, or for stopping early from deep inside the program."},{"p":"os","h":"eprint, read_line, read_all","u":"std/os.html#eprint-read_line-read_all","t":"Errors and progress go to stderr with eprint , so stdout stays clean for output that is piped on. In a task, waiting for input parks only the task."},{"p":"os","h":"run","u":"std/os.html#run","t":"Runs a program (found on PATH ), waits for it, and returns its exit status , what it printed on stdout ( out ) and on stderr ( err ). run_input also feeds it input on stdin. A status of 128 + n means signal n ended it; -1 means it couldn't be started, and then err says why. Arguments are passed as they are, with no shell in between (so no quoting or injection issues); to use shell features, run sh with -c . In a task, the wait happens on the blocking-thread pool, so other tasks keep running."},{"p":"os","h":"spawn","u":"std/os.html#spawn","t":"Starts a program and returns while it keeps running: a server to test against, a long-running job to watch, a program to talk to over its stdin and stdout. spawn gives it pipes for stdin and stdout and lets it print to our stderr. spawn_with also says where it runs ( dir , \"\" for here), adds \"NAME=value\" entries to its environment ( env ), and where its stdout ( out : \"pipe\" , \"inherit\" , \"null\" , or a file path) and stderr ( err : \"inherit\" , \"out\" for into its stdout, \"null\" , or a file path) go. Call What it does --- --- p.ok() bool false if it couldn't be started; os.error() says why p.wri"},{"p":"os","h":"cwd, chdir, setenv, error","u":"std/os.html#cwd-chdir-setenv-error","t":"cwd is the working directory; chdir changes it for the whole program (false if it can't, and os.error() says why). setenv sets a variable in the program's environment, which the programs it starts inherit (an empty value removes it). error says why the last spawn or chdir on this thread failed ( \"\" after a success)."},{"p":"os","h":"wait_signal","u":"std/os.html#wait_signal","t":"Waits for one of SIGINT (Ctrl-C), SIGTERM (what kill and container runtimes send), SIGHUP, SIGUSR1 or SIGUSR2 and returns its name. The first call makes those signals events for the program instead of ending it, so call it early (from a task) when you want to clean up before exiting. Each signal goes to one waiter. roost.serve handles SIGINT and SIGTERM itself: it stops accepting connections, lets the requests in progress finish (up to TERN_WEB_DRAIN_SEC seconds, default 10), and returns."},{"p":"os","h":"on_error, last_error_report","u":"std/os.html#on_error-last_error_report","t":"For error trackers (Sentry and the like): after os.on_error(f) , every runtime error calls f with a report, including the ones something catches: a task that fails, a web handler's error that became a 500 , an error inside parallel: . A report has the message as printed, the file, line and Tern function where it happened, the frames it came through (innermost first; each a Tern function name, or the C symbol, and its address), what's needed to symbolicate the addresses elsewhere (where the executable was loaded, and its build id: the Mach-O UUID or the ELF GNU build id, in hex), the task and t"},{"p":"os","h":"A complete example","u":"std/os.html#a-complete-example","t":"A small command-line tool that counts lines, words, and bytes in the files it is given, and exits with status 1 if any file can't be read (usage: ./wc file... ):"},{"p":"fs","h":"fs","u":"std/fs.html#fs","t":"The fs module reads and writes files and works with directories. There are seven functions, and none of them stops the program on failure: each one reports it with an empty result or false . This page covers every function, what \"failure\" looks like for each, and the patterns for reading line-based and JSON files. Paths are plain strings, passed to the operating system as they are. A relative path is relative to the directory the program was started in (not the program's own location). Files are read and written as bytes, with no newline translation or encoding checks."},{"p":"fs","h":"read","u":"std/fs.html#read","t":"The whole file as a string. If the file can't be read (it doesn't exist, it's a directory, or permission is denied), read returns \"\" . An empty file also gives \"\" , so when the difference matters, check fs.exists(path) first. The file is read in one go into memory. Strings are limited to 4GB. text.split(\"\\n\") on text that ends with a newline gives an empty last element. Skip empty lines as above, or split text.trim() (which also removes leading whitespace). Files written on Windows have \\r\\n line ends: line.trim() removes the \\r ."},{"p":"fs","h":"write","u":"std/fs.html#write","t":"Creates the file, or empties it if it exists, and writes text . Returns true if every byte was written and the file was closed cleanly. Returns false if the file can't be created, for example because its directory doesn't exist ( write doesn't create directories; use mkdir ) or permission is denied. The write is not atomic: a crash halfway through can leave a partial file. To replace an important file safely, write a temporary file next to it and check the result before removing the old one."},{"p":"fs","h":"append","u":"std/fs.html#append","t":"Adds text to the end of the file, creating it if it doesn't exist. Returns false in the same cases as write . Each call opens and closes the file, so it's fine for logs but slow for writing a large file piece by piece: build the text in a string and write it once."},{"p":"fs","h":"exists","u":"std/fs.html#exists","t":"true if something exists at path : a file or a directory."},{"p":"fs","h":"remove","u":"std/fs.html#remove","t":"Deletes a file, or an empty directory. Returns false if nothing was removed: the path doesn't exist, the directory isn't empty, or permission is denied. There is no recursive delete; list the directory and remove its entries first."},{"p":"fs","h":"mkdir","u":"std/fs.html#mkdir","t":"Creates one directory (permissions 755 , before the umask). Returns true if the directory was created or already exists , so it is safe to call every time the program starts. The parent must exist: mkdir(\"a/b/c\") fails if a/b doesn't. Note that mkdir also returns true if a file already has that name."},{"p":"fs","h":"list","u":"std/fs.html#list","t":"The names of the entries in a directory: files and subdirectories, without . and .. , and without the directory's path in front. The order is whatever the operating system returns, which is not sorted; sort with sort_by . A missing or unreadable directory gives . Entries aren't marked as files or directories ( list_marked marks them), and list doesn't look inside subdirectories."},{"p":"fs","h":"list_marked","u":"std/fs.html#list_marked","t":"list , with a / after the name of each directory (a symlink to one counts). The operating system says which entries are directories while listing them, so this is one system call per batch of names, where list followed by stat of every name is one more per name: walking a tree of 20,000 files takes about a third of the time."},{"p":"fs","h":"error","u":"std/fs.html#error","t":"Why the last fs call on this thread failed, with the operating system's reason: \"can't read notes.txt: No such file or directory\" , \"can't write /etc/x: Permission denied\" . It is \"\" after a call that succeeded, so check it right after the call you care about."},{"p":"fs","h":"stat and rename","u":"std/fs.html#stat-and-rename","t":"Info has the fields exists , is_dir , size (bytes) and modified (seconds since 1970, like time.now() ). For a path that can't be read, exists is false and fs.error() says why. rename moves a file or directory (replacing a file at to ); within one file system it is atomic, which makes \"write to a temporary name, then rename\" the safe way to replace a file."},{"p":"fs","h":"walk, copy, chmod, remove_all, temp_dir","u":"std/fs.html#walk-copy-chmod-remove_all-temp_dir","t":"chmod sets a file's permission bits: fs.chmod(path, 0o600) lets only its owner read and write it, which is what a file holding a key or a secret wants (on WebAssembly, which has no permissions, it only checks that the file exists). walk lists every file below dir (not the directories themselves), as dir/sub/name paths, sorted. copy copies a file's bytes and its permissions (a copied program stays runnable). remove_all removes a file, or a directory and everything in it; it returns true if the path is gone, including when it never existed. temp_dir makes a new empty directory in $TMPDIR (or /tm"},{"p":"fs","h":"Reading and writing a piece at a time","u":"std/fs.html#reading-and-writing-a-piece-at-a-time","t":"For files too big to read at once, or logs written as you go. Check f.ok() after opening ( fs.error() says why it failed). Lines keep their \"\\n\" , so an empty line is \"\\n\" and only the end of the file is \"\" ; the last line may lack the \"\\n\" . Writes are buffered: f.flush() writes out what was put so far, and f.close() (or the program's end) writes out the rest. Reading at the end of a file that is still growing (a log) returns \"\" , and the next read picks up what was added since. In a task, file calls run on the task's thread; for very large files use task.blocking so the other tasks on that c"},{"p":"fs","h":"With Result","u":"std/fs.html#with-result","t":"The same operations as Result values, whose error says what failed and why, so ? can pass a failure up:"},{"p":"fs","h":"A complete example","u":"std/fs.html#a-complete-example","t":"A small note-taking store: settings in a JSON file with defaults, and one file per note in a directory:"},{"p":"path","h":"path","u":"std/path.html#path","t":"The path module works with file paths as text: joining, cleaning, taking them apart, extensions, relative paths, and shell-style patterns. Paths use / , as on Linux, macOS and WebAssembly. Only path.abs (which reads the working directory) and path.glob (which lists directories) look at the disk; everything else is string work, the same as Go's path ."},{"p":"path","h":"Functions","u":"std/path.html#functions","t":""},{"p":"path","h":"Joining","u":"std/path.html#joining","t":"join puts one / between its parts and cleans the result. Like Go's filepath.Join , an absolute second part does not start over: join(\"/a\", \"/b\") is /a/b . When the second part may be absolute and should win (a path from a config file, relative to the config's directory unless it's absolute), use resolve , which works like Python's os.path.join :"},{"p":"path","h":"Cleaning","u":"std/path.html#cleaning","t":"clean applies Go's rules by text alone, without looking at the disk (so a/.. is removed even when a is a symlink): one / between names, no . names, each name/.. removed, no trailing / . A .. that would go above the start of a relative path stays, one above / is dropped. The empty path is . . A path that is already clean is returned as it is, after one pass over its bytes."},{"p":"path","h":"Names and extensions","u":"std/path.html#names-and-extensions","t":"dir and base follow Go: dir(\"a\") is . , dir(\"/a\") is / , base(\"a/b/\") is b . ext is from the last . of the last name, but a name's first byte doesn't count, so .bashrc has no extension (as in Python, unlike Go): with_ext is the replacement for writing p ..len(p) - 3 + \".o\" by hand: it leaves a dot in a directory name alone."},{"p":"path","h":"Relative paths","u":"std/path.html#relative-paths","t":"rel(base, target) is a path that names target when joined to base , both cleaned first. It is an Err when one is absolute and the other isn't, or when base climbs out with .. further than target (there is no way back down without knowing the directory names):"},{"p":"path","h":"Patterns and glob","u":"std/path.html#patterns-and-glob","t":"matches(pattern, name) matches the whole name: Pattern Matches --- --- any run of bytes, except / ? one byte, except / abc , a-z one byte in the set ( first, or - first or last, is itself) !a-z , ^a-z one byte not in the set (and not / ) \\x x itself ( \\ is a star) A malformed pattern (a with no , a \\ at the end) matches nothing. never makes matching slow: it keeps one place to retry from, so the work is at most the pattern's length times the name's. glob(pattern) lists the paths that exist and match, sorted. Each / -separated piece of the pattern is matched against one name, and a piece that i"},{"p":"path","h":"Speed","u":"std/path.html#speed","t":"bench/std runs the same work in Go ( path , path/filepath , and doublestar for ) and Rust ( std::path , plus the path-clean , pathdiff and glob crates) on a million paths, a quarter of them needing cleaning. Tern is the fastest of the three at each operation and uses the least memory: Tern Go Rust --- --- --- --- clean 37 ns 75 ns 200 ns dir + base + ext + stem 45 ns 62 ns 51 ns join 46 ns 140 ns 455 ns rel 120 ns 170 ns 190 ns matches , 4 patterns 18 ns 62 ns 28 ns (a compiled glob::Pattern ) glob(\" / .tn\") , 20,000 files 17.5 ms 40 ms 20 ms peak memory 122 MB 216 MB 232 MB clean and the name"},{"p":"flag","h":"flag","u":"std/flag.html#flag","t":"The flag module reads command-line flags into a struct. Each field is a flag, named after the field ( min_len is --min-len ), and its type says what the flag takes. The values the struct holds before parsing are the defaults, and --help is written for you. In a program, flag.parse(c, &o) reads os.args() (without the program's name) and returns the positional arguments. Make main return a Result so a mistake on the command line is printed as error: ... with exit status 1:"},{"p":"flag","h":"Functions","u":"std/flag.html#functions","t":"c.flag(...) changes c (its first parameter is mut ), so c must be a variable."},{"p":"flag","h":"What a flag takes","u":"std/flag.html#what-a-flag-takes","t":"Field type On the command line --- --- int , float --port 80 , --port=80 , -p 80 , -p80 , -p=80 str the same; the value may start with - ( --offset -5 ) bool --verbose (true), --verbose=false , --no-verbose ; -v , and short ones together: -vq an enum without payloads a variant's name in any case: --format json T of one of those repeated: --tag a --tag b Option T of one of those None unless the flag is given -- ends the flags (everything after it is positional), and - alone is a positional (by convention, standard input). Positionals may come before, between or after flags, unless c.stop_at_arg"},{"p":"flag","h":"Environment variables and required flags","u":"std/flag.html#environment-variables-and-required-flags","t":"c.env(field, var) reads var when the flag isn't on the command line (a list's variable holds a,b,c ); c.require(field) makes parsing fail unless the flag or its variable gives a value:"},{"p":"flag","h":"Help","u":"std/flag.html#help","t":"-h or --help prints the usage and exits with status 0. With c.exit_on_help = false it is an Err holding the usage instead (for tests). flag.usage(c, o) returns the same text:"},{"p":"flag","h":"Commands","u":"std/flag.html#commands","t":"For a tool with commands ( tool -v build --release ), parse the common flags with stop_at_args() , then the command's own flags from what's left:"},{"p":"flag","h":"How it works, and speed","u":"std/flag.html#how-it-works-and-speed","t":"The compiler writes a small table of each struct's fields (name, type, an enum's variants) into the program as a constant, and a setter that stores an int, float, bool, string or enum variant into field number k. flag looks each flag up in the table (in C, without copying the argument), converts its value, and calls the setter: no reflection, and no text in between. On an error, and for --help , the struct is put back as it was. bench/std builds the parser and parses a 16-argument command line into 8 fields, against Go's flag and Rust's clap (derive) and lexopt (a match written by hand, no hel"},{"p":"term","h":"term","u":"std/term.html#term","t":"The term module is for programs that talk to a person at a terminal: whether output goes to one, its size, colors that turn themselves off when they shouldn't show, aligned tables, a progress line, and reading a password without echoing it."},{"p":"term","h":"Functions","u":"std/term.html#functions","t":""},{"p":"term","h":"Colors","u":"std/term.html#colors","t":"The color functions wrap text in ANSI escapes only when they would show: stdout is a terminal, NO_COLOR is unset, and TERM isn't dumb . FORCE_COLOR=1 turns them on regardless (CI logs that render color), and term.set_color(on) overrides both. The decision is made once, on the first use. Styles nest: term.bold(term.red(\"x\")) ."},{"p":"term","h":"Progress and passwords","u":"std/term.html#progress-and-passwords","t":"term.progress(done, total, label) draws label -------- 42% 21/50 on stderr, over the previous one, and ends the line when done == total . It draws nothing when stderr isn't a terminal, so a program can call it unconditionally and still have clean logs when piped. term.secret(prompt) turns echo off while the person types (when stdin is a terminal) and returns the line without its line end, for a password or a token. On WebAssembly, which has no terminal control, it reads a plain line."},{"p":"term","h":"Reading a line","u":"std/term.html#reading-a-line","t":"term.read_line(prompt) is os.read_line with a prompt and line editing: when stdin and stderr are terminals, the arrow keys, Home/End, Backspace/Delete and Ctrl-A/E/K/U/W edit the line, and Up/Down bring back the lines it returned before, as in a shell. It returns the line with its \"\\n\" , \"\" at the end of input (Ctrl-D on an empty line), and \"\\x03\" for Ctrl-C. Piped input is read as a plain line. tern repl reads with it."},{"p":"url","h":"url","u":"std/url.html#url","t":"The url module parses, builds and resolves URLs and escapes their parts, by the rules of Go's net/url (RFC 3986). A Url is its text, written the way format writes it, plus where each part is: you read the parts with methods ( u.host() , u.path() ), decoded where that applies (the path, user and fragment), and change them with setters. The query stays as written until you ask for its pairs."},{"p":"url","h":"Functions","u":"std/url.html#functions","t":""},{"p":"url","h":"The parts","u":"std/url.html#the-parts","t":"Method https://ada:pw@example.com:8443/a%20b?q=1 top --- --- u.scheme() \"https\" (lower case when parsed) u.user() , u.password() \"ada\" , \"pw\" (decoded) u.host() \"example.com\" (an IPv6 address without its brackets: \"::1\" ) u.port() 8443 ( 0 when the URL has none) u.path() \"/a b\" (decoded); u.escaped_path() is \"/a%20b\" u.raw_query() \"q=1\" (as written; u.query() decodes it) u.fragment() \"top\" (decoded) u.opaque() what follows scheme: when it is neither // nor a path: mailto:ada@x.org has \"ada@x.org\" u.raw_path() and u.raw_fragment() give those parts as the text has them when that isn't how escapi"},{"p":"url","h":"Errors","u":"std/url.html#errors","t":""},{"p":"url","h":"Resolving links","u":"std/url.html#resolving-links","t":"resolve and join follow RFC 3986 section 5, including its dot segments:"},{"p":"url","h":"Queries","u":"std/url.html#queries","t":"A Query keeps its pairs in the order given ( q.keys , q.vals ), and a key may repeat. parse_query is an Err for a bad escape or a ; (once a separator, and servers still disagree on it), while u.query() skips such pairs, as a server reading a request would:"},{"p":"url","h":"Speed and memory","u":"std/url.html#speed-and-memory","t":"bench/std does the same work with Go's net/url and Rust's url crate (WHATWG URLs, which also normalizes) on a million generated URLs with paths, queries, some escapes, ports, user info and fragments. For parse each program also reads the host, path, query and port. ns per URL Tern Go Rust --- --- --- --- parse 195 320 238 format 4 162 26 resolve + format 94 534 124 query() , every pair 93 189 110 query_escape + unescape 90 200 271 peak memory (1M parsed) 238 MB 529 MB 496 MB ( format is a string Tern already has, shared; Rust's to_string copies its text.) A Url is its text and five ints of pos"},{"p":"toml","h":"toml","u":"std/toml.html#toml","t":"The toml module reads TOML 1.0 (config files such as Cargo.toml and pyproject.toml ) into a struct or into a json.Value , and writes structs back out as TOML. It passes every TOML 1.0 case in the toml-test (https://github.com/toml-lang/toml-test) suite: the valid documents parse to the expected values, and the invalid ones are errors. decode fills the struct by field name, as json.decode does: a key the file leaves out keeps the value the struct started with (so start it with the defaults), a key the struct doesn't have is ignored, and a field that may be missing can be an Option that starts a"},{"p":"toml","h":"Functions","u":"std/toml.html#functions","t":""},{"p":"toml","h":"Values","u":"std/toml.html#values","t":"TOML becomes --- --- table, inline table object ( Obj ), keys in the order written array, array of tables array string (all four kinds) string integer ( 1_000 , 0xff , 0o7 , 0b1 ) number (64-bit) float number; inf is a float too big for a double (inf when read into a float ), nan is null true , false bool date and time string, in RFC 3339 form: \"1979-05-27T07:32:00Z\" , \"1979-05-27T07:32:00\" , \"1979-05-27\" , \"07:32:00\" A document of unknown shape is a json.Value , read with field , at and the _or defaults:"},{"p":"toml","h":"Errors","u":"std/toml.html#errors","t":"An error names the line and what is wrong there:"},{"p":"toml","h":"Writing","u":"std/toml.html#writing","t":"encode and dump write a table's plain keys first, then its tables under headers and its arrays of tables under headers ; other values (arrays, tables inside arrays) are written inline. TOML has no null, so a None field or a null is left out. Only a struct or a map can be a document: toml.encode(5) is an Err ."},{"p":"toml","h":"Speed and memory","u":"std/toml.html#speed-and-memory","t":"bench/std does the same work with Go's pelletier/go-toml v2 (the fastest Go TOML library) and Rust's toml crate with serde: a 9.7 MB Cargo.lock-like file of 40,000 packages parsed into a dynamic value, decoded into structs, and the structs encoded; and a 1.2 KB service config decoded into a struct 20,000 times. Tern Go (go-toml v2) Rust (toml 0.9) --- --- --- --- parse into a dynamic value 378 MB/s 170 216 decode into structs 473 MB/s 252 244 encode the structs 398 MB/s 205 315 decode a 1.2 KB config 4.2 us 9.8 7.6 peak RSS 83 MB 91 294 (best of 5 interleaved runs; Go's peak memory moves betwe"},{"p":"binary","h":"binary","u":"std/binary.html#binary","t":"The binary module reads and writes numbers in binary formats: fixed-width integers and floats in big- or little-endian byte order, and varints (LEB128, the variable-length integers of protobuf, Go's encoding/binary , WebAssembly and SQLite records). Bytes are a str (any bytes, NULs included); a byte converts with to_str() and to_bytes() without a copy. Build bytes with a Writer , read them back in order with a Reader , or read and overwrite at any offset:"},{"p":"binary","h":"Writer","u":"std/binary.html#writer","t":"A put of an int writes its low bytes, so signed and unsigned values are written alike: put_u32be(-1) is FF FF FF FF , and put_u16le(70000) keeps the low 16 bits. A Writer is a value like any other: copying one ( w2 = w ) gives two writers that go their own ways, and bytes() doesn't change when the Writer writes more afterwards."},{"p":"binary","h":"Reader","u":"std/binary.html#reader","t":"A read that doesn't fit (past the end of the data, a varint cut short or longer than 64 bits, as Go's binary.Uvarint decides) returns 0 (or \"\" ), and so does every read after it. The first problem is kept with its offset, so a parser reads a whole message and checks once: has checks a fixed-size record once: after if not r.has(22): ... the compiler drops the checks of the reads that follow, so they cost what reads of a plain array do."},{"p":"binary","h":"At an offset","u":"std/binary.html#at-an-offset","t":"Reading or writing past the end of s is a runtime error, as s.byte(i) is. int is 64 bits, so a u64 above 2^63-1 reads as a negative number with the same bits: u64be of eight FF bytes is -1."},{"p":"binary","h":"Many floats at once","u":"std/binary.html#many-floats-at-once","t":"Vectors, embeddings and model weights are stored as raw little-endian floats (numpy's .tofile , .npy data, safetensors). These read a whole run into an f32 in one copy, and write one back; halves save half the space where 3 significant digits are enough."},{"p":"binary","h":"Speed and memory","u":"std/binary.html#speed-and-memory","t":"bench/std does the same work with Go's encoding/binary ( Append , the byte-order getters, Uvarint ) and Rust's bytes crate ( BufMut on a Vec<u8 , Buf on a & u8 ) with prost's varints, the ones protobuf uses in Rust. Fixed-width: 100,000 records of a u16, a u32, a u64 and an f64 (2.2 MB) appended, then read back at offsets and in order; varints: 500,000 values of every length from 1 to 10 bytes, and 500,000 small ones (one or two bytes, as tags and lengths mostly are). Millions of values a second, best of 7 interleaved runs: Tern Go Rust --- --- --- --- fixed-width put 773 205 672 fixed-width g"},{"p":"heap","h":"heap","u":"std/heap.html#heap","t":"The heap module is a heap of ints, floats or strings: pop gives the smallest value first ( min_heap ) or the largest ( max_heap ). Use it for the k smallest of a stream, merging sorted lists, or any \"next lowest\" loop. For values that aren't ordered themselves, such as structs, use pqueue (pqueue.md)."},{"p":"heap","h":"Functions","u":"std/heap.html#functions","t":"T is int , float or str . A heap is a value like any other: h2 = h copies it, and the functions that change one take it mut , so h.push(x) is push(&h, x) ."},{"p":"heap","h":"How it works, and speed","u":"std/heap.html#how-it-works-and-speed","t":"The heap is 4-ary: each value has up to four children, side by side in memory. That halves the levels of a binary heap, so pop , which moves the hole at the top down to a leaf by the best child and then lets the old last value rise, takes about half the cache misses. A heap that has held 4096 values or more gives its memory back when pop empties it. bench/std pushes 2 million ints and pops them all: Tern 115 ms, Rust's BinaryHeap (binary) 129 ms, Go's container/heap 411 ms."},{"p":"pqueue","h":"pqueue","u":"std/pqueue.html#pqueue","t":"The pqueue module is a priority queue: values of any type, each pushed with a priority (an int, float or string), taken out lowest priority first ( queue ) or highest first ( queue_max ). The priority comes first, as in Python's heapq tuples. It's what Dijkstra's algorithm, schedulers and event loops need."},{"p":"pqueue","h":"Functions","u":"std/pqueue.html#functions","t":"Values with equal priorities come out in no particular order. For shortest paths, push a node again when its distance improves and skip it on the way out if it's stale:"},{"p":"pqueue","h":"Speed","u":"std/pqueue.html#speed","t":"A 4-ary heap, like heap (heap.md)'s, kept as two arrays (priorities and values). bench/std runs Dijkstra on a 1000 x 1000 grid: Tern 29.3 ms, Rust's BinaryHeap of tuples 32.2 ms, Go's container/heap 108 ms."},{"p":"deque","h":"deque","u":"std/deque.html#deque","t":"The deque module is a double-ended queue: push and pop at either end in O(1). Use it as a queue (breadth-first search), a sliding window, or a bounded history."},{"p":"deque","h":"Functions","u":"std/deque.html#functions","t":""},{"p":"deque","h":"How it works, and speed","u":"std/deque.html#how-it-works-and-speed","t":"A ring buffer whose size is a power of two. Growing doubles it by appending a copy of itself (Tern has no empty slots), which also puts the values that had wrapped around to the front right after the old end: nothing moves, and the array grows in place when it can. A popped slot keeps its old value until it's written again; clear() lets go of everything. bench/std keeps a window of 1000 over 20 million values with a push at the front every 16th: Tern 59.3 ms, Rust's VecDeque 54.7 ms, Go ( gammazero/deque ; Go's standard library has none) 92 ms. The difference with Rust is the copy-on-write che"},{"p":"bitset","h":"bitset","u":"std/bitset.html#bitset","t":"The bitset module is a set of small non-negative ints, one bit each: a sieve, a visited set, a set of flags. Setting a bit past the end grows the set."},{"p":"bitset","h":"Functions","u":"std/bitset.html#functions","t":""},{"p":"bitset","h":"Speed","u":"std/bitset.html#speed","t":"bench/std sieves the primes up to 100 million and sums them: Tern 366 ms, Rust's fixedbitset 366 ms, Go's bits-and-blooms/bitset 445 ms."},{"p":"json","h":"json","u":"std/json.html#json","t":"The json module converts Tern values to JSON text and back. json.encode turns any struct, array, map, or basic value into compact JSON; json.decode fills a typed variable from JSON text and tells you whether it worked. This page covers exactly how each type maps to JSON, and what decode does with missing keys, unknown keys, null , and wrong types. There is no reflection in Tern: the compiler writes a dedicated encoder and decoder for each type you use with json , so both are fast and type-checked at compile time."},{"p":"json","h":"How types map to JSON","u":"std/json.html#how-types-map-to-json","t":"Tern type JSON --- --- int a number without a fraction: 42 float a number, always with a fraction or exponent: 2.0 , 0.1 , 1e+20 ; NaN and infinity become null bool true / false str a string. \" , \\ , and control characters are escaped; other bytes (UTF-8 text such as \u00e9 ) are written as they are T an array {str: T} an object {int: T} an object whose keys are the numbers as strings: {\"1\": ...} a struct an object with one key per field, in declaration order Option T null for None , the value for Some(v) . Decoding, null or a missing key gives None an enum \"Name\" for a variant without fields; {\"Na"},{"p":"json","h":"encode","u":"std/json.html#encode","t":"The JSON text of any value whose type is in the table above. The output is compact (no spaces or newlines) and always valid JSON. Floats use the shortest text that reads back as the same number, so a float round-trips exactly. For a single string, the builtin json_quote (../builtins.md json_quote) does the same as json.encode without the import."},{"p":"json","h":"decode","u":"std/json.html#decode","t":"Parses text as JSON and stores it in out , which you pass with & . Returns true on success. Decoding is all-or-nothing : if it returns false , out is exactly as it was, even if part of the text was valid. Whitespace around the value is allowed; anything else after it makes decoding fail. Into a struct, it follows these rules: - Missing keys keep the current value. Fill out with defaults before decoding, and keys the JSON leaves out keep them. This is how you merge a partial update. - Unknown keys are ignored , whatever their value (objects, arrays, anything valid). - null keeps the current val"},{"p":"json","h":"Type mismatches","u":"std/json.html#type-mismatches","t":"A value of the wrong type makes the whole decode fail: Field type Accepts Rejects --- --- --- int whole numbers, including 4.0 and 1e2 4.5 , \"5\" , true , numbers beyond 64 bits float any number ( 3 becomes 3.0 ) \"3.5\" , true bool true , false 1 , \"true\" str strings (with \\n , \\\" , \u00e9 , and surrogate-pair escapes decoded) numbers, true T an array whose every element fits T null elements, a lone value {str: T} an object an array {int: T} an object whose keys are whole numbers as strings ( \"12\" ) any other key a struct an object an array, null at the top level The parser is slightly more lenient t"},{"p":"json","h":"Decoding plain values","u":"std/json.html#decoding-plain-values","t":"out can be any JSON-compatible type, not just a struct:"},{"p":"json","h":"schema","u":"std/json.html#schema","t":"json.schema T () is the JSON Schema (https://json-schema.org) (2020-12) of what json.encode writes for a type T , as a string. Like the encoder, it is generated while compiling, so it costs nothing at run time. Structs go under $defs and are referred to with $ref (so a struct can contain arrays of itself), and every field is required , because json.encode always writes all of them. The openapi package builds API descriptions from it. Enums and tuples have no JSON form, so json.schema refuses them, as json.encode does. A struct from another module is named with its module ( models.User ), and a"},{"p":"json","h":"Working with JSON of unknown shape","u":"std/json.html#working-with-json-of-unknown-shape","t":"decode needs a type to fill. For JSON whose shape varies or isn't known ahead of time (a field that is sometimes a number and sometimes a string, a webhook with dozens of optional fields, a config you only read a few keys of), json.parse(text) gives a json.Value : an enum of Null , Bool(b) , Num(x, text) , Str(s) , Arr(items) and Obj(keys, vals) . Lookups never fail: v.field(key) and v.at(i) give Null when the key or element isn't there (or v isn't an object or array), so they chain, and str_or , int_or , float_or , bool_or end the chain with a default. match on the Value when you need to know"},{"p":"json","h":"A complete example","u":"std/json.html#a-complete-example","t":"Loading a configuration file with defaults, validating it, and saving it back:"},{"p":"re","h":"re","u":"std/re.html#re","t":"The re module has regular expressions: search, extract, validate, replace and split text by pattern. It runs in time linear in the text's length, whatever the pattern, so a pattern from a user or a config file can't hang a server (no \"catastrophic backtracking\"). re.compile returns Result re.Regex, str : the error says what's wrong and where. For a pattern written in the program, re.must(pattern) gives the Regex directly (a bad pattern is a runtime error). Compiling is cached: the same pattern compiles once per program, so re.must inside a loop or a handler is fine. Patterns are ordinary strin"},{"p":"re","h":"Functions","u":"std/re.html#functions","t":"A re.Match has start and end (byte offsets: s m.start..m.end is the match), text , groups ( groups 0 is the whole match, groups 1 the first group, \"\" for a group that didn't take part), starts (each group's start, -1 if it didn't take part), and named ( {name: text} for named groups; m.group(\"name\") reads one)."},{"p":"re","h":"Groups, names and validation","u":"std/re.html#groups-names-and-validation","t":"Use full to check that a whole string has a format; test and search look anywhere in it."},{"p":"re","h":"Replacing and splitting","u":"std/re.html#replacing-and-splitting","t":"In a replacement, $1 is group 1 (one digit; ${12} for more), ${name} a named group, $0 the whole match and $$ a dollar sign."},{"p":"re","h":"Syntax","u":"std/re.html#syntax","t":"The syntax is RE2's, which Go, Rust, Python and JavaScript share for everything below. Text is UTF-8, and matching is by character: . or \u00e0-\u00ff matches \u00e9 (two bytes) as one character. Offsets are in bytes, like everywhere else in Tern. (?i) folds ASCII, Latin-1, Greek and Cyrillic letters. Not supported: backreferences ( \\1 ) and lookaround ( (?=x) , (?!x) , (?<=x) ), because they need a backtracking engine, which can take exponential time; and Unicode classes like \\p{L} . The error says so and suggests what to write instead."},{"p":"re","h":"Searching an index first","u":"std/re.html#searching-an-index-first","t":"re.literals(pattern, min) says which strings every match must contain, for narrowing a search with an index (n-grams, a full-text index) before running the pattern on what's left. It returns groups of literals: a text with a match holds at least one literal of each group. Literals are lowercase and compare ASCII letters without case (so an index that folds case can use them for any pattern); none is shorter than min bytes. means nothing is certain (like \\w+ ), so every text has to be checked. Groups come most selective first. It's the analysis from Russ Cox's code search: alternations and smal"},{"p":"re","h":"Performance","u":"std/re.html#performance","t":"A pattern compiles once per program (the last 1000 are kept) and is shared by every thread. Searching runs a lazy DFA, built as it's needed and kept with the pattern, that reads each byte of the text once; before it, a literal the matches start with ( memchr on its rarest byte), the possible first bytes (16 at a time with simd ), a few literal alternatives (Teddy, also with simd ), or a literal in the middle (an email's @ ) skip to where a match could begin. Groups are then filled in over just the matched text (one pass when the pattern allows, else a bounded backtracker). Time grows with the "},{"p":"simd","h":"simd","u":"std/simd.html#simd","t":"The simd module works on 16 bytes at once. A bytes16 is a value like an int (no allocation), and each operation is one instruction on ARM (NEON), x86 (SSE2/SSSE3) and wasm (SIMD128), or a short loop where there's none: for scanning text, parsing, byte tables and checksums. std re finds where a match may start with it."},{"p":"simd","h":"Functions and operators","u":"std/simd.html#functions-and-operators","t":"simd.lookup is the building block of byte-classification tricks: split each byte into its low and high nibble ( v & simd.splat(15) , v 4 ), look each up in a table of 16, and AND the two; that is how re checks three bytes of a few literal strings at 16 positions in a handful of instructions (Teddy). A bytes16 can be a struct field or an array element and compares with == ; it can't be a map key or go to JSON."},{"p":"simd","h":"Four f32s at once: f32x4","u":"std/simd.html#four-f32s-at-once-f32x4","t":"An f32x4 holds four f32 s (\u00a7 32-bit floats in the reference) and works on them in one instruction: dot products, distances and sums over f32 vectors. Like a bytes16 it is a value (no allocation); + - / work lane by lane, with another f32x4 or with an f32 (which goes to every lane). The lanes are added in a different order than a loop over the elements would, so a sum can differ from one in the last bits. Loads in a loop shaped like the dot product's ( while i + k <= n: or while i < n: , ending in i += step , and loading xs base + i + c where the loop never changes i otherwise, nor n , xs , or "},{"p":"http","h":"http","u":"std/http.html#http","t":"The http module is Tern's HTTP: two servers, a client, and the request and response types they share. http.serve is the one-line server: an HTTP/1.1 event loop on every core that calls one handler function for each request. The engine ( http.new_server ) is the server web frameworks are built on: routes, WebSockets, server-sent events, streamed bodies, static files and HTTPS, on the task runtime. roost (../packages/roost.md), the router and middleware framework, is written on top of it, and its roost.Request and roost.Response are this module's types under another name. This page covers the ty"},{"p":"http","h":"Request","u":"std/http.html#request","t":"- path is the part of the URL before ? , exactly as the client sent it: it is not URL-decoded ( /a%20b stays /a%20b ). http.match and route parameters decode what they capture. - raw_query is the part after ? , also raw, or \"\" if there is none. Read values with req.query(name) . - head holds the header lines as the client sent them. Read them with req.header(name) , which ignores case. - body is the request body (for example, a JSON document or a form), as bytes."},{"p":"http","h":"Reading requests","u":"std/http.html#reading-requests","t":"Call them as methods: req.query(\"page\") . query and form decode + and %XX ; a name that appears more than once gives its first value, and a name with no = (as in ?debug ) is \"\" . to_int reads a number: req.query(\"page\").to_int(1) . Part has name (the form field), filename ( \"\" for a plain field), content_type and data . http.new_request(method, target, body) builds a request the way the servers do, for calling a handler in a test: target is the path with its query string, and add_header adds headers."},{"p":"http","h":"Response","u":"std/http.html#response","t":"The server writes the status line, a Content-Type header from content_type (left out if it is \"\" ), the lines in headers as they are, a Content-Length , and the body. html sends the body as it is: escape user input yourself (see html (html.md)). json 's body must already be JSON text, usually from json.encode : http.json(json.encode(users)) . set_header(\"Content-Type\", ...) sets content_type ; other names add a line to headers ."},{"p":"http","h":"match","u":"std/http.html#match","t":"Routing for serve 's handlers (the engine and roost route by themselves). It matches a path against a pattern such as \"/users/{id}/posts/{post}\" , where each {name} matches one whole path segment. On a match, it returns true and replaces params with the captured values (URL-decoded), keyed by name. If it doesn't match, it returns false and leaves params unchanged. The path and the pattern must have the same number of / -separated segments, so /users/42/ (with a trailing slash) doesn't match /users/{id} . A segment can be empty: /users/ matches /users/{id} with id equal to \"\" , so check capture"},{"p":"http","h":"serve","u":"std/http.html#serve","t":"Listens on port on every network interface (IPv4) and answers requests forever; serve never returns. The handler must be a top-level function (not a lambda) of the shape shown. The compiler generates the glue between the server and your handler, which is why serve has three forms instead of a single signature. It prints tern: serving http://localhost:PORT on N threads to standard error. If the port can't be used (for example, another program has it), it prints tern: bind: Address already in use and the program exits with status 1."},{"p":"http","h":"Sharing state with handlers","u":"std/http.html#sharing-state-with-handlers","t":"The third argument chooses how handlers see your app's data: Form The handler gets Requests run --- --- --- serve(port, handle) nothing but the request fully in parallel serve(port, handle, app) app: App , read-only, shared by all threads fully in parallel serve(port, handle, &app) app: mut App , which it may change one at a time The read-only form is for data loaded once at start-up (configuration, lookup tables, a sqlite.Db ). The mut form lets handlers change shared state safely: the server runs such requests one at a time, so two requests can never race on app . Keep those handlers short; "},{"p":"http","h":"Threads","u":"std/http.html#threads","t":"The server runs an event loop (kqueue on macOS, epoll on Linux and Android) on one thread per core, with keep-alive connections and pipelining. Set TERN_HTTP_THREADS to choose the number of threads. A handler may itself use parallel blocks. A handler runs on the event loop's thread, so it shouldn't wait on tasks or channels; use the engine for that."},{"p":"http","h":"Limits and timeouts","u":"std/http.html#limits-and-timeouts","t":"Limit Default What happens Change with --- --- --- --- Request body size 16 MB 413 Payload Too Large , connection closed TERN_HTTP_MAX_BODY_MB Request line and headers 64 KB 400 Bad Request , connection closed Idle keep-alive connection 60 s connection closed TERN_HTTP_IDLE_SEC Slow client (a request started but not finished) 10 s connection closed TERN_HTTP_HEADER_SEC Malformed requests get 400 Bad Request . The environment variables are read when serve starts, e.g. TERN_HTTP_MAX_BODY_MB=64 ./app . Not supported by serve : TLS (put a proxy such as Caddy or nginx in front, or use the engine's "},{"p":"http","h":"Errors in handlers","u":"std/http.html#errors-in-handlers","t":"A runtime error inside a handler (an index out of range, a missing map key, a division by zero, a failed SQL statement) doesn't stop the server. That request is answered with 500 internal server error , a line is logged to standard error, and the server carries on: With serve(port, handle, &app) , changes a failing handler made to app before the error are kept, so update app at the end of the handler, after the checks that can fail. The servers and the client are not available on WebAssembly (a compile error). The request and response functions work everywhere, so request-handling code can be "},{"p":"http","h":"A complete example","u":"std/http.html#a-complete-example","t":"A guestbook: an HTML form, a form-encoded POST , a JSON API, and state shared between requests with mut : For a database-backed server, see the example at the end of sqlite (../packages/sqlite.md a-complete-example)."},{"p":"http","h":"The engine","u":"std/http.html#the-engine","t":"The engine runs each connection as a task on the task runtime (every core), so a handler may wait (sleep, channels, the client below, a database) without holding up other requests. Handlers are closures fn(http.Request) http.Response , so they can capture the state they need (a task.shared value for state that changes). A runtime error in a handler answers 500 and the server keeps running. s.add_route(method, pattern, h, ws, sse, stream) registers a route; method \" \" matches every method. Patterns: \"/users/{id}\" (one segment, read with req.param(\"id\") ), \"/files/{ rest}\" (the rest of the path)"},{"p":"http","h":"WebSockets and server-sent events","u":"std/http.html#websockets-and-server-sent-events","t":"A WebSocket route answers the handshake with http.ws_upgrade and names its socket handler, which then has the connection to itself; an event-stream route does the same with http.sse_open . Pings are answered and fragmented messages put together for you. wss:// URLs connect over TLS (default port 443) with SNI and certificate verification, like https:// , and need import tls in the program; without it ws_connect fails with \"wss:// needs import tls \". The Host header carries the URL's host and port, and the handshake accepts what CDNs add (extra headers, Connection: upgrade in any case). out.for"},{"p":"http","h":"Streamed bodies and files","u":"std/http.html#streamed-bodies-and-files","t":"A route added with stream set to true runs its handler as soon as the headers arrive, with req.body empty; the handler reads the body itself, a piece at a time, in constant memory and without the body size limit ( Content-Length and chunked bodies both work). http.static_file(dir, rel, req) answers with the file rel under dir : the content type from the extension, ETag and Last-Modified with 304 answers, single byte ranges ( 206 ), and big files streamed from disk. Paths with .. , and dot files other than .well-known , are 404 ; a directory serves its index.html . http.file_response(path, req)"},{"p":"http","h":"The client","u":"std/http.html#the-client","t":"An HTTP/1.1 client for tasks: a request waits without blocking its thread, and keep-alive connections are pooled per host and shared by all tasks. https:// URLs need import tls (certificates are verified; see tls (tls.md)). A request that gets no response at all returns status 0 with the reason in body ; try_get and try_fetch make that an Err , while 4xx and 5xx responses are Ok ."},{"p":"http","h":"For middleware","u":"std/http.html#for-middleware","t":"These are what roost (../packages/roost.md)'s middleware (logging, rate limits, request ids, bearer tokens, gzip, metrics) is made of. A limiter holds a token bucket per key (a client IP, an API key), refilled at per_second up to burst , shared by every thread; idle buckets are forgotten. client_ip is the connection's address unless trust_proxies(n) was called: then it takes the X-Forwarded-For entry the n proxies in front of the server added, counting from the right (entries further left were written by the client)."},{"p":"crypto","h":"crypto","u":"std/crypto.html#crypto","t":"The crypto module has the pieces a web service needs to handle secrets: SHA-256 and SHA-512, HMAC signatures, password hashing with Argon2id, PBKDF2, secure random bytes and numbers, constant-time comparison, hex and base64, and UUIDs (v4 random, v7 time-ordered). It is self-contained C inside the Tern runtime (no OpenSSL), works on every target including WebAssembly, and is compiled into a program only when it imports crypto ."},{"p":"crypto","h":"Bytes in strings","u":"std/crypto.html#bytes-in-strings","t":"Hashes, MACs, keys and random bytes are raw bytes , returned in a str (Tern strings hold any bytes, including zeros). Printing one shows unreadable characters; turn it into text with crypto.hex or crypto.base64 first, and back with crypto.unhex or crypto.base64_decode . len(s) is the number of bytes."},{"p":"crypto","h":"Hashes and MACs","u":"std/crypto.html#hashes-and-macs","t":"SHA-1 is broken for collision resistance: don't use it for signatures or content addressing. HMAC-SHA1 is still sound, and it's what TOTP codes (RFC 6238, every authenticator app) and some older protocols require. SHA-256 uses the CPU's SHA instructions where it has them (Apple silicon and other ARMv8 chips). To check a signature someone sent you, compute the expected MAC and compare with crypto.equal , never == : == stops at the first differing byte, which leaks through timing how much of a forged signature was right."},{"p":"crypto","h":"Passwords","u":"std/crypto.html#passwords","t":"hash_password hashes with Argon2id (RFC 9106), the algorithm OWASP recommends, using a fresh 16-byte random salt and OWASP's cost settings (19 MiB of memory, 2 passes, 1 lane). The result is a PHC string that records the algorithm, the cost and the salt, so it is all you need to store: It is the same format the argon2 libraries of other languages use (Rust's argon2 , Go's alexedwards/argon2id , Python's argon2-cffi ), so hashes move between them. verify_password reads the cost from the string, so hashes made with other settings still verify; it returns false for a wrong password and for a stri"},{"p":"crypto","h":"Key derivation","u":"std/crypto.html#key-derivation","t":"The raw functions, for deriving encryption keys or talking to a protocol that fixes the parameters (PostgreSQL's SCRAM login uses PBKDF2). argon2id needs a salt of at least 8 bytes, t = 1 passes, m_kib KiB of memory (at least 8 p ), and p lanes; the result has length bytes. Bad parameters are a runtime error."},{"p":"crypto","h":"Random numbers","u":"std/crypto.html#random-numbers","t":"From the operating system's secure generator ( arc4random_buf on macOS, iOS, Android and the BSDs, getrandom on Linux, WASI random_get on WebAssembly). Use these, not the random module, for tokens, keys, salts and anything an attacker must not guess. random_int has no modulo bias."},{"p":"crypto","h":"UUIDs","u":"std/crypto.html#uuids","t":"Both return the 36-character text form. Version 7 UUIDs sort in the order they were made (within one millisecond, a thread's UUIDs count up), so they make good database keys: new rows land at the end of the index instead of all over it."},{"p":"crypto","h":"Encryption","u":"std/crypto.html#encryption","t":"seal encrypts with ChaCha20-Poly1305 (RFC 8439), which is authenticated: unseal fails if a single byte of the sealed data was changed, or if the key is wrong, instead of returning garbage. The key is 32 random bytes; keep it outside the data it protects (an environment variable, a secrets manager, a file readable only by the service). The raw forms take your own 12-byte nonce, which must never repeat under the same key (a counter works; seal 's random nonces are safe for billions of messages per key), and additional data that is authenticated but not encrypted (a record id, so a sealed value c"},{"p":"crypto","h":"Comparing secrets","u":"std/crypto.html#comparing-secrets","t":"True when a and b are the same bytes. It takes the same time wherever they differ (only the lengths can be learned from timing), so use it for MACs, tokens and API keys."},{"p":"crypto","h":"Hex and base64","u":"std/crypto.html#hex-and-base64","t":"A decoder given bad input returns \"\" and crypto.error() says why; after a successful call crypto.error() is \"\" (so you can tell a bad input from an empty one)."},{"p":"crypto","h":"With Result","u":"std/crypto.html#with-result","t":"try_unhex(s) , try_base64_decode(s) and try_base64url_decode(s) return Result str, str : key = crypto.try_base64_decode(env_key)? , with errors like \"crypto: base64_decode: not base64\" ."},{"p":"crypto","h":"Performance","u":"std/crypto.html#performance","t":"Single core, Apple M-series, against Rust ( sha2 , hmac , argon2 crates) and Go ( crypto/sha256 , golang.org/x/crypto/argon2 ); the programs are in bench/std/ : Tern Go Rust --- --- --- --- SHA-256, 64 MiB 1937 MB/s 2042 MB/s 1930 MB/s HMAC-SHA256 of 64 bytes 140 ns 416 ns 140 ns Argon2id, m=19 MiB t=2 p=1 ( hash_password ) 20.7 ms 40.5 ms 21.6 ms PBKDF2-SHA256, 600,000 iterations 38.1 ms 68.2 ms 47.2 ms ChaCha20-Poly1305, 1 MiB messages 678 MB/s 1238 MB/s (assembly) 713 MB/s ( chacha20poly1305 )"},{"p":"compress","h":"compress","u":"std/compress.html#compress","t":"The compress module compresses bytes with zstd (https://facebook.github.io/zstd/) (fast to compress, very fast to decompress, and smaller than gzip at the same speed) and with gzip, zlib and raw deflate, for the files and protocols that use them ( .gz files, HTTP Content-Encoding , PNG, zip entries). Both are built into Tern (zstd's official library and libdeflate (https://github.com/ebiggers/libdeflate), vendored), so there is nothing to install, and they're linked into a program only when it imports compress . They work on every target, WebAssembly included."},{"p":"compress","h":"Functions","u":"std/compress.html#functions","t":"Function What it does --- --- compress.zstd(data str, level int) str One zstd frame holding data , with its size in the frame header. compress.unzstd(data str) Result str, str The bytes of every zstd frame in data , in order. compress.unzstd_max(data str, max_out int) Result str, str The same, but Err as soon as the output passes max_out bytes. compress.zstd_bound(n int) int The most bytes compress.zstd of n bytes can produce. compress.gzip(data str, level int) str One gzip member (what a .gz file holds). compress.gunzip(data str) Result str, str The bytes of every gzip member in data , in ord"},{"p":"compress","h":"Levels","u":"std/compress.html#levels","t":"Level 3 is zstd's default and a good choice for most data. Levels 1 to 19 trade speed for size (19 is several times slower to compress than 3 and a little smaller); 20 to 22 use much more memory. Negative levels are faster still and compress less. A level outside zstd's range is clamped to it, and every level decompresses at the same speed."},{"p":"compress","h":"Decompressing","u":"std/compress.html#decompressing","t":"unzstd reads frames whether or not they record their size: the frames the zstd command writes while streaming from a pipe, other libraries' streaming encoders, and several frames one after another (it returns their bytes joined). Input that isn't whole zstd frames gives an Err :"},{"p":"compress","h":"Untrusted input","u":"std/compress.html#untrusted-input","t":"A few bytes of zstd can expand to gigabytes. When the data comes from outside (a request body, an upload), use unzstd_max with the largest result you are willing to hold. It stops as soon as the output passes the limit, before it is all in memory:"},{"p":"compress","h":"Limits","u":"std/compress.html#limits","t":"A str holds up to 4GB, so that is the most either side can be. In a task, a call runs on the task's thread: a very large input holds up that core's other tasks briefly, as a big file read does. Wrap it in task.blocking if that matters."},{"p":"compress","h":"gzip, zlib and deflate","u":"std/compress.html#gzip-zlib-and-deflate","t":"Levels go from 1 (fastest) to 12 (smallest, slowest); 6 is what gzip uses by default and a good choice, and 0 stores the data uncompressed. Out of range is clamped. gunzip reads every member of a file made by joining .gz files ( cat a.gz b.gz ), as gzip -d does, and ignores zeros after the last one; a bad checksum, data cut short, or anything else after the end is an Err . What gzip , zlib and deflate write, other gzip and zlib programs read, and the reverse (the run tests include output from gzip -9 and Python's zlib ). Each thread keeps its compressor between calls (making one costs more tha"},{"p":"compress","h":"Speed","u":"std/compress.html#speed","t":"bench/std compresses ~40 MB of log lines and 2 KB pieces of them, against Go's compress/gzip and Rust's flate2 (its default backend, miniz_oxide, and zlib-rs): Tern Go Rust flate2 Rust flate2 + zlib-rs --- --- --- --- --- gzip, level 1 623 MB/s (7.12 MB) 327 (7.89 MB) 491 (8.43 MB) 536 (9.78 MB) gzip, level 6 227 MB/s (5.55 MB) 89 (5.65 MB) 128 (5.65 MB) 181 (5.94 MB) gunzip 2,936 MB/s 623 963 1,476 gzip of a 2 KB piece 14.3 us 30.7 15.9 18.3 peak memory 89 MB 201 MB 208 MB 214 MB Tern's output is also the smallest at both levels."},{"p":"tls","h":"tls","u":"std/tls.html#tls","t":"The tls module encrypts connections with TLS 1.2 and 1.3. Importing it does three things: tls.connect opens verified TLS connections as ordinary net.Conn values, the HTTP clients ( http.get , http.fetch ) accept https:// URLs, and http.listen_tls (or roost.serve_tls ) serves HTTPS. It uses aws-lc (https://github.com/aws/aws-lc) (the library under rustls), which tern builds and links statically into programs that import tls , and only those, so the program runs on machines without OpenSSL installed. See Where TLS comes from ( where-tls-comes-from). Without import tls , an https:// URL fails wit"},{"p":"tls","h":"Clients","u":"std/tls.html#clients","t":"tls.connect connects, does the TLS handshake, and checks the server's certificate: it must chain to a trusted root and name host (a DNS name or an IP address). Check c.ok() ; when it is false, tls.error() says why (a refused connection, a failed name lookup, certificate verify failed: hostname mismatch , ...). The connection is an ordinary net.Conn , so c.read , c.read_line , c.read_exact , c.write and c.close encrypt and decrypt, and like every socket wait, a handshake or a read waits only in its task. tls.upgrade starts TLS on a connection that is already open, for protocols that begin in pl"},{"p":"tls","h":"Servers","u":"std/tls.html#servers","t":"cert is a PEM file with the server's certificate followed by any intermediate certificates (Let's Encrypt's fullchain.pem ), and key is its private key ( privkey.pem ). A file that can't be loaded, or a key that doesn't match the certificate, is a runtime error at startup. Each connection's handshake runs in the connection's task. For a TLS server that isn't HTTP, accept plain connections and call tls.accept in each connection's task: For development, mkcert localhost or openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -nodes -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem -days 365 -subj /CN=local"},{"p":"tls","h":"ALPN: choosing the protocol","u":"std/tls.html#alpn-choosing-the-protocol","t":"Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation lets the client and server agree, during the handshake, on what they will speak over the connection: \"h2\" for HTTP/2 (which gRPC over TLS requires), \"http/1.1\" , or any other registered name. tls.set_alpn gives a server the protocols it speaks, preferred first; a client gets the first of them that it offered. A client that offers only other protocols fails the handshake (the no_application_protocol alert, as RFC 7301 says), and one that offers none gets none: tls.alpn(c) is then \"\" , and the server decides whether to go on. tls.connect_alpn connects like "},{"p":"tls","h":"Performance","u":"std/tls.html#performance","t":"A one-thread HTTPS server on Linux (Docker, arm64, 4 CPUs), with the same ECDSA P-256 certificate and TLS 1.3 with the hybrid post-quantum X25519MLKEM768 key exchange, against Go ( net/http ) and Rust (axum + rustls on aws-lc-rs). Method and machine load are in bench/std/results.md : server, 1 thread Tern (aws-lc) Tern ( TERN_TLS=system , OpenSSL 3.0) Rust (rustls) Go --- --- --- --- --- keep-alive requests/s 272,576 243,566 239,753 158,784 CPU per keep-alive request 3.2 us 3.7 us 3.7 us 5.8 us full handshakes/s 8,115 6,773 7,232 5,968 CPU per full handshake 80 us 146 us 89 us 165 us p99 at 10"},{"p":"tls","h":"Where TLS comes from","u":"std/tls.html#where-tls-comes-from","t":"The first build that imports tls for a target downloads aws-lc and compiles it, once, into the build cache ( ~/.cache/tern/aws-lc-<version /<target / ); later builds reuse it. With git installed, the download is a shallow sparse clone of the pinned release tag that fetches only the files the build needs (about 8 MB, 41 MB on disk) and is checked against the tag's pinned commit; without git , tern downloads the release tarball (about 130 MB) with curl and checks its pinned SHA-256. Either way tern prints a line when it starts: Compiling takes 20 to 60 seconds per target, with the same C compile"},{"p":"tls","h":"Licenses","u":"std/tls.html#licenses","t":"A program that imports tls links aws-lc statically; programs that don't contain none of it. aws-lc is available under the Apache License 2.0 or the ISC license, with code inherited from BoringSSL and OpenSSL under the ISC, OpenSSL and SSLeay licenses, and small parts under MIT, BSD-3-Clause and MIT-0. The full texts are in aws-lc's LICENSE and NOTICE files, in the source tree tern unpacks under ~/.cache/tern/aws-lc-<version / . When you distribute such a binary, include those license texts with it (in its documentation or an \"open source licenses\" screen, for example). The OpenSSL and SSLeay l"},{"p":"tls","h":"Binary size","u":"std/tls.html#binary-size","t":"aws-lc adds about 2 MB to a program that imports tls : a minimal HTTPS GET is 2.3 MB on macOS (2.1 MB stripped) and 2.5 MB on Linux arm64, with no dependency beyond libc. tern compiles aws-lc with each function in its own section and links with dead-code removal, so only the parts the program uses stay in."},{"p":"log","h":"log","u":"std/log.html#log","t":"The log module writes leveled, structured log lines to stderr: one line per call, in logfmt (the default), JSON, or a plain text format for people. Fields are a {str: str} map, so a line carries the values a log search needs, not just a sentence. On stderr that prints:"},{"p":"log","h":"Functions","u":"std/log.html#functions","t":"set_level and set_format return false for a name they don't know (and change nothing). The environment variables TERN_LOG_LEVEL and TERN_LOG_FORMAT set the same things without changing the program, which is how you'd turn on debug logs in production for a while. A call below the level returns right away without formatting its fields."},{"p":"log","h":"Formats","u":"std/log.html#formats","t":"Times are UTC with milliseconds. In logfmt, values with spaces, quotes or = are quoted and escaped. Field values are strings ( str(n) for numbers), so JSON output has them as JSON strings. Each line is a single write , so lines from different threads and tasks never interleave. For per-request lines in a web server, roost.logger() middleware does the access log; use log inside handlers for everything else."},{"p":"log","h":"Recent records","u":"std/log.html#recent-records","t":"Whatever the output level, the last 100 records at warn and above stay in memory, so an error reporter can attach what led up to a failure (the Sentry package sends them as breadcrumbs). Record has time (seconds since 1970), level , msg and fields ."},{"p":"html","h":"html","u":"std/html.html#html","t":"The html module escapes text for HTML and renders templates that escape by default: a value printed with {{name}} can't inject markup, however it was typed. A template renders a JSON value, the output of json.encode , so any struct, map or array can feed one."},{"p":"html","h":"Functions","u":"std/html.html#functions","t":"render_file reads a template file, parses it once and keeps it (a changed file is read again), and lets it include others with {{ header.html}} , relative to its own directory. A template with a mistake (an unclosed block, {{else}} outside {{ if}} , a missing file) renders as \"\" , and html.error() says what and where ( \"line 3: {{ ...}} is never closed\" ); after a success it's \"\" ."},{"p":"html","h":"Templates","u":"std/html.html#templates","t":"Tag Does --- --- {{name}} , {{user.name}} the value, HTML-escaped; null or a missing name prints nothing {{.}} the current element inside {{ each}} {{{name}}} the value as it is: only for HTML you made or trust {{json name}} the value as JSON, for <script (with < , , & escaped as < ...) {{ each items}} ... {{/each}} the block once per array element (or object value); {{@index}} counts from 0 {{ if x}} ... {{else}} ... {{/if}} x counts as false if it's false , null , 0 , \"\" , or {} {{ unless x}} ... {{/unless}} the opposite {{ file.html}} another template file (with render_file ) {{! note }} a "},{"p":"html","h":"With Result","u":"std/html.html#with-result","t":"try_render(template, data) and try_render_file(path, data) return Result str, str , so a handler can write page = html.try_render_file(path, json.encode(v))? in a helper that returns a Result, and map the Err ( \"html: render failed: line 3: ...\" ) to a 500."},{"p":"csv","h":"csv","u":"std/csv.html#csv","t":"The csv module reads and writes comma-separated values as RFC 4180 describes them, with the same rules as Go's encoding/csv . A field in double quotes may hold commas, line breaks and \"\" (one quote); \\r\\n line ends read as \\n ; empty lines are skipped. Every field is a str : convert numbers with to_int / to_float when you read them and str(n) when you write them."},{"p":"csv","h":"Functions","u":"std/csv.html#functions","t":""},{"p":"csv","h":"Records by column name","u":"std/csv.html#records-by-column-name","t":"records uses the first row as the header, which is what most exported spreadsheets have:"},{"p":"csv","h":"Big files, one record at a time","u":"std/csv.html#big-files-one-record-at-a-time","t":"parse builds every row. To go through a big file without that, use a reader: r.next(&row) puts the next record in row and returns false at the end, or at an error, which r.err then names (it's \"\" after a clean end). r.line is the line the next record starts on."},{"p":"csv","h":"Options","u":"std/csv.html#options","t":"csv.options() returns the defaults; change the fields you need and pass them to a _with function: Field Default Does --- --- --- sep \",\" the field separator, one byte ( csv.tsv() sets \"\\t\" ) comment \"\" lines starting with this byte are skipped fields 0 fields per record: 0 as many as the first record, n exactly n, -1 any number lazy_quotes false a \" inside an unquoted field, or a lone \" in a quoted one, is kept as text trim_leading_space false spaces, tabs, \\r , \\v and \\f before each field are dropped crlf false format ends lines with \\r\\n"},{"p":"csv","h":"Errors","u":"std/csv.html#errors","t":"Malformed input is an Err naming the line and column (1-based, in bytes), never a partial result:"},{"p":"csv","h":"Writing","u":"std/csv.html#writing","t":"format quotes a field only when it must: when it holds the separator, a quote, \\r or \\n , or starts with a space or tab. Quotes inside are doubled. A record that is one empty field is written as \"\" , so it reads back as a record and not as a skipped empty line. What format writes, parse reads back to the same rows."},{"p":"csv","h":"Speed","u":"std/csv.html#speed","t":"bench/std compares it with Go's encoding/csv and Rust's csv crate on 40 MB of 8-field rows, with and without quoted fields: Tern is the fastest of the three at writing, at reading one record at a time (1.1-1.4x Rust, 2-2.6x Go) and at parse (3.4-4.4x Rust, which allocates every field, and about 2x Go). A reader allocates nothing per record once its row's strings are big enough. The numbers are in bench/std/results.md ."},{"p":"Packages","h":"Packages","u":"packages/index.html#packages","t":"These are Tern's first-party packages: maintained with the language, but not part of the standard library (../std/index.md). A program imports one by name, like a standard module ( import roost ), with nothing to install; they ship in the checkout under library/NAME/ . They are written the way any third-party package (../tour/12-modules.md) is, and roost, openapi and sentry are planned to move to repositories of their own, fetched with tern add . Package What it's for --- --- roost (roost.md) A web framework on the http engine: routing, middleware, WebSockets, SSE, static files, uploads, valid"},{"p":"roost","h":"roost","u":"packages/roost.html#roost","t":"The roost module is an axum-style web framework on Tern's tasks: a router with path parameters, middleware, WebSockets, server-sent events, and (covered on this page) static files, file uploads, streamed request bodies, HTTPS, connection limits and graceful shutdown. It is plain Tern on the server engine in http (../std/http.md): roost.Request and roost.Response are http.Request and http.Response under another name, so the request and response functions on that page work on them too. Every function is listed at the top of library/roost/roost.tn ; the reference (../reference.md) has the one-lin"},{"p":"roost","h":"Static files","u":"packages/roost.html#static-files","t":"r.files(\"/assets\", \"./public\") answers GET /assets/css/app.css with ./public/css/app.css (and GET /assets/ with ./public/index.html ). It sets Content-Type from the file extension, sends ETag and Last-Modified and answers 304 Not Modified to a client that already has the file, serves single byte ranges ( 206 , for video seeking and resumed downloads), and streams big files from disk instead of reading them into memory. Paths containing .. , and dot files other than .well-known , are 404 , so a request can't reach outside dir or read .env . roost.file_response(path, req) sends one file from a h"},{"p":"roost","h":"File uploads","u":"packages/roost.html#file-uploads","t":"An HTML form with enctype=\"multipart/form-data\" (any form with a file input) sends its fields as parts. req.parts() returns them all; Part has name (the form field), filename ( \"\" for a plain field), content_type and data . These read the request's buffered body, which is limited to TERN_WEB_MAX_BODY_MB (16 MB by default)."},{"p":"roost","h":"Streamed request bodies","u":"packages/roost.html#streamed-request-bodies","t":"For uploads of any size in constant memory, register the route with post_stream (or put_stream ). Its handler runs as soon as the request's headers arrive, with req.body empty, and reads the body itself, a piece at a time; Content-Length and chunked bodies both work, and the size limit doesn't apply. Whatever the handler doesn't read is skipped before the next request on the connection. Read the body in the handler's own task (not in a task it spawns)."},{"p":"roost","h":"Validating input","u":"packages/roost.html#validating-input","t":"A Check collects every problem with a request (each field keeps its first), so the client learns about all of them at once; v.problems() answers 422 with {\"errors\": {\"name\": \"is required\", \"age\": \"must be between 13 and 120\"}} ."},{"p":"roost","h":"Rate limiting and client addresses","u":"packages/roost.html#rate-limiting-and-client-addresses","t":"roost.rate_limit(5.0, 20) gives each client IP a token bucket that holds 20 requests and refills at 5 a second; past it, requests get 429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After . rate_limit_by buckets by your own key instead (an API key header, a user id). Buckets are shared by all threads and idle ones are forgotten. req.client_ip() is the connection's address. Behind a load balancer or reverse proxy, call roost.trust_proxies(n) with the number of proxies in front of the server: client_ip then takes the X-Forwarded-For entry those proxies added, counting n from the right. The entries further left"},{"p":"roost","h":"Metrics","u":"packages/roost.html#metrics","t":"r.metrics(\"/metrics\") serves Prometheus' text format: tern_http_requests_total and a tern_http_request_duration_seconds histogram by route pattern, method and status, tern_http_connections , and the process's CPU seconds, peak memory and uptime, plus whatever the program records with roost.metric_add(\"jobs_total\", \"queue=\\\"email\\\"\", 1.0) and roost.metric_set(\"queue_depth\", \"\", 42.0) . Counting starts when a router asks for metrics, and costs a timer read and a short lock per request."},{"p":"roost","h":"HTTPS","u":"packages/roost.html#https","t":"With import tls , a server can speak HTTPS: cert is the PEM certificate chain and key its private key. See tls (../std/tls.md), which also covers https:// URLs in the client ( http.get )."},{"p":"roost","h":"Connection limits and shutdown","u":"packages/roost.html#connection-limits-and-shutdown","t":"r.max_connections(n) (or TERN_WEB_MAX_CONNS=n ) caps the connections open at once; at the cap the server stops accepting until one closes, and new clients wait in the kernel's queue instead of being refused. roost.serve shuts down gracefully on SIGINT (Ctrl-C) and SIGTERM (what kill , systemd and container runtimes send): it stops accepting, closes idle keep-alive connections, lets the requests in progress finish for up to TERN_WEB_DRAIN_SEC seconds (default 10; a second signal cuts it short), then returns, so code after roost.serve runs and the program exits 0."},{"p":"sqlite","h":"sqlite","u":"packages/sqlite.html#sqlite","t":"The sqlite package uses SQLite, the embedded SQL database, through the system's SQLite library. You open a database file, run statements with exec , and read rows either as maps of strings with query or straight into your own structs with query_into . This page covers the four functions, how parameters are bound, what happens on errors, and how connections work across threads and web requests. SQLite is linked only into programs that import sqlite . It is available on macOS, Linux, and iOS, but not on Android (the NDK doesn't ship it) or WebAssembly; importing sqlite there is a compile error."},{"p":"sqlite","h":"The Db type","u":"packages/sqlite.html#the-db-type","t":"A sqlite.Db names an open database. It is a small handle, so copying it is free and every copy refers to the same database. That is also why a read-only parameter d: sqlite.Db can still run INSERT s: the database lives outside the program's values, and \"read-only\" is about Tern values only. All four functions can be called with method syntax: d.exec(...) is sqlite.exec(d, ...) ."},{"p":"sqlite","h":"open","u":"packages/sqlite.html#open","t":"Opens the SQLite database file at path , creating it if it doesn't exist. If it can't be opened (for example, the directory doesn't exist), the program stops with a runtime error. - File databases are switched to SQLite's WAL mode, which lets readers and a writer work at the same time. SQLite then keeps two helper files next to the database, name-wal and name-shm ; leave them alone. - \":memory:\" opens a temporary in-memory database. Each thread gets its own, separate in-memory database (see Threads ( threads-and-connections) below), so use a file whenever parallel code or a web server needs to"},{"p":"sqlite","h":"exec","u":"packages/sqlite.html#exec","t":"Runs one SQL statement ( CREATE , INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE , BEGIN , ...) and returns the row id of the most recent successful INSERT on this connection. After an INSERT into a table with an INTEGER PRIMARY KEY , that is the new row's id. For other statements the value is left over from an earlier insert (or 0 ), so ignore it. sql may hold several statements separated by ; (a schema, say), and they run in order. With ? parameters, write one statement per call: SQLite would bind the parameters to the first statement only, so that is a runtime error. ( query and query_into take exactly one state"},{"p":"sqlite","h":"query","u":"packages/sqlite.html#query","t":"Runs a SELECT (or any statement that returns rows) and returns every row as a map from column name to value, as text. Numbers come back as their text ( \"42\" , \"9.5\" ), and NULL comes back as \"\" . Each map keeps the columns in the order of the SELECT . Use AS to name computed columns; if two columns have the same name, the last one wins. query is the quick way to look at data. For real code, query_into is faster (no maps, no string conversion) and gives you typed values."},{"p":"sqlite","h":"query_into","u":"packages/sqlite.html#query_into","t":"Runs a query and reads the rows straight into structs. out is a struct, or an array of structs, whose fields are all int , float , bool , str , or an enum without payloads, or an Option of one; pass it with & . An enum field reads a text column by variant name, loosely ( in_progress fills InProgress ); text naming no variant is a runtime error. Columns are matched to fields by name (use AS to rename), columns with no matching field are ignored, and a field with no matching column is left alone. NULL reads as 0 , 0.0 , false , or \"\" , and as None in an Option field. Returns the number of rows t"},{"p":"sqlite","h":"Parameters","u":"packages/sqlite.html#parameters","t":"Every function takes the statement's parameters as a str , one string per ? in the SQL, in order. Parameters are always bound by SQLite, never pasted into the SQL text, so user input can't change the statement (no SQL injection). Build the SQL from constant text only, and pass every value as a parameter. - Convert other values with str(...) : str(id), str(price) , and \"1\" / \"0\" for booleans. - A string that is a plain whole number ( \"42\" , \"-7\" ; not \"007\" or \"4.0\" ) is bound as an integer, other strings as text. SQLite then converts by the column's type: \"9.99\" stored in a REAL column is a nu"},{"p":"sqlite","h":"Errors","u":"packages/sqlite.html#errors","t":"A statement that SQLite rejects (a syntax error, a missing table, a violated UNIQUE or NOT NULL constraint, a locked database) is a runtime error: the program prints runtime error: sqlite: <SQLite's message to standard error and exits with status 2. Inside a web request, the same error ends only that request, which gets a 500 internal server error response while the server keeps running (see http (../std/http.md errors-in-handlers)). So in a server, a constraint failure can't take the site down, but it can't be caught and answered with a nicer message either. When you expect a conflict, check "},{"p":"sqlite","h":"describe","u":"packages/sqlite.html#describe","t":"What SQLite knows about a statement without running it: its parameters ( params : their names as written, \"@id\" , \":name\" , \"?1\" , or \"\" for a plain ? ) and its result columns ( columns : name , decl_type , table , column , not_null ). decl_type is the type the table declares, \"\" for an expression; not_null is true for a column declared NOT NULL (and an INTEGER PRIMARY KEY ). It is what sqlgen (sqlgen.md) reads to generate typed functions."},{"p":"sqlite","h":"Threads and connections","u":"packages/sqlite.html#threads-and-connections","t":"A Db can be used from any thread: each thread lazily opens its own connection to the database file and keeps its own cache of prepared statements (up to 64), so parallel tasks and server threads run queries at the same time without locks in Tern. SQLite itself lets many readers work at once, and one writer at a time. Two consequences: - Transactions ( BEGIN ... COMMIT ) belong to one thread's connection. Start and finish a transaction in the same function, not across a parallel block. - \":memory:\" databases are per connection, so each thread sees its own empty database. Inside a web request, r"},{"p":"sqlite","h":"Many writers at once","u":"packages/sqlite.html#many-writers-at-once","t":"SQLite commits one write transaction at a time, and each commit costs far more than the statement itself. So a single INSERT , UPDATE , or DELETE run from a task (every web request is one), outside an explicit transaction, joins its thread's write batch : the first such write opens a transaction, the thread's other tasks that are ready to run add their writes to it, and it commits once for all of them. Threads' batches take turns in memory, not by polling SQLite's lock. Each statement still returns only after its batch has committed, so it behaves exactly like its own transaction: its rows are"},{"p":"sqlite","h":"Writes that may wait: exec_later and flush","u":"packages/sqlite.html#writes-that-may-wait-exec_later-and-flush","t":"Some writes don't need to be saved before the response: a click count, a view counter, a \"last seen\" time. d.exec_later(sql, params) queues such a write and returns at once; a background thread commits everything queued, in order and in one transaction, every 50 milliseconds ( TERN_DB_LATER_MS changes that), and once more when the program exits. The request doesn't wait for SQLite at all, which makes it the fastest way to write on a hot path. The price: if the process crashes (or is killed with SIGKILL ), the writes of the last few milliseconds are lost. A queued write that fails (a constraint"},{"p":"sqlite","h":"A complete example","u":"packages/sqlite.html#a-complete-example","t":"A JSON API for bookmarks, backed by a database file (build it with tern build bookmarks.tn -o bookmarks ): Try it with curl :"},{"p":"pg","h":"pg","u":"packages/pg.html#pg","t":"The pg module is a PostgreSQL client for tasks: a pool of connections that any number of tasks share, parameters sent separately from the SQL (no injection), rows decoded into your structs through JSON, and transactions that belong to the task that started them. It speaks the PostgreSQL protocol directly (no libpq), logs in with SCRAM-SHA-256, and can use TLS."},{"p":"pg","h":"Connecting","u":"packages/pg.html#connecting","t":"The URL is postgres://user:password@host:port/database , with ?sslmode=require (TLS) or ?sslmode=verify-full (TLS, and the server's certificate must match the host) if the program also does import tls . Characters like @ in a password are written %40 . ?application_name=NAME names the connections in pg_stat_activity (default tern ). A pool holds up to size connections. The first one opens right away, so p.ok() tells you whether the URL and password work (and pg.error() why not); the rest open when enough tasks need them at once. A task that finds every connection busy waits (only that task) un"},{"p":"pg","h":"Queries","u":"packages/pg.html#queries","t":"Parameters are $1 , $2 , ... in the SQL and strings in params ; PostgreSQL converts each to its column's type ( \"42\" to an int, \"true\" to a bool), and pg.NULL is SQL NULL . Every statement is prepared the first time a connection runs it and reused after that, so a repeated query costs one round trip. - exec returns the number of rows the statement changed (or returned, for a SELECT), or -1 if it failed. - query returns the rows as maps with every value as text; NULL is \"\" . - query_json returns the rows as a JSON array with typed values: integers, floats and numerics as numbers, booleans as tr"},{"p":"pg","h":"Typed rows with Result","u":"packages/pg.html#typed-rows-with-result","t":"fetch decodes the rows straight into your struct type, with code the compiler writes for it (no JSON in between, so it's the fastest way to read rows). Columns are matched to fields by name; other columns are ignored, and a field without a column is zero ( None for an Option ). A NULL is None in an Option field and zero in any other. int , float , and bool fields read the column's text as a number or t / f (a float field also reads a timestamptz , timestamp or date as seconds since 1970, UTC), an enum field (one without payloads) reads a text or enum column by variant name, loosely ( in_progre"},{"p":"pg","h":"Describing a statement","u":"packages/pg.html#describing-a-statement","t":"What the server knows about a statement without running it (a Parse and a Describe): the types of its parameters ( params , like \"int8\", \"text\" ) and of its result columns ( columns : name , type_name such as \"int8\" , \"text\" , \"_int4\" for int4 , type_oid , kind ( \"b\" base, \"e\" enum, \"d\" domain), base_type for a domain, table , and not_null , true for a table column declared NOT NULL ). A mistake in the SQL is an Err with the server's message. sqlgen (sqlgen.md) uses it to generate typed functions from your queries. Rows decode into structs with Option fields: a NULL column is None ."},{"p":"pg","h":"Transactions","u":"packages/pg.html#transactions","t":"A transaction belongs to the task that starts it. From begin (or any statement that leaves the connection inside a transaction, such as exec(\"BEGIN\", ) ) until commit or rollback , that task's statements all run on the same connection, and no other task can use it, even while the task waits on a channel or a socket in the middle. Other tasks keep using the rest of the pool."},{"p":"pg","h":"Performance","u":"packages/pg.html#performance","t":"One client thread, 256 tasks sharing 16 connections, against PostgreSQL 17 on the same machine, TechEmpower-style (see bench/std/results.md for the method and machine load): requests/s Tern pg Rust tokio-postgres Go pgx Rust sqlx --- --- --- --- --- one query 165,732 153,343 131,983 50,958 20 queries 8,132 7,527 6,402 2,518 20 queries + 20 updates 3,424 3,069 2,917 1,234 client CPU per statement 6.2 us 6.7 us 7.9 us 19.8 us 100 rows into structs ( fetch ) 101,180 61,697 29,839 20,892 fetch decodes rows with code made for the struct, and once a statement is prepared on a connection it asks for "},{"p":"redis","h":"redis","u":"packages/redis.html#redis","t":"The redis package is a Redis (and Valkey) client for tasks: a pool of connections that every task shares, the everyday commands as functions, any other command through cmd , pipelines (several commands in one round trip), and publish / subscribe. It is written in Tern on top of net ."},{"p":"redis","h":"Connecting","u":"packages/redis.html#connecting","t":"The URL is redis:// :password@ host :port /db (port 6379 and database 0 by default), or rediss://... for TLS, which needs import tls and checks the server's certificate (use tls.trust(ca_file) for a private CA, as managed Redis services often have). The first connection opens right away so c.ok() reports a wrong address or password; the others open when enough tasks need them at once, and a connection that breaks is replaced on its next use. Each task keeps to one of the pool's connections, so the commands of tasks woken by one batch of replies go out together in one write. A Client is a handl"},{"p":"redis","h":"Commands","u":"packages/redis.html#commands","t":"( get and set are builtin names in Tern, hence get_str and set_str .) Every other command goes through cmd , which returns a Reply : kind is \"str\" , \"int\" , \"array\" , \"nil\" or \"error\" , with the value in text , num or items :"},{"p":"redis","h":"Publish / subscribe","u":"packages/redis.html#publish--subscribe","t":"A subscription has a connection of its own. Channel names with or ? are patterns ( PSUBSCRIBE ). sub.next(&m) waits (only its task) for the next message, whose fields are channel and data , and returns false once the subscription is closed or its connection drops."},{"p":"redis","h":"With Result","u":"packages/redis.html#with-result","t":"try_get tells a missing key ( Ok(None) ) from an empty value ( Ok(Some(\"\")) ) and from a failure ( Err(\"redis: connection lost\") ); try_cmd turns an error reply into Err(\"redis: command failed: WRONGTYPE ...\") ."},{"p":"redis","h":"Performance","u":"packages/redis.html#performance","t":"See bench/std/results.md ( tern run bench/std/run.tn -- redis , against the Rust redis crate and Go's go-redis )."},{"p":"migrate","h":"migrate","u":"packages/migrate.html#migrate","t":"The migrate package keeps a PostgreSQL or SQLite database's schema in step with your code. You write the changes as numbered .sql files, and migrate applies the ones the database doesn't have yet, in order, each in a transaction. It records what it applied in a tern_migrations table. It is a first-party package: import migrate , nothing to install."},{"p":"migrate","h":"Migration files","u":"packages/migrate.html#migration-files","t":"A migrations directory holds one file per change, named VERSION_NAME.sql : They run in version order. Other files (a README) are ignored; a .sql file without a version, or two files with the same version, is an error that names them. A file is the SQL that applies the change. Marker lines split it into parts: Marker Meaning --- --- -- migrate:up (optional) the SQL that applies the migration starts here -- migrate:down the SQL that undoes it starts here; without one, down refuses to roll it back -- migrate:no-transaction run it outside a transaction (see below) If you come from golang-migrate, "},{"p":"migrate","h":"Running migrations","u":"packages/migrate.html#running-migrations","t":"Each returns the migrations it ran, in the order it ran them. up is what an app calls as it starts; it does nothing when the database is current: A Status has version , name , applied_at ( \"2026-09-28T12:00:00Z\" , or \"\" when pending), and state : State Meaning --- --- applied in the database, and the file is unchanged pending not applied yet; up will apply it changed applied, but the file's up SQL is different now; up refuses to run missing applied, but there is no file for it (older code, or a deleted file)"},{"p":"migrate","h":"What it guarantees","u":"packages/migrate.html#what-it-guarantees","t":"- All or nothing. A migration and its row in tern_migrations commit together. If a statement fails, the transaction rolls back, the error names the migration and gives the database's message ( migrate: 0003_add_posts failed: ERROR 42P01: relation \"user\" does not exist ), and up stops there. PostgreSQL rolls back schema changes too. SQLite does as well, but some PRAGMAs ( foreign_keys ) do nothing inside a transaction. - One run at a time. On PostgreSQL, a run holds an advisory lock on a connection of its own, so every instance of an app can call up as it starts: one applies the migrations and "},{"p":"migrate","h":"Outside a transaction","u":"packages/migrate.html#outside-a-transaction","t":"A few statements refuse to run in a transaction: CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY , DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY , VACUUM , ALTER SYSTEM . Mark their file: Its statements run one at a time, and it is recorded after the last one succeeds. If one fails, the ones before it stay done, so keep one statement per file ( IF NOT EXISTS helps a retry). A CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY that fails leaves an invalid index behind; drop it before running again."},{"p":"migrate","h":"The migrate command","u":"packages/migrate.html#the-migrate-command","t":"tern migrate works on ./migrations (or --dir DIR ) and the database given by --db URL or $DATABASE_URL : It is migrate.command(dir, url, args) , which a program of your own can call too ( migrate.cli(dir, url) takes the program's arguments): examples/sql/ in the Tern repository has two migrations, plus queries turned into typed functions by sqlgen (sqlgen.md)."},{"p":"migrate","h":"Migrations in code","u":"packages/migrate.html#migrations-in-code","t":"The directory is read when the program runs, so ship it next to the binary. To build migrations some other way, make them with parse and run them with the functions that take a list: A Migration has version , name , up , down ( \"\" if none), transaction , and checksum (the up SQL's SHA-256, in hex). split_statements splits a script at the semicolons that end statements, leaving alone the ones in quotes, $$ function bodies, and comments. Not available on WebAssembly (like sqlite and pg ), or on Android (no SQLite in the NDK). To call your queries as typed functions checked against the migrated s"},{"p":"sqlgen","h":"sqlgen","u":"packages/sqlgen.html#sqlgen","t":"The sqlgen package turns SQL queries into typed Tern functions, like Go's sqlc. You write each query in plain SQL under a -- name: line. sqlgen asks PostgreSQL or SQLite for the types of its parameters and columns, and writes a module with a struct per result and a function per query. A misspelled column, a missing parameter, or a nullable column read as if it couldn't be NULL then fails when you generate or compile, not when the query runs in production. It is a first-party package: import sqlgen , nothing to install. It pairs with migrate (migrate.md): point it at your migrations and it chec"},{"p":"sqlgen","h":"Queries","u":"packages/sqlgen.html#queries","t":"After the name Generated function returns (PostgreSQL) (SQLite) --- --- --- :one Result Option Row , str : the first row, or None Option Row :many Result Row , str Row :exec Result int, str : the rows changed int - Comment lines right under the -- name: line become the function's comment. - Parameters are @name ; using a name twice passes one value. Numbered parameters work too ( $1 in PostgreSQL, ?1 or ? in SQLite) and are called arg1 , arg2 , ... - A query that returns one column gives that column's values ( Option int , str ) instead of a one-field struct. - A query that returns every colum"},{"p":"sqlgen","h":"Types and NULL","u":"packages/sqlgen.html#types-and-null","t":"PostgreSQL SQLite (declared type) Tern --- --- --- int2 , int4 , int8 , oid contains INT int float4 , float8 , numeric REAL , FLOAT , DOUBLE , NUMERIC , DECIMAL float bool contains BOOL bool timestamptz , timestamp float : seconds since 1970, UTC, like time.now() (parameters too) an enum type ( CREATE TYPE mood AS ENUM ... ) a Tern enum sqlgen writes for it (parameters too) int8 , text , ... (arrays of the types above) int , str , ... (parameters too) everything else: text , date , uuid , json , enums everything else (SQLite timestamps too) str (as the database writes it) A column is an Option"},{"p":"sqlgen","h":"Enums","u":"packages/sqlgen.html#enums","t":"A PostgreSQL enum type becomes a Tern enum in the generated module, with a function giving each variant's label: Columns of the type read into it ( Option OrderStatus when they can be NULL ), and parameters of the type take it: orders_with(pool, orders_db.OrderStatus.Shipped) . A match on it must cover every variant, so adding a label and regenerating shows every place that needs to handle it. Labels become variant names by capitalizing their words ( in_progress , in progress and in-progress are all InProgress ); a label with other characters ( a.b ) or starting with a digit is an error that n"},{"p":"sqlgen","h":"What it writes","u":"packages/sqlgen.html#what-it-writes","t":"For PostgreSQL, the same queries become functions on a pg.Pool that return Result s:"},{"p":"sqlgen","h":"Running it","u":"packages/sqlgen.html#running-it","t":"tern sqlgen is the command. The database is --db URL , or $DATABASE_URL : The same command is a function too, for a build script of your own: sqlgen.command(url, \"queries.sql\", \"-o\", \"users_db.tn\" ) , or sqlgen.cli(url) to take the arguments from the program's. - Without --migrations , the queries are checked against the database as it is. - With --migrations DIR , sqlgen creates a scratch database, applies the migrations with migrate (migrate.md), generates, and drops it. For PostgreSQL this needs permission to create databases; for SQLite it's a temporary file. Nothing needs a schema set up "},{"p":"sqlgen","h":"Functions","u":"packages/sqlgen.html#functions","t":"Errors name the file, line, and query:"},{"p":"auth","h":"auth","u":"packages/auth.html#auth","t":"The auth package signs and checks JSON Web Tokens (HS256) and keeps sessions in signed cookies, with a middleware that guards web routes. It's a package of its own ( import auth ) so web programs that don't need it don't carry the crypto code. For password hashing use crypto (../std/crypto.md)'s hash_password and verify_password ."},{"p":"auth","h":"JSON Web Tokens","u":"packages/auth.html#json-web-tokens","t":"jwt_for makes a token whose payload is {\"sub\": subject, \"iat\": now, \"exp\": now + ttl} ; jwt_encode signs any JSON object you give it. jwt_verify returns a Token with ok , payload (the JSON: json.decode it into your own struct for custom claims), subject (the sub claim) and error ( \"bad signature\" , \"expired\" , \"not valid yet\" , \"unsupported algorithm (only HS256)\" , \"malformed token\" ). The signature is compared in constant time, and a token claiming any algorithm other than HS256 (including none ) is rejected. Tokens interoperate with other JWT libraries. auth.require_jwt(secret) is middlewar"},{"p":"auth","h":"Sessions","u":"packages/auth.html#sessions","t":"The session lives in a cookie named session : the data as JSON, base64url-encoded and signed with HMAC-SHA256, set HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax for 30 days ( set_session_for picks the lifetime and adds attributes, such as Secure behind HTTPS). session returns {} if there is no cookie, or it was changed, or it has expired. The user can read the data but not change it, so store ids and flags, not secrets, and keep it small (browsers keep about 4 KB per cookie). There's no server-side store, so ending a session means clearing the cookie."},{"p":"openapi","h":"openapi","u":"packages/openapi.html#openapi","t":"Describe a web API with Tern types, and get an OpenAPI 3.1 (https://spec.openapis.org/oas/v3.1.0) document, Swagger UI, and a typed TypeScript client from the routes you register. Schemas come from json.schema T () (../std/json.md schema), so they always match what json.encode writes. openapi.add registers the route on the router (like r.route(method, path, handler) ) and records the operation; openapi.serve adds GET /openapi.json and GET /docs (Swagger UI, loaded from jsDelivr), so call it after the last add ."},{"p":"openapi","h":"Describing an operation","u":"packages/openapi.html#describing-an-operation","t":"openapi.op(method, path, summary) starts one; each of these returns the changed Op , so calls chain: --- --- o.returns T () the response is JSON of type T o.accepts T () the request body is JSON of type T o.returns_text() the response is text/plain o.status(code) the success status (default 200) o.query(name, type, description) a query parameter; type is \"string\" , \"integer\" , \"number\" or \"boolean\" o.describe(text) , o.tag(name) , o.id(operation_id) documentation; the id defaults to the method and path ( getUsersId ) o.fails(code, description) a documented error response; secured operations ge"},{"p":"openapi","h":"The document and the client","u":"packages/openapi.html#the-document-and-the-client","t":"- openapi.document(spec) str : the OpenAPI 3.1 JSON. Structs become components/schemas ; with openapi.bearer(&spec, description) the document declares a bearer scheme, requires it everywhere but public() operations, and lists 401 responses. - openapi.typescript(spec) str : export type for every schema, and createClient({ baseUrl, token, fetch, init }) with one method per operation, named by its operation id: path parameters first, then the body, then the query. Errors throw ApiError with the status and body. Enums become string unions ( \"Active\" \"Banned\" ) or, with payloads, tagged objects ( {"},{"p":"sentry","h":"sentry","u":"packages/sentry.html#sentry","t":"The sentry package reports errors and messages to Sentry (https://sentry.io), the hosted service or a self-hosted install. Runtime errors come with native stack traces, and errors inside web handlers come with the request and route they happened in. It's a package of its own ( import sentry ), so programs that don't report errors don't carry it. A DSN that starts with https:// needs import tls , as for any HTTPS request. Without a DSN, or with a bad one, c.ok() is false and c.error() says why. Capturing then does nothing, so the same program runs in development without Sentry."},{"p":"sentry","h":"Setting up","u":"packages/sentry.html#setting-up","t":"sentry.options() fills in the defaults; change the fields you need before calling init : field default --- --- --- release $SENTRY_RELEASE the version, e.g. \"shop@1.4.2\" (Sentry tracks regressions by release) environment $SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT , else \"production\" server_name $HOSTNAME sample_rate 1.0 the share of events sent max_queue 100 events waiting to be sent; more are dropped breadcrumbs 50 recent log records sent with each event timeout_ms 10000 per request to Sentry scrub see Scrubbing ( scrubbing) names whose values are hidden"},{"p":"sentry","h":"Capturing","u":"packages/sentry.html#capturing","t":"Each returns the event's id (32 hex digits), or \"\" if nothing was queued: sending is off, the event wasn't sampled, the queue is full, or Sentry asked us to wait. Levels are \"fatal\" , \"error\" , \"warning\" (or \"warn\" ), \"info\" , and \"debug\" . capture_result sends an Err as an error event whose message is the error text, and ignores an Ok . It fits the library's error format ( \"pg: query failed: timeout\" ): sentry.install(c) hands every runtime error to c through os.on_error . That includes a failed task, a web handler that answered 500, and the error that ends the program. Each one is sent befor"},{"p":"sentry","h":"Scope: tags, user, request","u":"packages/sentry.html#scope-tags-user-request","t":"Tags, extra values, and the user apply to the events of the task that set them, including a runtime error in that task, and to tasks it spawns. They're kept in task-locals (../reference.md 15-parallelism), so concurrent requests don't see each other's. sentry.middleware(c) records the request for each event from inside a handler: method, URL, query string, headers, and the client's IP. It also sets the transaction to the matched route ( GET /orders/{id} ), so Sentry groups events by route rather than by URL. A panic inside the handler arrives with that request data too, since error reports car"},{"p":"sentry","h":"Scrubbing","u":"packages/sentry.html#scrubbing","t":"The values of headers, query parameters, set_extra keys, and log fields are replaced with \" Filtered \" when the name, lowercased, contains one of o.scrub . By default those are auth , cookie , password , passwd , secret , token , key , session , and credential . That covers Authorization , Cookie , Set-Cookie , X-Api-Key , ?token= , api_key= , and the like. Add your own before init :"},{"p":"sentry","h":"Breadcrumbs","u":"packages/sentry.html#breadcrumbs","t":"Each event carries the last o.breadcrumbs records from log (../std/log.md)'s memory ( log.recent ), with their fields scrubbed. By default the log keeps records at warn and up; log.keep(\"info\") keeps info records too."},{"p":"sentry","h":"Sending","u":"packages/sentry.html#sending","t":"Capturing builds the event, puts it on a queue, and returns; one background task per client sends the queue, so a slow or unreachable Sentry never holds up a request. The sender works like Sentry's own SDKs: - It retries after a network error or a 5xx answer, 1 s, 2 s, and 4 s later, then drops the event. - A 429 answer, or an X-Sentry-Rate-Limits header covering error events, makes the client drop new events until the time Sentry gave has passed. - The X-Sentry-Auth header and the envelope format are Sentry's current ones (protocol version 7). flush waits, parking only the calling task, until"},{"p":"sentry","h":"Stack traces","u":"packages/sentry.html#stack-traces","t":"Frames are sent as native addresses, with the function names Tern already knows (Tern names like load or roost._limited , and C symbols for the runtime). Each event also describes the executable: its build id (Mach-O UUID or ELF GNU build id), load address, and path. Sentry uses these to symbolicate the addresses, giving file names and lines for every frame, from a debug file you upload: For source lines in every frame, build with tern build -g (no optimization), or upload the file that dsymutil app makes on macOS. Frames from your own code are marked in-app; frames from the runtime, the stand"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Tern language reference","u":"reference.html#tern-language-reference","t":"This is the complete reference for writing Tern programs. It is written so that a reader who has never seen Tern (a person or an LLM) can write correct code on the first try. Every tern block below is a complete program that the test suite ( tern run tests/runner/main.tn -- docs ) compiles and runs; when an output block follows, the program's output must match it exactly. So the examples here are known to work."},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"0. The short version","u":"reference.html#0-the-short-version","t":"Tern looks like Python, but it is statically typed and compiled, and every value is a copy : there are no references, pointers, None , classes, or exceptions. The rules that differ most from Python: 1. Functions are fn name(a: int, b: str) int: . Every parameter needs a type. The return type comes after the ) with no arrow ( - int is also accepted). No return type = returns nothing. 2. x = 5 creates a variable. Its type is fixed from then on. x: float = 0.0 states a type. There is no let or var , and there are no global variables. Top-level const NAME = value declares a constant (computed at c"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"1. Running programs","u":"reference.html#1-running-programs","t":"check --json prints {\"ok\": true, \"file\": ...} or {\"ok\": false, \"errors\": ... } , where each error is {\"file\", \"line\", \"message\", \"hint\", \"source\"} ( hint is null when there is none; source is the offending line). Compile errors look like prog.tn:12: error: <message , often followed by a hint: line when the code looks like another language. All errors in a file are reported at once. A runtime error (index out of bounds, missing map key, division by zero, pop() on an empty array) prints runtime error in f (prog.tn:N): <message to stderr ( (util.tn, line N) when it happened in another file), then"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"2. Lexical rules","u":"reference.html#2-lexical-rules","t":"- Comments start with and run to the end of the line. There are no block comments or docstrings. - Blocks are made by indentation after a : , like Python. Use 4 spaces. A block with one statement may go on the same line: if x 0: print(x) . - A line continues while a ( , , or { is open, or after a trailing \\ . - No semicolons. - Integers: 42 , 1_000_000 , hex 0xff , binary 0b1010 , octal 0o17 ( _ may separate digits anywhere). A decimal literal must fit in an int (up to 9223372036854775807 ); a hex, binary, or octal one may use all 64 bits, and above that value it is negative ( 0xffff_ffff_ffff"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"3. Variables and types","u":"reference.html#3-variables-and-types","t":"- A variable is created by its first assignment and lives until the end of the block it was created in. Create it before an if if you need it after: - There is no declaration without a value ( x: int alone is an error); use x = 0 . - Constants go at the top level: const NAME = value or const NAME: type = value . A value made of literals, other such constants, and operators (including a if c else b ) is an int, float, str, or bool computed when compiling; it can also be a match pattern. A constant can be used anywhere a value can, and from other modules ( config.PORT , from config import PORT )"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"4. Operators","u":"reference.html#4-operators","t":"Operator Works on Notes --- --- --- + - / int with int, float with float / on ints truncates toward zero: 7 / 2 == 3 , -7 / 2 == -3 % int sign follows the left side: -7 % 3 == -1 + str with str concatenation str with int repetition: \"ab\" 3 == != any two values of the same type structs, tuples, and enums field by field, arrays and maps element by element; not functions or task handles < <= = int, float, str strings compare by bytes and or not bool short-circuit in x in xs (array), k in m (map key), \"a\" in s (substring) & \\ ^ int with int bitwise and, or, xor (for bools, use and / or ) << int wi"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"5. Control flow","u":"reference.html#5-control-flow","t":"- for i in a..b with a = b runs zero times. To count down, use while . - The loop variable can't be assigned, and the array or map being looped over can't be changed inside the loop. To change elements while looping, loop over indices: for i in 0..len(xs): xs i = xs i 2 . - match is covered in \u00a79. There is no switch , try , with , goto , or do/while ."},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"6. Functions","u":"reference.html#6-functions","t":"- Every parameter has a type. There are no default values, keyword arguments (named arguments are only for building structs), variadic parameters, or overloading. - A function with a return type must end with a return on every path. - Parameter modes: - default: read-only. Assigning to it, or calling push on it, is an error. - mut : the function may change it and the caller sees the change. The caller must write &x . - take : the value moves into the function (no copy). The caller can't use x afterwards (pass copy(x) to keep a copy). - To return several values, return a tuple: fn divmod(a: int"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"7. Structs","u":"reference.html#7-structs","t":"- All fields must be given when building a struct; there are no default field values. Write a constructor function if you want defaults: fn new_player(name: str) Player: . - No self , no classes, no inheritance, no interfaces. == compares two structs field by field; when equality means something else, write fn same_user(a: User, b: User) bool . - A struct may contain an array of itself ( kids: Node ) but not itself directly. For graphs, store nodes in an array and refer to them by index. - print shows structs as Name{field: value, ...} ."},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Tuples","u":"reference.html#tuples","t":"A tuple is a fixed group of values of any types, written (a, b) ; its type is written the same way, (int, str) . It is a value like a struct whose fields have no names: read them by position with t.0 , t.1 . Functions return several values as a tuple, and a, b = ... unpacks one (each name on the left is a new variable, an existing one, a field or element, or _ to skip a value). - A tuple has two or more values; there is no one-value tuple. t.0 = 5 changes one value. - The number of names on the left of a, b = ... must match the tuple. - Tuples can go anywhere a type can: in arrays, maps, struc"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Generics","u":"reference.html#generics","t":"Functions, structs, and enums can take type parameters in after their name. A use with concrete types makes a version of the function or type for those types (like C++ templates or Rust): it runs exactly as fast as code written by hand for them. - Type arguments are usually inferred: from the arguments, then from the type the result is going into (a typed variable, a return , a parameter), then from lambdas. When they can't be, write them: first int (xs) , Pair int, str (1, \"a\") , Tree int .Leaf . - A generic type always needs its arguments where a type is written: Pair int, str , not Pair . -"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Type aliases","u":"reference.html#type-aliases","t":"type Name = T at the top level of a module gives a type a second name. The alias is the type: values pass freely between the two names, Name(...) constructs one, and x.f() finds f in the module that defines the original. A package uses aliases to present types that live elsewhere under its own name ( roost.Request is http.Request ). - The alias's target is resolved in the alias's own module, so type Request = http.Request needs import http there; users of the alias don't. - An alias can name any type ( T , {K: V} , fn(int) str , another alias), but not itself, directly or through other aliases"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"8. Arrays [T]","u":"reference.html#8-arrays-t","t":"- xs.extend(ys) pushes every element of ys onto xs . - Slices clamp to the length ( 1, 2, 3 1..10 is 2, 3 ), for strings too. - Indexes start at 0. Out-of-range indexes are runtime errors. There are no negative indexes: the last element is xs len(xs) - 1 . - xs.sorted() is a sorted copy of an array of ints, floats, or strs; xs.reversed() is a copy in reverse order; sum(xs) adds an int or float (0 when empty; int sums wrap like + ), and min(xs) , max(xs) find its smallest and largest (an empty array is a runtime error). None of them changes xs : to sort in place, write xs = xs.sorted() . - sort"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Bytes","u":"reference.html#bytes","t":"byte is a number from 0 to 255, for binary data: pixels, network buffers, hashes. A byte stores one byte per element (an int uses eight), so a 1024x1024 RGBA image is 4MB. - In arithmetic and comparisons a byte counts as an int , and so does the result: px 0 77 can't overflow. -b is an int too. - Storing an int into a byte (a variable, element, field, or argument) takes byte(n) , which keeps the low 8 bits. A literal from 0 to 255 needs no conversion; one outside that range is an error. b += n (any op= ) on a byte wraps, as byte(b + n) would. - int(b) and float(b) convert back. s.byte(i) still"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"32-bit floats","u":"reference.html#32-bit-floats","t":"f32 is a 32-bit float, for big arrays of numbers: vectors, embeddings, audio, model weights. An f32 stores 4 bytes per element (a float uses eight), so a million 768-number embeddings take 3GB instead of 6GB. std simd works on four of them at once ( f32x4 ), and std binary reads and writes them as raw bytes ( binary.f32s_le , binary.f16s_le ). - Math on f32s stays in f32, as in Rust, Go and C. An f32 and a float don't mix: write f32(x) or float(x) (a literal like 0.5 adapts by itself). - print , str and f-strings show the shortest decimal that reads back as the same f32 ( 0.1 , not 0.100000001"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"9. Enums and match","u":"reference.html#9-enums-and-match","t":"- Build variants as Enum.Variant(values) or Enum.Variant . In patterns, write the bare variant name ( Circle(r) ) or Shape.Circle(r) ; _ ignores one value ( Rect(w, _) ). An enum from another module is named through it: json.Value.Null , or just json.Null when only one of the module's enums has that variant (both in patterns too). - A match must handle every value: on an enum, every variant (or end with _: ; missing variants are listed in the error); on an int or str, it needs a _: arm ( _: pass when nothing should happen); on a bool, true and false or _: . - Enum payloads can only be read wit"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Option and Result","u":"reference.html#option-and-result","t":"Two generic enums are always available, without an import: Option T is a value that may be missing (Tern has no null); Result T, E is a result or an error (Tern has no exceptions). Build them bare: Some(3) , None , Ok(v) , Err(\"bad\") . Test them with match , or with x is Pattern , whose names are bound in the if or while block. And x? gives the value inside Some / Ok , or else returns the None / Err from the function at once (the function must return an Option, or a Result with the same error type). - Methods: o.unwrap_or(default) , o.unwrap() (a runtime error on None), o.expect(msg) , o.is_so"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"10. Strings str","u":"reference.html#10-strings-str","t":"Strings are UTF-8 bytes. len , indexing, and slicing count bytes , and s i is a one-byte str . For ASCII text this is the same as characters. Function Result --- --- len(s) / s.len() length in bytes s i , s a..b , s a.. , s ..b one-byte string, substring s.find(sub) index of the first match, or -1 s.find_from(sub, start) the first match at or after start , or -1 s.strip_prefix(p) , s.strip_suffix(p) s without p at its start / end ( s itself if it isn't there) s.contains(sub) , sub in s bool s.starts_with(p) , s.ends_with(p) bool s.replace(old, new) every occurrence replaced s.trim() without le"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Multi-line and raw strings","u":"reference.html#multi-line-and-raw-strings","t":"- \"\"\" strings drop the line break after the opening quotes and before the closing ones, and the indentation their lines share: indent them with the code. - raw strings keep every character: \\d is a backslash and a d , handy for re patterns, JSON, and Windows paths. They have no escapes, so they can't hold a backtick."},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"11. Maps {K: V}","u":"reference.html#11-maps-k-v","t":"Keys are int , str , bool , or structs, enums, tuples, and arrays made of those (so not float or functions): {(int, int): str} for a grid, {Point: bool} for a set of points. Iteration order is insertion order. - Reading a missing key with m k is a runtime error; use k in m or m.get(k, default) . - m.remove(k) does nothing if k is missing. m.keys() and m.values() return arrays. - for k, v in m: loops over pairs; for k in m: loops over keys. - Write not (k in m) with the parentheses; there is no not in operator. - A map can't be changed while looping over it. Loop over a copy of its keys instead"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"12. Printing","u":"reference.html#12-printing","t":"print(a, b, ...) prints its arguments separated by spaces, then a newline. It accepts any value. Floats show a decimal point ( 2.0 ) and use the shortest exact form ( 0.1 + 0.2 prints 0.30000000000000004 ); very large or small ones use exponent form ( 1e+19 ). Strings print raw at the top level and quoted inside arrays, maps, and structs. There is no end= or sep= ; build a string instead. F-strings have no format specs. The fmt module does fixed decimals, padding, and digit grouping:"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"13. Functions as values (closures)","u":"reference.html#13-functions-as-values-closures","t":"- A lambda is fn(params) expression : one expression, no : and no return . For more than one line, define a nested fn and pass it by name. - Parameter types may be left out when the context says them (a typed parameter, field, or variable, or map / filter / sort_by ). Otherwise write them: fn(x: int) x 2 . - A closure copies the variables it uses when it is created, and can't change them. To accumulate, loop instead, or use a mut parameter."},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"14. Values, mut, and the safety rules","u":"reference.html#14-values-mut-and-the-safety-rules","t":"Tern has no references, so aliasing bugs and data races are compile errors. The rules and the fix for each: You wrote Error says Fix --- --- --- n += 1 where n is a parameter can't modify parameter n declare it n: mut int and call with &x , or return the new value inc(x) where inc takes mut write &x inc(&x) swap(&a, &a) or f(&a, a) would see the same value twice pass a copy: b = a then f(&a, b) use of xs after f(xs) where f takes take was handed to a take parameter pass copy(xs) xs.push(...) inside for x in xs: can't modify xs while looping over it for i in 0..len(xs): , or build a new array t"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"15. Parallelism","u":"reference.html#15-parallelism","t":"- A variable created by a one-line task is visible after the block. Variables created inside a do: group are local to it; create them before the block to use them after. - The compiler rejects any task that writes a variable another task reads or writes. One exception: tasks may write different elements of an array written with literal indexes ( xs 0 = a() in one task, xs 1 = b() in another), if they don't touch the array otherwise. - parallel needs no threads, locks, or channels. For long-running concurrent work (tasks that outlive a block, channels between them, shared state behind a lock), "},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Groups of tasks: task.all, task.try_all (Go's errgroup)","u":"reference.html#groups-of-tasks-taskall-tasktry_all-gos-errgroup","t":"task.all(fs) runs every function in fs ( fn() T ) at once and returns their results in order; a runtime error in one is raised in the caller. task.try_all(fs) does the same for functions that return Result T, E : Ok with every value in order, or the first Err to arrive, after cancelling the tasks still running. task.try_all_limit(fs, n) runs at most n at a time (Go's SetLimit ), for \"fetch these 500 URLs, 8 at once\"."},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"Counters and caches: task.atomic, task.shared_map","u":"reference.html#counters-and-caches-taskatomic-taskshared_map","t":"shared T changes any value safely, but each get and update takes a lock that tasks wait for in turn: fine for occasional changes, slow for a counter or cache that every request touches. For those two cases there are faster handles (copies share one, like channels): - task.atomic(v) : an int changed with atomic instructions. a.load() , a.store(v) , a.add(n) (returns the new value), a.cas(old, new) (stores new if the value was old ). - task.shared_map() : a str - str map any task can use at once ( m.put(k, v) , m.get_or(k, default) , m.has(k) , m.remove(k) , m.count() ), plus an int per key for "},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"16. Standard library","u":"reference.html#16-standard-library","t":"Import a module with import name , then call name.fn(...) . Or from name import fn1, Type . Module Functions and types --- --- fmt fixed(x: float, digits: int) str ( \"3.14\" ), cents(n: int) str ( 1205 \u2192 \"12.05\" ), zero_pad(n, width) str , pad_left(s, width, fill) str , pad_right(s, width, fill) str , thousands(n) str ( \"1,234,567\" ) math sqrt floor ceil round log exp sin cos tan (x: float) float , pow(x: float, y: float) float , pi() float random int(lo: int, hi: int) int (inclusive), float() float in 0, 1) time now() float (seconds since 1970), now_ms() int , sleep(ms) , mono_ns() int , mono_"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"17. Modules and packages","u":"reference.html#17-modules-and-packages","t":"Every .tn file is a module. import models looks in the standard library and the first-party packages ( roost , queue , pg ), then for models.tn or models/ (a directory of .tn files forming one package) next to the main file, then in deps/ . Use models.User , models.load(...) , or from models import User, load . Names starting with _ are private. Third-party packages are git repositories: list them in a tern.mod file next to your program and run tern get , which vendors them into deps/ , where import finds them (see cli.md (cli.md packages))."},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"18. Other targets and C","u":"reference.html#18-other-targets-and-c","t":"- --target wasm ios ios-sim android ; build --lib makes a C library with a header from export fn functions; --shared makes a .so / .dylib . On wasm, --lib also writes a JavaScript loader. Wasm runs parallel sequentially; sqlite and http aren't available there. - Calling C: an extern \"header.h\": block of fn signatures (see README.md, \"Calling C\"). Only int float bool str ptr int float and functions of those cross the boundary. ptr is an opaque C pointer; null is the null pointer ( p: ptr = null ) and p.is_null() tests for it. null is only a ptr : other types have no null. A str result stops at "},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"19. What Tern does not have (and what to write instead)","u":"reference.html#19-what-tern-does-not-have-and-what-to-write-instead","t":"From another language In Tern --- --- def f(x): , func , function , fun fn f(x: int) int: class , methods, self , impl , interfaces, traits struct + functions taking it first; x.f() calls f(x) let x , var x , const X , x := 1 , auto x = 1 global variables const MAX_USERS = 100 for fixed values; const CACHE = task.shared_map() for a program-wide cache; pass a struct for other state that changes None , null , nil , Option<T , T? Option T : Some(x) or None (\u00a79) exceptions, try/except , throw return a Result T, E : Ok(v) or Err(e) ; r? passes an Err up, r.context(\"...\") adds where (\u00a79) panic! , as"},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"20. Builtin names","u":"reference.html#20-builtin-names","t":"These are the builtin functions: A function of your own, in the program or in a module, may have one of these names. A call get(a, ...) or a.get(...) goes to your get when its first parameter fits a and the number of arguments matches; otherwise it goes to the builtin, so the builtin still works on strings, arrays, and maps next to yours: When the first argument is itself of a builtin type (a string, number, array, or map), a call that doesn't fit your function goes to the builtin; for any other type, the error is about your function. Functions in an extern block and export fn functions can't "},{"p":"Tern language reference","h":"21. Idioms","u":"reference.html#21-idioms","t":"Graphs and trees that need links between nodes use indexes into an array:"},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Builtin functions","u":"builtins.html#builtin-functions","t":"Builtins are the functions that are part of the language: they need no import , and their names are reserved. This page lists every one with its signature, what it does, the errors it can cause (at compile time and at run time), and a tested example. It starts with the rule that lets you call any function as a method."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Method calls: x.f(a) is f(x, a)","u":"builtins.html#method-calls-xfa-is-fx-a","t":"Any function, builtin or your own, can be called with its first argument in front: s.upper() is upper(s) , xs.sort_by(fn(x) x) is sort_by(xs, fn(x) x) , and \"-\".join(parts) is join(\"-\", parts) . The two spellings are the same call; use whichever reads better. If the first parameter is mut , the method form passes it with & for you: xs.push(v) is push(&xs, v) . For a value whose type comes from a module, x.f also looks in that module: d.exec(sql, params) calls sqlite.exec(d, sql, params) because d is a sqlite.Db . The signatures below use T , K , and V for \"any type\" (an element type, a map's k"},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Your functions with builtin names","u":"builtins.html#your-functions-with-builtin-names","t":"A function of your own may have one of these names. A call goes to yours when its first parameter fits the first argument (and the argument count matches), and to the builtin otherwise; so find on a string still means the builtin here: Functions in an extern block and export fn functions can't have a builtin's name: C sees their names."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Output","u":"builtins.html#output","t":""},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"print","u":"builtins.html#print","t":"Prints its arguments to standard output, separated by single spaces, then a newline. It takes any number of values of any type, including arrays, maps, structs, and enums. - Strings print as they are at the top level, and in double quotes inside arrays, maps, and structs. - Floats always show a decimal point or an exponent ( 2.0 , 1e+20 ) and use the shortest text that reads back as the same number ( 0.1 + 0.2 prints 0.30000000000000004 ). - Structs print as Name{field: value, ...} , enums as Enum.Variant or Enum.Variant(field: value) , maps as {key: value, ...} . - There is no end= or sep= : "},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Numbers and conversions","u":"builtins.html#numbers-and-conversions","t":""},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"len","u":"builtins.html#len","t":"The length of a string in bytes , the number of elements in an array, or the number of entries in a map. It takes constant time. Any other type is a compile error. \"h\u00e9llo\" is six bytes because \u00e9 takes two bytes in UTF-8."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"int","u":"builtins.html#int","t":"Converts a float to an int by cutting off the fraction (rounding toward zero): int(2.9) is 2 and int(-2.9) is -2 . To round instead, use int(math.round(x)) or int(math.floor(x)) (see math (std/math.md)). The result for NaN, infinity, or a value outside the int range is unspecified. int(b) of a byte gives its value. int of anything else is a compile error; to parse a string, use to_int ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"float","u":"builtins.html#float","t":"Converts an int (or a byte or an f32) to a float. Ints above 2<sup 53</sup (about 9 \u00d7 10<sup 15</sup ) may be rounded to the nearest float. float of anything else is a compile error."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"f32","u":"builtins.html#f32","t":"Rounds a float (or an int) to the nearest f32 (reference.md 32-bit-floats), the 32-bit float: about 7 significant digits, up to about 3.4 \u00d7 10<sup 38</sup (larger is inf ). float(x) widens it back exactly."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"str","u":"builtins.html#str","t":"The text of a number or a bool, written the same way print writes it: str(42) is \"42\" , str(2.0) is \"2.0\" , str(true) is \"true\" . Arrays, maps, structs, and enums are a compile error; use json.encode(x) for text, or print to show them. F-strings convert the same way: f\"n={n}\" equals \"n=\" + str(n) ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"abs","u":"builtins.html#abs","t":"The absolute value. The result has the argument's type. abs of the most negative int (-9223372036854775808) is itself, since the positive value doesn't fit."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"min, max","u":"builtins.html#min-max","t":"The smaller or larger of two numbers of the same type (mixing int and float is a compile error), or the smallest or largest element of an array of numbers. An empty array is a runtime error, max() of an empty array : check len(xs) 0 first. In a float array, NaN elements are skipped."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"sqrt","u":"builtins.html#sqrt","t":"The square root, the same as math.sqrt without the import. It takes a float only: write sqrt(float(n)) . The square root of a negative number is NaN."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"chr","u":"builtins.html#chr","t":"A one-byte string whose byte value is n : chr(65) is \"A\" . n must be 0 to 255; any other value is a runtime error. Bytes 128 to 255 are not characters on their own in UTF-8: chr(233) is not \"\u00e9\" . The reverse is s.byte(i) ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"copy","u":"builtins.html#copy","t":"An explicit copy of any value. Assignment already copies ( b = a ), so copy is only needed where a value would otherwise be moved : when passing it to a take parameter or to push (below) while still using it afterwards. Copies are lazy, so copy itself is cheap; the real copy happens only if one side is later changed."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Arrays","u":"builtins.html#arrays","t":""},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"push","u":"builtins.html#push","t":"Adds v to the end of xs ; write xs.push(v) . Adding is amortized constant time. push copies v into the array, the way = does: the copy is lazy (the two share storage until one is written), and when v is a variable that isn't used afterwards, its value moves in without even that. Compile errors: pushing a value of the wrong type ( argument 2 of push should be int, not str ), and pushing onto the array a for loop is iterating over."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"pop","u":"builtins.html#pop","t":"Removes the last element of xs and returns it; write xs.pop() . On an empty array it is a runtime error: pop() from an empty array . Check len(xs) 0 first. There is no way to remove from the front or the middle in place; slice ( xs 1.. ) or filter instead."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"map","u":"builtins.html#map","t":"A new array with f applied to each element, in order. f must return a value; the result's element type is whatever f returns. xs is not changed."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"filter","u":"builtins.html#filter","t":"A new array with the elements for which keep returns true , in their original order. keep must return bool ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"sort_by","u":"builtins.html#sort_by","t":"A new array sorted by the key key returns for each element, smallest first. xs itself is not changed. The sort is stable : elements with equal keys keep their original order, so you can sort by one key and then another. Strings compare by bytes, so uppercase sorts before lowercase ( \"B\" < \"a\" ); sort by w.lower() to ignore case. For descending order, negate a number key ( fn(x) -x.score ). A key of any other type is a compile error. The function in map , filter , and sort_by is usually a lambda, whose parameter type is inferred from the array; a named function works too."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"sum","u":"builtins.html#sum","t":"The elements added up, in order; 0 (or 0.0 ) for an empty array. Int sums wrap on overflow, like + . Any other element type is a compile error: sum() needs an array of numbers ( int , float or f32 ), not str ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"sorted, reversed","u":"builtins.html#sorted-reversed","t":"A sorted copy (smallest first) and a copy in reverse order. xs is not changed; to sort in place, write xs = xs.sorted() . For other orders, use sort_by . ( sorted comes from the prelude rather than the language, so it isn't in the list of builtin names above; a function of yours named sorted takes its place.)"},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Strings","u":"builtins.html#strings","t":"Strings are UTF-8 bytes, and every position and length below counts bytes . For ASCII text that is the same as characters. None of these functions change the string they are called on: strings are values, and each function returns a new one."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"find","u":"builtins.html#find","t":"The byte index of the first occurrence of sub in s , or -1 if there is none. An empty sub is found at 0 ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"contains","u":"builtins.html#contains","t":"Whether sub occurs in s . Same as sub in s ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"starts_with, ends_with","u":"builtins.html#starts_with-ends_with","t":"Whether s begins or ends with the given text. Every string starts and ends with \"\" ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"replace","u":"builtins.html#replace","t":"s with every non-overlapping occurrence of old replaced by new , scanning left to right. An empty old returns s unchanged."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"trim","u":"builtins.html#trim","t":"s without leading and trailing spaces, tabs, \\n , and \\r . There is no left-only or right-only version."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"upper, lower","u":"builtins.html#upper-lower","t":"s with ASCII letters changed to upper or lower case. Other bytes, including non-ASCII letters like \u00e9 , are unchanged."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"repeat","u":"builtins.html#repeat","t":"s repeated n times; \"\" if n is 0 or negative. s n is the same."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"split","u":"builtins.html#split","t":"The pieces of s between occurrences of sep . Empty pieces are kept, so len(s.split(sep)) is always one more than the number of separators, and \"\".split(\",\") is \"\" . An empty sep splits into single bytes. There is no \"split on any whitespace\"; replace tabs and newlines with spaces first, and skip empty pieces."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"join","u":"builtins.html#join","t":"The strings in parts with sep between them. It is called on the separator, like Python: \", \".join(names) . Joining gives \"\" ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"to_int","u":"builtins.html#to_int","t":"s parsed as a whole number, or default if s isn't one. It accepts an optional + or - followed by decimal digits, and nothing else: no spaces (trim first), no _ , no decimal point, no hex. Numbers too large for an int are not detected and wrap around, so limit the length of untrusted input."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"to_float","u":"builtins.html#to_float","t":"s parsed as a number, or default if it isn't one. It accepts what C's strtod accepts: integers, decimals, exponents ( 1e3 ), leading whitespace, and also inf , nan , and hex floats ( 0x10 ), so check the result's range if the input is untrusted. Trailing text makes it fail."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"byte","u":"builtins.html#byte","t":"byte(n) makes a byte (reference.md bytes) from an int, keeping its low 8 bits: byte(300) is 44 , byte(-1) is 255 . s.byte(i) is the byte at index i , as an int from 0 to 255. An index outside the string is a runtime error, as with s i (which gives a one-byte string instead of a number)."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"to_bytes, to_str","u":"builtins.html#to_bytes-to_str","t":"A string's bytes as a byte , and back. Neither copies: a str and a byte are stored the same way, so both share the block until one of them is changed. to_str doesn't check that the bytes are valid UTF-8."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Maps","u":"builtins.html#maps","t":""},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"get","u":"builtins.html#get","t":"The value for key , or default if m has no such key. Reading m key for a missing key is a runtime error, so get is the safe way to read. The default is required."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"remove","u":"builtins.html#remove","t":"Deletes key and its value; write m.remove(key) . Removing a key that isn't there does nothing. It returns nothing, so read the value first if you need it."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"keys, values","u":"builtins.html#keys-values","t":"The map's keys, or values, as a new array, in insertion order (the order for k, v in m: uses). A key that is removed and added again moves to the end. Common compile errors: calling these on something that isn't a map ( get() works on maps, not int ), a key or default of the wrong type, and leaving out get 's default."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Web helpers","u":"builtins.html#web-helpers","t":""},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"json_quote","u":"builtins.html#json_quote","t":"s as a JSON string literal, including the surrounding double quotes: \" , \\ , and control characters are escaped, and other bytes are kept. It is the same as json.encode(s) , for building JSON by hand without importing json ."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"url_decode","u":"builtins.html#url_decode","t":"s with URL encoding undone: + becomes a space and %XX (two hex digits) becomes that byte. A % not followed by two hex digits is kept as it is. req.query , req.form and http.match call it for you."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"C pointers","u":"builtins.html#c-pointers","t":""},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"is_null","u":"builtins.html#is_null","t":"Whether a C pointer is null. ptr is the opaque pointer type used with extern functions; see Calling C (guides/c-interop.md)."},{"p":"Builtin functions","h":"Runtime errors","u":"builtins.html#runtime-errors","t":"These builtins can stop a program at run time. The program prints runtime error in f (prog.tn:N): <message to standard error and exits with status 2 (inside a web request handler, the request gets a 500 instead; see http (std/http.md errors-in-handlers)): Call Message --- --- xs.pop() on an empty array pop() from an empty array s.byte(i) with i out of range index 5 out of bounds (length 3) m key for a missing key (not a builtin, but common) key not found (use m.get(key, default) or key in m ) Everything else on this page either can't fail or reports failure through its result ( find gives -1 ,"},{"p":"Tern grammar","h":"Tern grammar","u":"grammar.html#tern-grammar","t":"The exact syntax accepted by the compiler ( compiler/syntax/lexer.tn , compiler/syntax/parser.tn ), in EBNF. { x } means zero or more, x optional, alternatives, quoted text is literal. For what the constructs mean, see reference.md (reference.md)."},{"p":"Tern grammar","h":"Tokens","u":"grammar.html#tokens","t":""},{"p":"Tern grammar","h":"Program","u":"grammar.html#program","t":""},{"p":"Tern grammar","h":"Types","u":"grammar.html#types","t":""},{"p":"Tern grammar","h":"Statements","u":"grammar.html#statements","t":""},{"p":"Tern grammar","h":"Expressions","u":"grammar.html#expressions","t":"Lowest to highest precedence; all binary operators are left-associative. Notes: - not binds tighter than in and == , so write not (k in m) and not (a == b) . - Name(...) where Name is a struct builds a struct; Enum.Variant(...) builds a variant. - x.f(args) calls f(x, args) (or a function-valued field f of struct x ). - & before an operand is the mut -argument marker ( f(&x) ); between two operands it is bitwise and. - There is no c ? a : b (write a if c else b ), no , no // , no not in , no is not ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Compiler errors","u":"errors.html#compiler-errors","t":"This page lists the errors the Tern compiler reports, with what each one means and how to fix it. Search the page for the text of your error: each heading is an error message, with the parts that change (names, types, counts) written in capitals, like NAME , TYPE , or N . Every entry has a short program that produces the error, the exact output of the compiler, and a fixed program. The last section covers runtime errors, which happen while a program runs."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"How to read an error","u":"errors.html#how-to-read-an-error","t":"The compiler checks the whole file before it builds anything. Each problem is one line: - prog.tn:12 is the file and line. - error: says what is wrong. Type and safety errors are all reported at once; a syntax error (the file can't be read as Tern at all) stops at the first one. - hint: appears when the compiler recognizes a habit from another language or a likely typo, and says what to write instead. Not every error has one. In the examples below the file name is left out, so 5: error: ... means line 5. For tools and agents, check --json prints the same information as JSON on stdout: hint is "},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Contents","u":"errors.html#contents","t":"- Syntax ( syntax) - Habits from other languages ( habits-from-other-languages) - Names and types ( names-and-types) - Operators and conversions ( operators-and-conversions) - Functions and calls ( functions-and-calls) - Structs ( structs) - Enums ( enums) - Arrays, maps, and strings ( arrays-maps-and-strings) - Mutation, mut , and & ( mutation-mut-and-) - Aliasing ( aliasing) - take and moved values ( take-and-moved-values) - Loops ( loops) - Parallelism and races ( parallelism-and-races) - Closures ( closures) - Modules ( modules) - C interop and export ( c-interop-and-export) - Web, databas"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Syntax","u":"errors.html#syntax","t":"Syntax errors come from reading the file, before any types are checked. Only the first one is reported. Many of them come with a hint when the line looks like another language; those are listed in Habits from other languages ( habits-from-other-languages)."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"expected fn, struct, enum, type, const, or extern at the top level","u":"errors.html#expected-fn-struct-enum-type-const-or-extern-at-the-top-level","t":"The top level of a file may only hold import , from ... import , struct , enum , const , fn , extern , and export fn . Statements and variables live inside functions. There are no global variables; a value that never changes can be a const . Make it a constant (or, if it changes, a variable inside a function that you pass around):"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"expected TOKEN, found TOKEN","u":"errors.html#expected-token-found-token","t":"The parser needed a particular token and saw something else. The most common case is a missing : at the end of an if , for , while , fn , struct , or match line."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"expected an indented block, found TOKEN","u":"errors.html#expected-an-indented-block-found-token","t":"A line that ends in : must be followed by an indented block (or a single statement on the same line). For an empty block, write pass ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"expected a name, found TOKEN","u":"errors.html#expected-a-name-found-token","t":"The parser needed a name (of a variable, function, field, or type) here."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"expected end of line, found TOKEN","u":"errors.html#expected-end-of-line-found-token","t":"The statement was complete, but more text followed on the same line. Put one statement on each line."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"expected =, found end of line","u":"errors.html#expected--found-end-of-line","t":"A variable is created by giving it a value. x: int alone is not a declaration."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"unexpected TOKEN","u":"errors.html#unexpected-token","t":"The parser found a token that can't start or continue an expression here."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"unexpected indentation: this line is indented more than the block it is in","u":"errors.html#unexpected-indentation-this-line-is-indented-more-than-the-block-it-is-in","t":"A line is indented more than the line before it, but the line before doesn't end in : . Only a block after : may be indented further."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"this line's indentation doesn't match any enclosing block","u":"errors.html#this-lines-indentation-doesnt-match-any-enclosing-block","t":"When a block ends, the next line must line up with one of the blocks around it. Here line 4 is indented 6 spaces, but the blocks are at 4 and 8. Use 4 spaces per level."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"unterminated string","u":"errors.html#unterminated-string","t":"A string has no closing \" on the same line. Strings are one line; write \\n for a line break."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"unterminated string inside f-string","u":"errors.html#unterminated-string-inside-f-string","t":"This usually means a { in an f-string has no closing } . The compiler keeps reading the expression inside the braces, takes the f-string's closing \" as the start of a new string, and reaches the end of the line."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"f-string has } without an opening { (write }} for a brace)","u":"errors.html#f-string-has--without-an-opening--write--for-a-brace","t":"In an f-string, { and } mark expressions. For a literal brace, double it."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't read TEXT inside the f-string","u":"errors.html#cant-read-text-inside-the-f-string","t":"The text between { and } isn't an expression. The usual cause is a format spec such as {x:.2f} : f-strings have none. Use the fmt module instead."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"unexpected character 'C'","u":"errors.html#unexpected-character-c","t":"The character can't appear in Tern code outside a string. Common ones are ; , ' , ! , $ , and @ ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"left side of = must be a variable, field, or element","u":"errors.html#left-side-of--must-be-a-variable-field-or-element","t":"Only a variable ( x ), a field ( p.x ), or an element ( xs i , m k ) can be assigned to."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"positional arguments must come before named ones","u":"errors.html#positional-arguments-must-come-before-named-ones","t":"When building a struct you may give fields by position, by name, or both, but the positional ones come first."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"named arguments are only for building structs","u":"errors.html#named-arguments-are-only-for-building-structs","t":"Functions take positional arguments only. name=value works only in a struct or enum variant constructor. print has no end= or sep= ; build the line as a string first."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"& needs a variable, field, or element","u":"errors.html#-needs-a-variable-field-or-element","t":"&x passes a place that a mut parameter can change. A literal or a computed value has no place to change."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"export goes before fn","u":"errors.html#export-goes-before-fn","t":"Only functions can be exported (for C, JavaScript, or Swift callers). Structs and enums can't."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"function NAME is defined twice","u":"errors.html#function-name-is-defined-twice","t":"There is no overloading: each function name in a module is used once. Give the second one another name."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is defined twice","u":"errors.html#name-is-defined-twice","t":"Two enums (or an enum and a struct) have the same name."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"enum NAME has two variants named VARIANT","u":"errors.html#enum-name-has-two-variants-named-variant","t":"Each variant of an enum needs its own name."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Habits from other languages","u":"errors.html#habits-from-other-languages","t":"These are syntax or name errors whose hint recognizes a habit from Python, JavaScript, Go, Rust, or C. The headings here are the hint text, since the error itself ( expected ... or unexpected ... ) says little on its own."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"functions are written fn name(x: int, y: str) int: (return type after the ))","u":"errors.html#functions-are-written-fn-namex-int-y-str-int-return-type-after-the-","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"there are no classes: declare data with struct Name: (fields only)","u":"errors.html#there-are-no-classes-declare-data-with-struct-name-fields-only","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"variables are created by assigning: x = 5 (no let/var/const)","u":"errors.html#variables-are-created-by-assigning-x--5-no-letvarconst","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"variables are created with =: x = 5","u":"errors.html#variables-are-created-with--x--5","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"write and / write or / write not x","u":"errors.html#write-and--write-or--write-not-x","t":"Logic operators are words."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"write elif cond:","u":"errors.html#write-elif-cond","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"write x += 1 / x -= 1","u":"errors.html#write-x--1--x---1","t":"There is no ++ or -- ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"statements end at the line end; remove the ;","u":"errors.html#statements-end-at-the-line-end-remove-the-","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"strings use double quotes: \"text\" (or text, raw, when it holds double quotes)","u":"errors.html#strings-use-double-quotes-text-or-text-raw-when-it-holds-double-quotes","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"there is no c ? a : b; write a if c else b","u":"errors.html#there-is-no-c--a--b-write-a-if-c-else-b","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"lambdas are written fn(x) x + 1 (one expression)","u":"errors.html#lambdas-are-written-fnx-x--1-one-expression","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"a lambda is one expression with no colon: fn(x) x * 2","u":"errors.html#a-lambda-is-one-expression-with-no-colon-fnx-x--2","t":"A lambda can't hold statements. For more than one line, define a nested fn and pass it by name."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"there are no comprehensions: xs.map(fn(x) ...) and xs.filter(fn(x) ...)","u":"errors.html#there-are-no-comprehensions-xsmapfnx--and-xsfilterfnx-","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"write not (x in xs)","u":"errors.html#write-not-x-in-xs","t":"There is no not in operator. not binds tighter than in , so the parentheses are needed."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"integer literal ... is too big for an int","u":"errors.html#integer-literal--is-too-big-for-an-int","t":"An int is 64 bits: decimal literals go up to 9223372036854775807 . For bigger numbers use a float ( 1e20 ); for bit patterns, a hex literal may use all 64 bits ( 0xffff_ffff_ffff_ffff )."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"parameters are written name: type, e.g. fn f(a: int, b: str)","u":"errors.html#parameters-are-written-name-type-eg-fn-fa-int-b-str","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"there is no self: take the struct as a normal parameter","u":"errors.html#there-is-no-self-take-the-struct-as-a-normal-parameter","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"min_max returns int, not (int,int)","u":"errors.html#min_max-returns-int-not-intint","t":"A function returns several values as a tuple, and its return type must say so:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"import math, then math.pow(x, y) (floats)","u":"errors.html#import-math-then-mathpowx-y-floats","t":"There is no operator."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"/ on two ints already divides without a remainder; comments start with #","u":"errors.html#-on-two-ints-already-divides-without-a-remainder-comments-start-with-","t":"There is no // operator. / on two ints truncates toward zero."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"there are no exceptions: return a bool or a status enum","u":"errors.html#there-are-no-exceptions-return-a-bool-or-a-status-enum","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"use match value: with one arm per case (_: for the default)","u":"errors.html#use-match-value-with-one-arm-per-case-_-for-the-default","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"match arms are Pattern: followed by an indented block","u":"errors.html#match-arms-are-pattern-followed-by-an-indented-block","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"import standard modules by their name: import math, import http","u":"errors.html#import-standard-modules-by-their-name-import-math-import-http","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"array types are [int], map types {str: int}, tuple types (int, str), and a generic type's arguments go in [ ] (Pair[int, str]); there are no set or Option types","u":"errors.html#array-types-are-int-map-types-str-int-tuple-types-int-str-and-a-generic-types-arguments-go-in---pairint-str-there-are-no-set-or-option-types","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Blocks written with braces","u":"errors.html#blocks-written-with-braces","t":"Braces don't make blocks, and there is no hint for this yet: { opens a map literal, so the error is reported on a later line and can be confusing. Use : and indentation."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Names and types","u":"errors.html#names-and-types","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"unknown variable NAME","u":"errors.html#unknown-variable-name","t":"The name isn't a variable in scope. Usually it is a typo (the hint suggests a close name), a variable created inside a block and used after it, or a name from another language ( True , None , self ). A variable lives until the end of the block that created it, so create it before the if or loop if you need it after. Booleans are lowercase. None exists, as the empty Option , but it needs to know what kind of Option it is:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is a module; add import NAME at the top of this file","u":"errors.html#name-is-a-module-add-import-name-at-the-top-of-this-file","t":"The code uses a standard module ( math , fs , json , ...) without importing it. The first error is the one to fix; the second goes away with it. Note that for math.sqrt , math.floor , and the other names that are also builtins, a missing import can instead show up as a wrong argument count."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"unknown type NAME","u":"errors.html#unknown-type-name","t":"The type doesn't exist. Tern's types are int , float , bool , str , T , {K: V} , fn(...) , ptr , and your structs and enums; names from other languages ( string , i32 , f64 , list , dict , Vec ) get a hint."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"unknown function NAME","u":"errors.html#unknown-function-name","t":"No function, builtin, or local function value has this name. The hint covers common names from other languages ( range , append , sort , input , enumerate , strip , ...) and close spellings. xs.f(...) is a call to a function f(xs, ...) , so a missing method is also an unknown function:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is declared as TYPE but the value is TYPE","u":"errors.html#name-is-declared-as-type-but-the-value-is-type","t":"A variable with a stated type ( x: int = ... ) was given a value of another type. There are no implicit conversions."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't assign TYPE to NAME of type TYPE","u":"errors.html#cant-assign-type-to-name-of-type-type","t":"A variable's type is fixed by its first assignment. Use a new variable for a value of another type, or convert."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't tell what NAME holds; add a type, e.g. NAME: [int] = []","u":"errors.html#cant-tell-what-name-holds-add-a-type-eg-name-int--","t":"An empty or {} gets its type from how it is used later (for example the first push ). If nothing ever says what goes in it, the type must be written."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't tell the type of an empty array; add a type, e.g. xs: [int] = []","u":"errors.html#cant-tell-the-type-of-an-empty-array-add-a-type-eg-xs-int--","t":"An empty array literal appears where nothing says its element type, such as a direct argument to print ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't tell the type of an empty map; add a type, e.g. m: {str: int} = {}","u":"errors.html#cant-tell-the-type-of-an-empty-map-add-a-type-eg-m-str-int--","t":"The same, for an empty map literal."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"this expression has no value","u":"errors.html#this-expression-has-no-value","t":"A function with no return type was used as a value."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"map keys must be int, str, bool, or arrays, structs, enums, or tuples of those (no floats or functions), not TYPE","u":"errors.html#map-keys-must-be-int-str-bool-or-arrays-structs-enums-or-tuples-of-those-no-floats-or-functions-not-type","t":"A map key must hash and compare exactly, so floats (where 0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3 ) and functions can't be keys, nor structs that hold them. For a float key, scale it to an int."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is a builtin, so an extern or exported function can't have that name","u":"errors.html#name-is-a-builtin-so-an-extern-or-exported-function-cant-have-that-name","t":"Your own functions may share a builtin's name ( builtin names (reference.md 20-builtin-names)), but a function in an extern block or an export fn can't: C sees its name. Rename an extern function with = \"c_name\" , and pick another name for an exported one."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"no fn main() found (a test file? name it *_test.tn and run tern test; a library? tern build --lib)","u":"errors.html#no-fn-main-found-a-test-file-name-it-_testtn-and-run-tern-test-a-library-tern-build---lib","t":"A program starts at fn main(): . A file with only helper functions can be imported by another file, or built as a C library with build --lib (its export fn functions become the library's API). Test files ( _test.tn ) have no main : tern test runs them, and tern check checks them without one."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Operators and conversions","u":"errors.html#operators-and-conversions","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NUMBER doesn't fit in a byte (0 to 255); byte(NUMBER) keeps its low 8 bits","u":"errors.html#number-doesnt-fit-in-a-byte-0-to-255-bytenumber-keeps-its-low-8-bits","t":"A literal where a byte is expected must be from 0 to 255. To wrap a bigger number around on purpose, convert it with byte(...) ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't assign int to NAME of type byte: convert with byte(...), which keeps the low 8 bits","u":"errors.html#cant-assign-int-to-name-of-type-byte-convert-with-byte-which-keeps-the-low-8-bits","t":"Arithmetic on bytes gives an int, so storing the result back into a byte takes byte(...) . ( b += n and the other op= forms wrap on their own.)"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"OP with mismatched types TYPE and TYPE (use float()/int() to convert)","u":"errors.html#op-with-mismatched-types-type-and-type-use-floatint-to-convert","t":"Both sides of an arithmetic or comparison operator must have the same type. Ints and floats never mix. For str + int the parenthetical advice doesn't apply: convert the number with str() , or use an f-string, as the hint says."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"OP needs numbers, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#op-needs-numbers-not-type","t":"Arithmetic works on int and float only (and + on strings)."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"% needs ints","u":"errors.html#-needs-ints","t":"The remainder operator works on ints only. For floats, use x - math.floor(x / y) y ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"OP doesn't work on strings (use + to join them)","u":"errors.html#op-doesnt-work-on-strings-use--to-join-them","t":"Strings support + (join) and (repeat), not - or / . To remove text, use replace ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"OP needs bools","u":"errors.html#op-needs-bools","t":"and and or work on bool values only. There is no truthiness: compare explicitly."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"not needs a bool","u":"errors.html#not-needs-a-bool","t":"not applies to a bool. It binds tighter than == and in , so not x == 2 means (not x) == 2 . Add parentheses, or use != ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't negate TYPE","u":"errors.html#cant-negate-type","t":"Unary - works on int and float only."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"OP can't compare TYPE: functions, channels, tasks, and shared cells have no equality","u":"errors.html#op-cant-compare-type-functions-channels-tasks-and-shared-cells-have-no-equality","t":"== and != compare any values field by field and element by element (structs, arrays, maps, tuples, enums), except those holding a function or a task handle. Compare the other fields yourself:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"OP needs numbers or strings, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#op-needs-numbers-or-strings-not-type","t":"< , <= , , and = work on int , float , and str (strings compare by bytes)."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"condition must be a bool","u":"errors.html#condition-must-be-a-bool","t":"if , elif , and while need a bool . There is no truthiness for numbers, strings, or arrays."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"in a str looks for a substring, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#in-a-str-looks-for-a-substring-not-type","t":"x in s on a string checks for a substring, so x must be a str ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"in needs an element of TYPE","u":"errors.html#in-needs-an-element-of-type","t":"x in xs on an array checks for an element, so x must have the element type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"in needs a key of TYPE","u":"errors.html#in-needs-a-key-of-type","t":"k in m on a map checks for a key, so k must have the key type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"in works on str, arrays, and maps, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#in-works-on-str-arrays-and-maps-not-type","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"f-strings can show int, float, bool, or str values, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#f-strings-can-show-int-float-bool-or-str-values-not-type","t":"An f-string {...} takes a number, bool, or string. For arrays, maps, and structs, pass the value to print separately, or turn it into text with json.encode ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"str() converts int, float, bool, or str, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#str-converts-int-float-bool-or-str-not-type","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"int() needs a float","u":"errors.html#int-needs-a-float","t":"int() converts a float to an int (truncating). It doesn't parse strings: use s.to_int(default) . (The hint about parsing strings is also shown for int(true) ; for a bool, use an if .)"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"sqrt() needs a float","u":"errors.html#sqrt-needs-a-float","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"abs() needs a number","u":"errors.html#abs-needs-a-number","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"min() needs two numbers of the same type","u":"errors.html#min-needs-two-numbers-of-the-same-type","t":"min and max take exactly two numbers of one type. For the smallest element of an array, loop."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"chr() needs an int (a byte value 0-255)","u":"errors.html#chr-needs-an-int-a-byte-value-0-255","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Functions and calls","u":"errors.html#functions-and-calls","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"function NAME must end with a return","u":"errors.html#function-name-must-end-with-a-return","t":"A function with a return type must return on every path. The compiler doesn't reason about which conditions are possible, so an if / elif chain without a final else or return is not enough."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME must return a TYPE","u":"errors.html#name-must-return-a-type","t":"A bare return in a function that has a return type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME doesn't return a value","u":"errors.html#name-doesnt-return-a-value","t":"return value in a function without a return type. Add the return type after the ) ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME returns TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#name-returns-type-not-type","t":"The returned value doesn't match the declared return type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME takes N argument(s), got N","u":"errors.html#name-takes-n-arguments-got-n","t":"There are no default values or variadic parameters. Pass every argument."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"argument N of NAME should be TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#argument-n-of-name-should-be-type-not-type","t":"The argument has the wrong type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is a TYPE, not a function","u":"errors.html#name-is-a-type-not-a-function","t":"Something that isn't a function was called. Often a variable shadows a function name."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Structs","u":"errors.html#structs","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"struct NAME contains itself by value (values can't be infinite; use an enum with a variant for \"none\", or an array)","u":"errors.html#struct-name-contains-itself-by-value-values-cant-be-infinite-use-an-enum-with-a-variant-for-none-or-an-array","t":"Every value is stored inline, so a struct holding itself would be infinitely large. An array of itself is fine (it may be empty), and so is a recursive enum."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"STRUCT has N fields, got N values","u":"errors.html#struct-has-n-fields-got-n-values","t":"A struct is built with every field, in order (or by name). Too many values were given."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"missing field FIELD in STRUCT","u":"errors.html#missing-field-field-in-struct","t":"Every field must be given; there are no default field values. Write a constructor function if you want defaults."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"field FIELD given twice","u":"errors.html#field-field-given-twice","t":"The same field was given by position and by name, or twice by name. x was given by position and again by name, so y was never given; both are reported."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"TYPE has no field FIELD","u":"errors.html#type-has-no-field-field","t":"The struct has no field with this name, either in a constructor or in value.field . The hint suggests a close name. Arrays, maps, and strings have no fields at all: use len(x) and functions like s.upper() ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"field FIELD is TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#field-field-is-type-not-type","t":"A value in a struct constructor has the wrong type for its field."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Enums","u":"errors.html#enums","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"ENUM is an enum: build one of its variants, e.g. ENUM.VARIANT(...)","u":"errors.html#enum-is-an-enum-build-one-of-its-variants-eg-enumvariant","t":"An enum value is always one of its variants. Name the variant. (For a variant without values, leave off the parentheses, even though the message shows (...) .)"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"ENUM has no variant VARIANT (it has VARIANT, VARIANT, ...)","u":"errors.html#enum-has-no-variant-variant-it-has-variant-variant-","t":"The variant name is misspelled or missing from the enum. The message lists the ones that exist. This error also appears for a pattern in match ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"ENUM.VARIANT takes N value(s), got N","u":"errors.html#enumvariant-takes-n-values-got-n","t":"The variant was built with the wrong number of values."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"ENUM.VARIANT has no field NAME","u":"errors.html#enumvariant-has-no-field-name","t":"A named value doesn't match any of the variant's fields. The compiler also reports missing radius in Shape.Circle , since the real field was not given."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"missing FIELD in ENUM.VARIANT","u":"errors.html#missing-field-in-enumvariant","t":"Every value of a variant must be given."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"FIELD is TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#field-is-type-not-type","t":"A value given to an enum variant has the wrong type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"ENUM is an enum: use match to get at its values","u":"errors.html#enum-is-an-enum-use-match-to-get-at-its-values","t":"A variant's values can only be read with match , because the compiler can't know which variant a value holds until it checks."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"match works on enums, int, str, and bool, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#match-works-on-enums-int-str-and-bool-not-type","t":"match can't compare floats, arrays, maps, or structs. Use if / elif for those."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"ENUM is an enum: match its variants, not TYPE values","u":"errors.html#enum-is-an-enum-match-its-variants-not-type-values","t":"A literal pattern (a number, string, or bool) was used in a match on an enum."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"this pattern is TYPE, but the value being matched is TYPE","u":"errors.html#this-pattern-is-type-but-the-value-being-matched-is-type","t":"The literal in a pattern has a different type from the value being matched."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is a variant pattern, but the value being matched is TYPE","u":"errors.html#name-is-a-variant-pattern-but-the-value-being-matched-is-type","t":"A bare name in a pattern means an enum variant. Here the value is an int, str, or bool, so there are no variants. To match anything, use _ ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"ENUM.VARIANT doesn't match a TYPE","u":"errors.html#enumvariant-doesnt-match-a-type","t":"A qualified pattern names a variant of a different enum."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"VARIANT is already handled above","u":"errors.html#variant-is-already-handled-above","t":"A variant appears in two arms; the second could never run."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"ENUM.VARIANT has N value(s): write VARIANT(a, b)","u":"errors.html#enumvariant-has-n-values-write-varianta-b","t":"A pattern must bind every value of the variant, by position. Use _ for values you don't need."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"match on ENUM doesn't handle VARIANT, ... (add them, or a _: arm)","u":"errors.html#match-on-enum-doesnt-handle-variant--add-them-or-a-_-arm","t":"A match on an enum must cover every variant, so adding a variant later shows every place that needs to handle it. A match on int, str, or bool doesn't have to be complete."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't change NAME inside a match on it (its values are in use); change it after the match","u":"errors.html#cant-change-name-inside-a-match-on-it-its-values-are-in-use-change-it-after-the-match","t":"The arm's bound values come from the value being matched, so it can't be replaced while the arm runs. Record what to do, and change it after the match ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Arrays, maps, and strings","u":"errors.html#arrays-maps-and-strings","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't index into TYPE","u":"errors.html#cant-index-into-type","t":"Only arrays ( xs i ), maps ( m k ), and strings ( s i ) can be indexed."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"array index must be an int","u":"errors.html#array-index-must-be-an-int","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"this map's keys are TYPE","u":"errors.html#this-maps-keys-are-type","t":"The index has a different type from the map's keys."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can only slice a str or an array, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#can-only-slice-a-str-or-an-array-not-type","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"slice bounds must be ints","u":"errors.html#slice-bounds-must-be-ints","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"[value] * n: n must be an int","u":"errors.html#value--n-n-must-be-an-int","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"array mixes TYPE and TYPE","u":"errors.html#array-mixes-type-and-type","t":"All elements of an array have one type. Ints and floats don't mix; for different kinds of values, use a struct or an enum."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"map mixes TYPE: TYPE with TYPE: TYPE","u":"errors.html#map-mixes-type-type-with-type-type","t":"All keys of a map have one type, and all values have one type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"len() needs a str, array, map, or channel, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#len-needs-a-str-array-map-or-channel-not-type","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"push() needs an array, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#push-needs-an-array-not-type","t":"push adds to the end of an array. Maps grow with m k = v ; strings with += ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"argument 2 of push should be TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#argument-2-of-push-should-be-type-not-type","t":"The pushed value doesn't match the array's element type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"pop() needs an array, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#pop-needs-an-array-not-type","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME() works on maps, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#name-works-on-maps-not-type","t":"get , remove , keys , and values are map functions. To remove from an array, build a new one with filter or a loop."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"get(): the key should be TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#get-the-key-should-be-type-not-type","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"get(): the default should be TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#get-the-default-should-be-type-not-type","t":"The default is returned when the key is missing, so it must have the value type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"strings can't be changed in place; build a new one, e.g. s = s[..i] + x + s[i + 1..]","u":"errors.html#strings-cant-be-changed-in-place-build-a-new-one-eg-s--si--x--si--1","t":"Strings are immutable values. Build a new string and assign it."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME modifies its receiver, so it needs a variable, field, or element","u":"errors.html#name-modifies-its-receiver-so-it-needs-a-variable-field-or-element","t":"xs.push(v) and xs.pop() change xs , so xs must be something that can be changed, not a literal or a function result. The compiler also reports can only modify a variable, field, or element on the same line."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"map(xs, f) needs an array and a function, not TYPE and TYPE","u":"errors.html#mapxs-f-needs-an-array-and-a-function-not-type-and-type","t":"map , filter , and sort_by take an array and a function. The message may be preceded by an argument error that mentions a type like fn(int)? ; the ? there means \"any return type\"."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME(): the function should take one TYPE","u":"errors.html#name-the-function-should-take-one-type","t":"The function passed to map , filter , or sort_by must take one value of the array's element type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"map(): the function must return a value","u":"errors.html#map-the-function-must-return-a-value","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"filter(): the function should return bool, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#filter-the-function-should-return-bool-not-type","t":"For a lambda, the same mistake is reported as this function should return bool, not TYPE ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"sort_by(): the function should return an int, float, f32, or str to sort by, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#sort_by-the-function-should-return-an-int-float-f32-or-str-to-sort-by-not-type","t":"sort_by takes a key function. It can't sort by a bool, array, or struct directly; turn it into a number or string."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Mutation, mut, and &","u":"errors.html#mutation-mut-and-","t":"Tern has no references: every value is a copy, and a function can change a caller's variable only through a mut parameter, marked with & at the call. These errors enforce that. See Values, mut, and & (tour/09-values-and-mut.md)."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't modify parameter NAME: parameters are read-only unless declared mut","u":"errors.html#cant-modify-parameter-name-parameters-are-read-only-unless-declared-mut","t":"A parameter is the caller's value, lent to the function. Changing it would either change the caller's variable behind its back or silently change only a copy, so it is refused. Copy it to a local variable, return the new value, or declare it mut . This includes push , pop , and field assignment on a parameter:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't modify loop variable NAME","u":"errors.html#cant-modify-loop-variable-name","t":"The loop variable is a copy of the current element (or index). Assigning to it would not change the array, so it is refused. To change elements, loop over indices."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can only modify a variable, field, or element","u":"errors.html#can-only-modify-a-variable-field-or-element","t":"A mut argument or a changing method call needs a place to write to. A function result or literal isn't one."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"argument N of NAME is mut: write &x so the change is visible here","u":"errors.html#argument-n-of-name-is-mut-write-x-so-the-change-is-visible-here","t":"A mut parameter changes the caller's variable, and Tern requires the caller to say so with & , so every change is visible at the call."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"argument N of NAME isn't mut, so don't pass it with &","u":"errors.html#argument-n-of-name-isnt-mut-so-dont-pass-it-with-","t":"& promises that the function may change the argument. This parameter is read-only, so the & would be misleading."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"& is only for passing an argument to a mut parameter","u":"errors.html#-is-only-for-passing-an-argument-to-a-mut-parameter","t":"There are no references or pointers to Tern values. &x only appears as an argument."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't assign to function NAME","u":"errors.html#cant-assign-to-function-name","t":"A nested function's name refers to the function; it isn't a variable. (It is reported together with a type error.)"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Aliasing","u":"errors.html#aliasing","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME would see the same value twice: &PLACE can be changed, but PLACE is also passed (argument N)","u":"errors.html#name-would-see-the-same-value-twice-place-can-be-changed-but-place-is-also-passed-argument-n","t":"A function that takes a mut parameter may assume nothing else it can see changes at the same time. Passing the same variable twice breaks that (in transfer(&a, &a, 30) the second write would undo the first). Tern refuses the call. For array elements it can't prove two indices differ, so f(&xs i , &xs j ) is refused too. If you do need the function to read a value while changing it, pass a copy:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME would see the same value twice: PLACE is given away, but PLACE is also passed (argument N)","u":"errors.html#name-would-see-the-same-value-twice-place-is-given-away-but-place-is-also-passed-argument-n","t":"A value handed to a take parameter is moved, so it can't also be passed as another argument."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME would see the same value twice: &PLACE can be changed, but NAME is the function being called","u":"errors.html#name-would-see-the-same-value-twice-place-can-be-changed-but-name-is-the-function-being-called","t":"The function being called is stored in the value that is also passed mut , so the call could change the function while it runs."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't read NAME here: a call later in this expression changes it with &NAME, and that call runs first. Read NAME into a variable on the line before","u":"errors.html#cant-read-name-here-a-call-later-in-this-expression-changes-it-with-name-and-that-call-runs-first-read-name-into-a-variable-on-the-line-before","t":"Calls inside an expression run before the plain reads around them: each call's result is computed first. So when one part of an expression reads x and a call further along changes x through &x , the read would see the new value, which isn't what the code says. Tern refuses it instead of picking an order silently (Rust refuses it too). Reads after the call are fine ( bump(&x) + x reads the new x , as written), and so are and / or , which run their right side only after the left."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"take and moved values","u":"errors.html#take-and-moved-values","t":"A take parameter moves the value into the function instead of copying it. The caller's variable is gone afterwards."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME was handed to a take parameter earlier and is gone (pass copy(NAME) to keep it)","u":"errors.html#name-was-handed-to-a-take-parameter-earlier-and-is-gone-pass-copyname-to-keep-it","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME was handed to a take parameter earlier and is gone","u":"errors.html#name-was-handed-to-a-take-parameter-earlier-and-is-gone","t":"The same, when the moved variable is changed afterwards. Assigning a whole new value ( xs = ... ) is allowed and brings the variable back."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is handed over inside this loop, so the next pass would use it after it's gone (pass copy(NAME))","u":"errors.html#name-is-handed-over-inside-this-loop-so-the-next-pass-would-use-it-after-its-gone-pass-copyname","t":"A variable from outside a loop is moved inside it. The second pass would use it after the first moved it."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't give away mut parameter NAME (it belongs to the caller); pass copy(NAME)","u":"errors.html#cant-give-away-mut-parameter-name-it-belongs-to-the-caller-pass-copyname","t":"A mut parameter is the caller's variable. Moving it would leave the caller with nothing."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Loops","u":"errors.html#loops","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can only loop over an array, a str, a map, or a channel, not TYPE (for a range, write for i in 0..n:)","u":"errors.html#can-only-loop-over-an-array-a-str-a-map-or-a-channel-not-type-for-a-range-write-for-i-in-0n","t":"for x in n with a number is an error; write a range."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"range bounds must be int","u":"errors.html#range-bounds-must-be-int","t":"Ranges count integers. For float steps, loop over ints and convert."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"break is only allowed inside a loop","u":"errors.html#break-is-only-allowed-inside-a-loop","t":"The same message is used for continue ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't modify NAME while looping over it; loop over indices instead: for i in 0..len(NAME):","u":"errors.html#cant-modify-name-while-looping-over-it-loop-over-indices-instead-for-i-in-0lenname","t":"Changing an array while a for loop walks it would change what the loop sees, so it is refused. To change elements in place, loop over indices; to add or remove elements, build a new array."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't modify NAME while looping over it; loop over a copy of its keys instead: for k in NAME.keys():","u":"errors.html#cant-modify-name-while-looping-over-it-loop-over-a-copy-of-its-keys-instead-for-k-in-namekeys","t":"The same rule for a map. keys() returns a copy of the keys, so the loop can change the map:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Parallelism and races","u":"errors.html#parallelism-and-races","t":"Tasks in a parallel: block, and iterations of a parallel for , run at the same time. The compiler refuses any program where one of them writes a variable another one reads or writes. See Parallelism (tour/11-parallelism.md)."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"data race on NAME: the task on line N writes it while the task on line N reads it at the same time","u":"errors.html#data-race-on-name-the-task-on-line-n-writes-it-while-the-task-on-line-n-reads-it-at-the-same-time","t":"The full message ends with: \"Give each task its own variable and combine them after the block\"."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"iterations of parallel for run at the same time, so writing NAME from every iteration would race","u":"errors.html#iterations-of-parallel-for-run-at-the-same-time-so-writing-name-from-every-iteration-would-race","t":"Every iteration writes the same variable. Store each iteration's result in its own array slot, then combine after the loop. For an array, the message says write only NAME i : each iteration may write only the element at its own index."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"each iteration writes NAME[i], so reading any other part of NAME inside the loop would race","u":"errors.html#each-iteration-writes-namei-so-reading-any-other-part-of-name-inside-the-loop-would-race","t":"Another iteration may be writing the element being read. Read from a copy made before the loop."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is not an array","u":"errors.html#name-is-not-an-array","t":"A parallel for iteration may write NAME i only when NAME is an array. Maps can't be written from parallel iterations; fill an array, then build the map after the loop."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't tell the type of NAME","u":"errors.html#cant-tell-the-type-of-name","t":"A one-line task that creates a variable must give it a value whose type is known."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"do: groups several statements into one task, so it only works directly inside parallel:","u":"errors.html#do-groups-several-statements-into-one-task-so-it-only-works-directly-inside-parallel","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"return isn't allowed inside parallel; assign the result to a variable and return after the block","u":"errors.html#return-isnt-allowed-inside-parallel-assign-the-result-to-a-variable-and-return-after-the-block","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Closures","u":"errors.html#closures","t":"A closure (a lambda fn(x) ... or a nested fn ) copies the variables it uses when it is created. See Functions as values (tour/10-closures.md)."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't modify NAME here: this function has its own copy of it, made when the function was created (return a new value, or take it as a mut parameter)","u":"errors.html#cant-modify-name-here-this-function-has-its-own-copy-of-it-made-when-the-function-was-created-return-a-new-value-or-take-it-as-a-mut-parameter","t":"Because the closure holds a copy, changing it would not change the outer variable, so it is refused."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't tell the type of parameter NAME; write fn(NAME: int) ..., or give the variable a type, e.g. f: fn(int) int = ...","u":"errors.html#cant-tell-the-type-of-parameter-name-write-fnname-int--or-give-the-variable-a-type-eg-f-fnint-int--","t":"A lambda's parameter types can be left out only when the context gives them (a typed variable, parameter, or field, or map / filter / sort_by )."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"this function takes N parameter(s), but it should take N here (TYPE)","u":"errors.html#this-function-takes-n-parameters-but-it-should-take-n-here-type","t":"The lambda has a different number of parameters from the function type expected here."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"parameter NAME should be TYPE here","u":"errors.html#parameter-name-should-be-type-here","t":"A lambda parameter's written type differs from the one the context expects."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"this function should return TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#this-function-should-return-type-not-type","t":"The lambda's expression has a different type from the expected return type."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Modules","u":"errors.html#modules","t":"See Modules and packages (tour/12-modules.md) for how imports are found."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"can't find module NAME (looked in the standard library and DIR)","u":"errors.html#cant-find-module-name-looked-in-the-standard-library-and-dir","t":"import name looks in the standard library and the first-party packages, then for name.tn or a directory name/ next to the main file, then in deps/ when the project has a tern.mod (the message then also names ROOT/deps ). Check the spelling and where the file is."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is private to module MODULE","u":"errors.html#name-is-private-to-module-module","t":"Names that start with _ can only be used inside their own module."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"module MODULE has no NAME","u":"errors.html#module-module-has-no-name","t":"The module exists but has no function, struct, or enum with that name. The same error appears for from MODULE import NAME ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME isn't an imported module (add import NAME)","u":"errors.html#name-isnt-an-imported-module-add-import-name","t":"A type like http.Request names a module that this file doesn't import. (For a function call such as math.pi() , the message is NAME is a module; add import NAME ( name-is-a-module-add-import-name-at-the-top-of-this-file).)"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"import cycle: MODULE -> MODULE -> MODULE","u":"errors.html#import-cycle-module---module---module","t":"Two modules import each other, directly or through others. Move the shared code into a third module that both import. With main.tn importing a , and a.tn and b.tn importing each other: ( main is the program's main file.)"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is defined twice in module MODULE","u":"errors.html#name-is-defined-twice-in-module-module","t":"A package is a directory of .tn files that form one module, so their top-level names must be distinct across all the files. With shapes/one.tn and shapes/two.tn both defining fn area() :"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"only the main program can export functions (MODULE.NAME)","u":"errors.html#only-the-main-program-can-export-functions-modulename","t":"export fn makes a function callable from C, JavaScript, or Swift. Only the file you build can export; an imported module can't. Put an export fn wrapper in the main file that calls the module's function. With lib.tn containing export fn add(...) and the main file importing lib :"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"C interop and export","u":"errors.html#c-interop-and-export","t":"These are about extern blocks (calling C) and export fn (being called from C or JavaScript). See Calling C, and Tern as a library (guides/c-interop.md)."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"TYPE can't cross the C boundary (use int, float, bool, str, ptr, [int], [float], [byte], or a function of int/float/bool/ptr values)","u":"errors.html#type-cant-cross-the-c-boundary-use-int-float-bool-str-ptr-int-float-byte-or-a-function-of-intfloatboolptr-values","t":"A C function's parameters must have a C form. Structs, maps, enums, and arrays of other types don't. Pass the fields one by one, or use int / float ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"TYPE can't cross the C boundary (a mut parameter can be int, float, bool, ptr, [int], [float], or [byte])","u":"errors.html#type-cant-cross-the-c-boundary-a-mut-parameter-can-be-int-float-bool-ptr-int-float-or-byte","t":"C can write back into numbers and number arrays, not into a str . Have the C function return a string instead."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is a C function; take doesn't mean anything to C (drop it)","u":"errors.html#name-is-a-c-function-take-doesnt-mean-anything-to-c-drop-it","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME returns TYPE, which can't cross the C boundary (C functions return int, float, bool, str, or ptr)","u":"errors.html#name-returns-type-which-cant-cross-the-c-boundary-c-functions-return-int-float-bool-str-or-ptr","t":"A C function can't return an array. Pass a mut int or mut float for it to fill."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME is a C function; wrap it in a Tern function to use it as a value","u":"errors.html#name-is-a-c-function-wrap-it-in-a-tern-function-to-use-it-as-a-value","t":"A C function can't be stored in a variable or passed as a function value. Wrap it."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"an extern block holds fn signatures (no bodies)","u":"errors.html#an-extern-block-holds-fn-signatures-no-bodies","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"after =, write the C function name in quotes: = \"c_name\"","u":"errors.html#after--write-the-c-function-name-in-quotes--c_name","t":"= \"name\" gives a Tern name to a C function with a different name."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"WORD needs a quoted file or library name","u":"errors.html#word-needs-a-quoted-file-or-library-name","t":"After the header, an extern line may name C files to compile ( source \"impl.c\" ) and libraries to link ( link \"m\" ), each in quotes."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"exported NAME can't take mut or take parameters (C passes plain values)","u":"errors.html#exported-name-cant-take-mut-or-take-parameters-c-passes-plain-values","t":"An exported function is called from C or JavaScript, which pass plain values in and get one value out. Return the result instead."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"TYPE can't cross the C boundary (exported functions take int, float, bool, str, or ptr)","u":"errors.html#type-cant-cross-the-c-boundary-exported-functions-take-int-float-bool-str-or-ptr","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"exported NAME returns TYPE, which can't cross the C boundary (return int, float, bool, str, or ptr)","u":"errors.html#exported-name-returns-type-which-cant-cross-the-c-boundary-return-int-float-bool-str-or-ptr","t":"To hand structured data to the caller, return it as JSON text."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"is_null() needs a ptr, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#is_null-needs-a-ptr-not-type","t":"null and is_null() are only for ptr , the opaque C pointer type. Other types have no null."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Web, database, and JSON","u":"errors.html#web-database-and-json","t":"See Building web apps (guides/web-apps.md), json (std/json.md), and sqlite (packages/sqlite.md)."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"http.serve(port, handler) or http.serve(port, handler, app)","u":"errors.html#httpserveport-handler-or-httpserveport-handler-app","t":"http.serve takes a port, a handler function, and optionally the app state."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"serve(): the port must be an int","u":"errors.html#serve-the-port-must-be-an-int","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"serve(): the second argument must be the name of a handler function","u":"errors.html#serve-the-second-argument-must-be-the-name-of-a-handler-function","t":"The handler must be a top-level function named directly, not a lambda or a variable."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"serve(): NAME must look like fn handler(req: http.Request) http.Response","u":"errors.html#serve-name-must-look-like-fn-handlerreq-httprequest-httpresponse","t":"The handler takes the request (and the app state, when there is one) and returns a response. With app state the shape is fn handler(req: http.Request, app: App) http.Response (or app: mut App )."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME changes the app state, so pass it with &; requests then run one at a time","u":"errors.html#name-changes-the-app-state-so-pass-it-with--requests-then-run-one-at-a-time","t":"The handler takes app: mut App , so the call must pass &app . Requests that can change the state run one at a time, so they never race."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"NAME only reads the app state, so pass it without &","u":"errors.html#name-only-reads-the-app-state-so-pass-it-without-","t":"The handler takes the state read-only, so all requests can share it in parallel. Drop the & ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"serve(): the app state should be TYPE, not TYPE","u":"errors.html#serve-the-app-state-should-be-type-not-type","t":"The third argument must have the type of the handler's second parameter."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"json.encode can't handle TYPE","u":"errors.html#jsonencode-cant-handle-type","t":"JSON holds numbers, bools, strings, arrays, maps, structs, and enums made of those ( Option T is null or the value). Functions, tuples, ptr values, and recursive enums have no JSON form; encode the parts that do."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"json.decode can't fill a TYPE","u":"errors.html#jsondecode-cant-fill-a-type","t":"json.decode(text, &value) fills a value of a JSON-compatible type. The same limits as json.encode apply."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"json.decode(text, &value): text must be a str","u":"errors.html#jsondecodetext-value-text-must-be-a-str","t":""},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"query_into fills a struct (or array of structs) whose fields are int, float, bool, str, or an enum without payloads (or an Option of one); not TYPE","u":"errors.html#query_into-fills-a-struct-or-array-of-structs-whose-fields-are-int-float-bool-str-or-an-enum-without-payloads-or-an-option-of-one-not-type","t":"Each row becomes one struct, with columns matched to fields by name."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"d.query_into(sql, params, &out): sql is a str, params a [str]","u":"errors.html#dquery_intosql-params-out-sql-is-a-str-params-a-str","t":"SQL parameters are always strings, and are bound safely. Convert numbers with str() . (An argument error is reported on the same line.)"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Runtime errors","u":"errors.html#runtime-errors","t":"A program that compiles can still fail while it runs. A runtime error prints runtime error in FUNCTION (FILE:LINE): MESSAGE to stderr and ends the program with exit status 2, e.g. runtime error in util.average (util.tn:5): division by zero . When the function was called from others, a second line shows the way it came: called from report <- main . (Errors raised inside a library call, like sqlite 's, say runtime error: MESSAGE .) There are no exceptions to catch; check before the operation instead, as in the fixes below. Inside a web request handler, a runtime error becomes a 500 response and "},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error in F (FILE:N): index N out of bounds (length N)","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-in-f-filen-index-n-out-of-bounds-length-n","t":"An array or string index is negative or not less than the length. There are no negative indexes: the last element is xs len(xs) - 1 . This program: stops with: Check the index first, or loop over the array directly. (Slices don't fail: xs 1..10 stops at the end of the array.)"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error in F (FILE:N): key not found (use m.get(key, default) or key in m)","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-in-f-filen-key-not-found-use-mgetkey-default-or-key-in-m","t":"m k reads a key that isn't in the map. This program: stops with: Use get with a default, or test with in :"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error in F (FILE:N): division by zero","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-in-f-filen-division-by-zero","t":"Integer / or % by zero. (Float division by 0.0 doesn't fail; it gives inf or nan .) This program: stops with: Check the divisor:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error in F (FILE:N): pop() from an empty array","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-in-f-filen-pop-from-an-empty-array","t":"This program: stops with: Check the length first:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error: negative array length","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-negative-array-length","t":"value n with a negative n . The line number is reported as 0. Use max(n, 0) if n may be negative:"},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error: random.int: hi < lo","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-randomint-hi--lo","t":"random.int(lo, hi) returns a number from lo to hi inclusive, so hi must not be less than lo ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error: sqlite: MESSAGE","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-sqlite-message","t":"A SQL statement failed (a syntax error, a missing table, a constraint). The message comes from SQLite, and has no runtime error prefix; the program ends with status 2, or the request gets a 500 . For example, d.exec(\"SELEC 1\", ) stops with: sqlite.open on a path that can't be opened stops with runtime error: sqlite.open: can't open database ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error: stack overflow (recursion too deep)","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-stack-overflow-recursion-too-deep","t":"A function recursed so deeply that the thread's stack (64MB) ran out, on the main thread, inside parallel: , or in a task ( stack overflow in a task ). The program ends with status 2. Recursion a few hundred thousand calls deep is fine; unbounded recursion (a missing base case) is the usual cause. Turn deep recursion over long inputs into a loop."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error in F (FILE:N): can't convert inf to int","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-in-f-filen-cant-convert-inf-to-int","t":"int(x) of a float with no integer value: inf , -inf , nan , or a value outside int's range ( can't convert 1e+20 to int (out of range) ). Check first with x == x and x < 9_000_000_000_000_000_000.0 and x -9_000_000_000_000_000_000.0 ."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error in F (FILE:N): chr(N): the byte value must be 0 to 255","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-in-f-filen-chrn-the-byte-value-must-be-0-to-255","t":"chr(n) makes a one-byte string, so n must be a byte value."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error: all tasks are blocked (deadlock)","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-all-tasks-are-blocked-deadlock","t":"Every task, main included, waits on a channel, a join, or a shared value with no timeout, so nothing can ever wake them. The message says what main and the other tasks wait for: Usually a value is never sent, or two tasks each wait for the other. Waits on sockets, timers ( task.sleep , recv_timeout ), and task.blocking aren't counted, so a server waiting for connections is never reported."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"runtime error in F (FILE:N): a shared value was used inside its own update","u":"errors.html#runtime-error-in-f-filen-a-shared-value-was-used-inside-its-own-update","t":"The function passed to s.update(f) called s.get() , s.set(v) , or s.update(g) on the same shared value. The update holds the value until f returns, so that would wait forever. Use the value f is given instead."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"task failed: MESSAGE","u":"errors.html#task-failed-message","t":"A task hit a runtime error and nothing joined it ( h.join() raises the error again). The error is printed, and when main returns the program exits with status 2. Join the task, or call h.cancel() on it if its failure is expected."},{"p":"Compiler errors","h":"Other runtime errors","u":"errors.html#other-runtime-errors","t":"These are rare: Message Meaning --- --- array too large (over 4 billion elements) an array outgrew the size limit string too long (over 4GB) a string outgrew the size limit called a function value that was never set a function value was called before any function was stored in it sqlite.open: too many databases too many databases open at once"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Command line","u":"cli.html#command-line","t":"tern is the Tern compiler. It checks a program, translates it to C, and has clang build the C into a native binary, a library, or a WebAssembly module. It also runs tests, formats code, fetches packages, and serves editors. This page lists every command and flag, the exit codes, the JSON error format for tools, the targets and the toolchains they need, the environment variables the compiler and compiled programs read, and the commands that run the test suites."},{"p":"Command line","h":"Installing","u":"cli.html#installing","t":"tern is written in Tern ( compiler/ ). ./install.sh builds it into bin/tern and links it into ~/.local/bin . It builds the compiler with the previous bin/tern ; on a fresh checkout (or when bin/tern is too old) it first compiles bootstrap/ternc.c , the compiler emitted as one C file, with cc (see bootstrap/README.md ). tern needs clang to build anything (on macOS, xcode-select --install ). Run it from anywhere; it finds the standard library and runtime next to itself. Every target ( --target wasm , linux , ios , android , ...) is handled by tern itself; see Targets ( targets) for the toolchain"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Commands","u":"cli.html#commands","t":"Command What it does --- --- run FILE Checks, compiles, and runs the program, passing it the arguments after -- . Exits with the program's exit status. build FILE Checks and compiles the program to a file (see Build outputs ( build-outputs)). check FILE Only the type and safety checks; prints FILE: ok . Fast: no C compiler runs. repl FILE... An interactive prompt: type an expression to see its value, or statements, fn s, struct s and import s that later lines use. Each FILE's definitions are loaded first (see REPL ( repl)). emit FILE Prints the generated C to standard output. test PATH... Runs"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Flags","u":"cli.html#flags","t":"Flags go after the command, before or after the file name. Everything after -- is passed to the program by run . An unknown flag is an error that suggests the closest one ( unknown flag --nativ ; did you mean --native ? ), and so is an unknown command. Flag Meaning --- --- -o PATH Output file for build (and, for run , where to put the binary). --json With check , run , or build : report compile errors as JSON on standard output (below). --symbols With check --json : also list every definition and reference (below), for editors. --target T , --target=T Compile for another platform (see Targets "},{"p":"Command line","h":"REPL","u":"cli.html#repl","t":"tern repl reads Tern a line at a time. An expression prints its value (strings quoted); x = 5 makes a variable later lines can use; fn , struct , enum , const and import add to the program, and typing a fn or struct again replaces it. A line ending in : starts a block, ended by an empty line. Commands: :show prints the program so far, :undo takes back the last input, :reset starts over, :load FILE adds a file's definitions (not its main ), :quit or Ctrl-D ends. Tern is compiled, so the session is one program that is compiled and run again for each input (about a third of a second for a small o"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Exit codes","u":"cli.html#exit-codes","t":"Status When --- --- 0 Success. For run , whatever the program returned: 0 when main returns normally. 1 Compile errors; the C compiler failed or isn't installed; a target's toolchain is missing; an unknown command or flag; a flag is missing its value; the runtime can't be found; guide found no matching section; a test failed; fmt --check found a file to format; get failed. 2 ( run only) The program stopped with a runtime error. 64 Usage error: no command, no file given, or run --lib . The usage text is printed. 66 The file doesn't exist: tern: can't open FILE . With run , any other status come"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Compile errors","u":"cli.html#compile-errors","t":"Errors go to standard error, one per line, with a hint: line after an error when the code looks like another language's habit ( def , let , && , append , None , ...). All the errors in a program are reported together, followed by a count: The compiler errors (errors.md) page explains the common ones."},{"p":"Command line","h":"JSON output","u":"cli.html#json-output","t":"--json prints the result as JSON on standard output instead, for editors, scripts, and agents. The exit status is the same as without it. On success ( check only): With errors: Field Type Meaning --- --- --- ok bool true if the program compiled file string (success) the file that was checked errors .file string the file the error is in (an imported module can be the one at fault), relative to the current directory when it's below it errors .line int line number, from 1 errors .message string the error, as in the text output errors .hint string or null a suggested fix, or null errors .source st"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Build outputs","u":"cli.html#build-outputs","t":"build writes into the current directory unless -o says otherwise. For prog.tn : Command Writes --- --- build prog.tn prog (an executable) build --target wasm prog.tn prog.wasm (a WASI program) build --lib prog.tn libprog.a and prog.h build --shared prog.tn libprog.dylib or libprog.so , and prog.h build --lib --target wasm prog.tn prog.wasm , prog.mjs (a JavaScript loader), and prog.d.ts (TypeScript types) build --lib --target wasm-threads prog.tn the same three; the loader also starts the worker threads The header goes in the same directory as the library, named after it without the lib prefix"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Build cache","u":"cli.html#build-cache","t":"A finished build is kept in ~/.cache/tern ( $XDG_CACHE_HOME/tern if that is set), named by a hash of everything that went into it: the generated C, the compiler and its flags, the C sources and headers from extern blocks, and the libraries in -L directories (by size and time). Building an unchanged program again copies the result instead of running clang: rebuilding the compiler itself ( tern build compiler/main.tn ) takes 0.2 s instead of 36 s. The cache keeps the 1000 most recently used builds. tern run and tern test run the cached program itself (through a hard link) rather than a copy: mac"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Debugging","u":"cli.html#debugging","t":"tern build -g prog.tn (or --debug ) builds with -g -O0 , keeps the generated C next to the output as prog.tern.c , and puts line directives in it, so a debugger shows and steps through the .tn source: Breakpoints by file.tn:line , stepping, and backtraces use Tern lines; stepping into the runtime shows the C. Variables have their C names: a parameter n is n_p , and a local total is total_2 (a number is added to keep names unique). gdb works the same way on Linux."},{"p":"Command line","h":"Testing","u":"cli.html#testing","t":"tern test runs tests: functions named test_... with no parameters, in files whose names end in _test.tn . - tern test searches the current directory and the directories below it (skipping deps/ , build/ , and names starting with . ); tern test DIR searches DIR , and tern test FILE runs the tests in any one file. - Each test runs in its own process, so a runtime error or a crash fails that test and the others still run. A test's output is shown only when it fails ( -v shows it always). - --filter TEXT runs only the tests whose name contains TEXT . - The exit status is 1 if any test failed or a "},{"p":"Command line","h":"Formatting","u":"cli.html#formatting","t":"tern fmt rewrites .tn files in the standard layout: 4-space indentation, one space around binary operators and after commas and colons, none inside brackets, at most one blank line in a row, one blank line before a top-level fn / struct / enum that follows a block, no trailing whitespace, and one newline at the end. Comments are kept, and an end-of-line comment keeps its column when it fits. Only whitespace changes: the tokens are the same before and after. Formatting a formatted file changes nothing. A file that doesn't parse is left alone and reported. Long lines aren't wrapped."},{"p":"Command line","h":"New projects","u":"cli.html#new-projects","t":"tern new creates the directory (it must not exist, or be empty), copies the template from templates/ in the Tern checkout with its name filled in, and runs git init (not with --no-git ). Every project it makes passes tern test as it is, has a README saying how to run it, a tern.mod for packages, and an AGENTS.md (plus a CLAUDE.md pointing to it) that tells coding agents how to write Tern and where things go in that project. A lib name must be a valid module name (lowercase letters, digits, _ )."},{"p":"Command line","h":"Packages","u":"cli.html#packages","t":"tern add looks the name up in the package index ( index/packages/NAME.txt in the Tern checkout, or $TERN_INDEX : a directory or a git URL; see index/README.md ), picks the newest release tag (the default branch's commit if there are none), and runs tern get for it. A name that is already built in ( roost , pg , json , ...) needs no adding, and tern add says so. A package's _test.tn files are never part of it. A project lists the packages it uses in a tern.mod file at its root, one per line: tern get clones each one at that version into deps/NAME/ (without its .git ) and records the exact commi"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Editors","u":"cli.html#editors","t":"editor/vscode/ is a VS Code extension: syntax highlighting, comment and bracket handling, indentation after : , and the language server. See its README to install it. tern lsp is a language server (the Language Server Protocol on standard input and output) for any editor that speaks it: - errors and hints as you type (it runs tern check --json --symbols on the file; unsaved text is checked from a hidden temporary copy next to the file, so imports still resolve); - go to definition, hover (the signature or type, and the comment above the definition), find references, and rename, for every name "},{"p":"Command line","h":"Targets","u":"cli.html#targets","t":"--target Builds for Needs Notes --- --- --- --- native this machine clang the default; the fast runtime macos-x86_64 Intel Macs clang runs under Rosetta 2 on Apple silicon linux , linux-arm64 Linux arm64 Docker built and run in a container (image built on first use) linux-x86_64 Linux x86-64 Docker through Docker's emulation linux-static , linux-static-x86_64 any Linux (fully static, musl) Docker one self-contained executable: runs on any distribution, even in a FROM scratch image wasm WebAssembly (WASI) brew install llvm lld wasi-libc wasi-runtimes , or a wasi-sdk ( WASI_SDK_PATH ) one thread"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Environment variables","u":"cli.html#environment-variables","t":""},{"p":"Command line","h":"Read by the compiler","u":"cli.html#read-by-the-compiler","t":"Variable Effect --- --- TERN_STATS=1 Build the program with memory counters (below). Read when compiling, not when running. CC The C compiler for native and macos-x86_64 builds (default clang ). TERN_ROOT The Tern checkout holding runtime/ , library/ , and docs/ (default: found by walking up from the tern executable). TERN_CACHE off turns the build cache ( build-cache) off; a directory moves it. TERN_SPLIT 0 compiles tern run / tern test programs as one unit; 1 uses the separate runtime object for tern build too (see build cache ( build-cache)). TERN_UNIT_BYTES A program whose generated C is o"},{"p":"Command line","h":"Read by compiled programs","u":"cli.html#read-by-compiled-programs","t":"Variable Default Effect --- --- --- TERN_THREADS number of cores Worker threads for parallel (1 to 256). TERN_HTTP_THREADS TERN_THREADS Threads serving HTTP requests. TERN_HTTP_MAX_BODY_MB 16 Largest request body accepted (larger: 413 ). TERN_HTTP_IDLE_SEC 60 Seconds before an idle keep-alive connection is closed. TERN_HTTP_HEADER_SEC 10 Seconds a client may take to finish sending a request. TERN_DB_SNAPSHOT on 0 turns off the per-request read snapshot of the sqlite (packages/sqlite.md) module. TERN_DB_GROUP on 0 turns off sqlite (packages/sqlite.md)'s write batches (single writes from tasks s"},{"p":"Command line","h":"TERN_STATS","u":"cli.html#tern_stats","t":"A program compiled with TERN_STATS=1 counts its heap allocations and prints a summary to standard error when it ends normally: - allocations and freed : heap blocks created and released. - leaked : blocks never released, not counting the ones that live for the whole run (string literals and one-byte strings, which WebAssembly makes at start-up: that is why \"freed\" can be far below \"allocations\" there with nothing leaked). It should always be 0; the test suites fail on any leak. - copy-on-write copies : times a shared array, map, or string had to be copied because one holder changed it. A high "},{"p":"Command line","h":"Tests","u":"cli.html#tests","t":"The test suites are a Tern program, tests/runner/ . From the repository root: The suites test $TERN if it is set, else bin/tern . - run compiles each program in tests/run/ with TERN_STATS=1 , runs it, compares its output with the .out file next to it, and fails on any leak. Each program in tests/reject/ must fail to compile with exactly the errors in its .err file. tests/embed/ runs C, JavaScript, and Python host programs that call Tern libraries. - docs compiles and runs every tern block in the documentation and compares it with the output block after it. tern check blocks are only compiled, "}];
